WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.299 --> 00:00:02.419
You’ve been walking for days.

00:00:02.419 --> 00:00:07.500
Your body aches, you’re dripping with sweat
from the heat of the sun bearing down overhead.

00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:13.520
And yet, you’re wrapped up in layers upon
layers of clothing - even your face is covered,

00:00:13.520 --> 00:00:18.470
and you’re wearing thick, black goggles
so that not a single centimeter of your body

00:00:18.470 --> 00:00:19.949
is exposed.

00:00:19.949 --> 00:00:25.419
Your journey has been long and you feel like
you might die from exhaustion, or from overheating

00:00:25.419 --> 00:00:28.189
due to these multiple layers of clothes.

00:00:28.189 --> 00:00:30.800
But dying is better than being exposed.

00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:35.309
You saw it happen when your entire team...changed.

00:00:35.309 --> 00:00:37.980
The light can’t be trusted.

00:00:37.980 --> 00:00:41.179
Not even for a fraction of a second.

00:00:41.179 --> 00:00:43.109
It’s been like this for years.

00:00:43.109 --> 00:00:46.890
You’ve learned and survived through painful
experience.

00:00:46.890 --> 00:00:49.840
Many of those you used to know cannot say
the same.

00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:52.940
You’ve been alone for so long.

00:00:52.940 --> 00:00:57.760
You might have given up all hope, if it wasn’t
for the distress signal coming from a nearby

00:00:57.760 --> 00:01:03.019
SCP Foundation Containment Facility - Site-46.

00:01:03.019 --> 00:01:08.010
Any kind of survivors would be better than
nothing, no matter what kind of sorry shape

00:01:08.010 --> 00:01:09.110
they were in.

00:01:09.110 --> 00:01:12.080
As long as they were still human.

00:01:12.080 --> 00:01:17.690
You find your way to an opening in the side
of a mountain, and slide into the cave, hoping

00:01:17.690 --> 00:01:19.080
you weren’t spotted.

00:01:19.080 --> 00:01:22.190
The whole world is crawling with those things
now.

00:01:22.190 --> 00:01:25.030
You can’t let yourself be seen.

00:01:25.030 --> 00:01:30.330
As you trudge down the cave towards the entrance,
you see what looks like a huge, black snail-trail

00:01:30.330 --> 00:01:34.220
splattered on the ground, leading into the
facility.

00:01:34.220 --> 00:01:36.420
You try to avoid it and press on.

00:01:36.420 --> 00:01:41.630
You don’t even need a keycard to enter:
The door has been left ajar.

00:01:41.630 --> 00:01:45.890
The facility reeks of those things, but you
can’t see any of them.

00:01:45.890 --> 00:01:50.670
You just hope they’ve moved on and left
some human survivors in their wake.

00:01:50.670 --> 00:01:52.580
The place looks abandoned.

00:01:52.580 --> 00:01:56.680
Every step you take echoes through the empty
halls.

00:01:56.680 --> 00:02:00.720
When you find that the elevator’s out, you
take the stairs all the way down to Level

00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:01.750
B5…

00:02:01.750 --> 00:02:03.830
Keter Containment.

00:02:03.830 --> 00:02:09.000
Lucky for you, it seems all the cells are
empty now, the horrors that were kept inside

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:12.230
of them have all long since flown the coop.

00:02:12.230 --> 00:02:16.840
You keep following that slimy, black trail
until you find an abandoned office.

00:02:16.840 --> 00:02:23.380
There are no people here anymore - just a
broken barricade, some empty medicine bottles,

00:02:23.380 --> 00:02:28.450
and a bucket that the people inside the office
had apparently been using as a toilet.

00:02:28.450 --> 00:02:33.800
You breathe a sigh of disappointment at finding
no one alive here, but you’re at least relieved

00:02:33.800 --> 00:02:35.340
to be out of the sun.

00:02:35.340 --> 00:02:38.620
You can finally remove your jacket and head-wrap.

00:02:38.620 --> 00:02:43.180
With your uncovered eyes, you notice that
a nearby computer terminal is still powered

00:02:43.180 --> 00:02:44.180
up.

00:02:44.180 --> 00:02:46.710
You sit down at the desk and turn on the monitor.

00:02:46.710 --> 00:02:52.260
Because of the Emergency Procedures put into
place in a K-Class scenario like this, safeguards

00:02:52.260 --> 00:02:53.930
no longer apply.

00:02:53.930 --> 00:02:59.410
You can access all the information you need
- up to and including finding out what actually

00:02:59.410 --> 00:03:00.410
happened.

00:03:00.410 --> 00:03:05.400
In the dull glow of a nearby emergency light,
you see a dark shape slumping through the

00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:06.900
halls in shadow.

00:03:06.900 --> 00:03:11.570
You tense up, then exhale as it slithers off
into an adjoining hall.

00:03:11.570 --> 00:03:13.520
You’re safe - for now.

00:03:13.520 --> 00:03:17.520
The terminal has finally loaded and authenticated
your access.

00:03:17.520 --> 00:03:20.080
You’re staring at the file for S.D.

00:03:20.080 --> 00:03:24.530
Locke’s Proposal for SCP-001: When Day Breaks.

00:03:24.530 --> 00:03:30.240
It’s the only name you can give the apocalypse
your world is currently experiencing.

00:03:30.240 --> 00:03:35.420
This is one of the only anomalies in the entire
history of the SCP Foundation to be given

00:03:35.420 --> 00:03:41.470
the Apollyon containment class - meaning containment
is truly impossible.

00:03:41.470 --> 00:03:48.680
SCP-001 is the most dangerous enemy that the
Foundation, and planet earth, has ever faced.

00:03:48.680 --> 00:03:53.160
It’s always been a principle of the SCP
Foundation to battle in the dark so that the

00:03:53.160 --> 00:03:59.370
civilian world can thrive in the light, but
now, the light has become the enemy.

00:03:59.370 --> 00:04:04.180
Anyone exposed to any amount of sunlight for
even the briefest period of time is subjected

00:04:04.180 --> 00:04:10.730
to the effects of SCP-001 - and those effects
are beyond horrifying.

00:04:10.730 --> 00:04:15.350
The SCP Foundation Administrator released
an urgent memo telling Foundation personnel

00:04:15.350 --> 00:04:21.489
to make their way to Site-19 at all costs,
because they need all the help they can get.

00:04:21.489 --> 00:04:27.539
Those exposed to SCP-001 in the process are
no longer considered human - their new designation

00:04:27.539 --> 00:04:34.849
is SCP-001-A. These new entities are to be
avoided at all costs.

00:04:34.849 --> 00:04:40.539
But, in case of emergencies, the Administrator
says it is permitted to cut off parts of your

00:04:40.539 --> 00:04:44.999
transformed comrades and eat them to avoid
starvation.

00:04:44.999 --> 00:04:51.949
No attempt should be made to kill them, since
you won’t succeed, you’ll just put yourself

00:04:51.949 --> 00:04:53.199
at risk.

00:04:53.199 --> 00:05:00.509
When the sun changed and became SCP-001, it
instantly affected 6.8 billion innocent people.

00:05:00.509 --> 00:05:04.629
The second the visible light touched them
- whether it was from the sun itself or even

00:05:04.629 --> 00:05:11.309
reflected off the moon - their bodies liquefied,
melting like candle wax into puddles of living,

00:05:11.309 --> 00:05:12.930
gelatinous slime.

00:05:12.930 --> 00:05:18.490
This effect isn’t isolated to humans, either:
Any biological entity exposed to sunlight

00:05:18.490 --> 00:05:24.610
immediately underwent the same irreversible
melting process into SCP-001-A.

00:05:24.610 --> 00:05:29.789
And the horror had only just begun.

00:05:29.789 --> 00:05:35.979
People transformed into SCP-001-A will retain
shades of their former intelligence and personality.

00:05:35.979 --> 00:05:41.639
They may even try to will their new gooey
mass into a shape resembling their original

00:05:41.639 --> 00:05:42.639
form.

00:05:42.639 --> 00:05:46.789
However, these individuals will lose their
sense of self if they come into contact with

00:05:46.789 --> 00:05:55.750
other instances of SCP-001-A. When they come
together, 001-A instances will bond on a molecular

00:05:55.750 --> 00:06:02.520
level, wadding up into horrific, giant blobs
with only one purpose: Integrating more matter

00:06:02.520 --> 00:06:03.560
into their bodies.

00:06:03.560 --> 00:06:08.000
That’s why they have to be avoided at all
costs.

00:06:08.000 --> 00:06:11.509
You continue to search the computer terminal
for answers.

00:06:11.509 --> 00:06:15.009
Perhaps there was some kind of contingency
plan put in place for this.

00:06:15.009 --> 00:06:20.449
Some way to reverse the effects, or at least
escape the nightmare Earth has become.

00:06:20.449 --> 00:06:26.719
Instead, you find a series of attachments
linked to the SCP-001 file, detailing what

00:06:26.719 --> 00:06:31.800
seems to be the last days of the people who’d
barricaded themselves in the facility.

00:06:31.800 --> 00:06:37.419
Most prominent among these were Researcher
Dr. Logan Igotta, her partner, Ari, a security

00:06:37.419 --> 00:06:40.560
officer named Commander Anand, and a few others.

00:06:40.560 --> 00:06:45.370
Dr. Igotta had locked herself in the office,
where she recorded her final messages to the

00:06:45.370 --> 00:06:46.370
world.

00:06:46.370 --> 00:06:51.509
In the first audio log, Dr. Igotta and her
companions seemed afraid but hopeful that

00:06:51.509 --> 00:06:54.100
there may be some way out of this situation.

00:06:54.100 --> 00:06:58.960
Dr. Igotta reported that most of the workers
at the facility were transformed during the

00:06:58.960 --> 00:06:59.979
initial event.

00:06:59.979 --> 00:07:05.229
Their melted bodies had fused outside the
facility, and now they were trying to bust

00:07:05.229 --> 00:07:06.860
their way back in.

00:07:06.860 --> 00:07:11.439
The defenses had held so far though, and they
seemed confident they would hold long enough

00:07:11.439 --> 00:07:15.349
for them to figure out a way to escape this
awful situation.

00:07:15.349 --> 00:07:20.490
You open the second attachment - an incident
report - and realize that things may not have

00:07:20.490 --> 00:07:23.310
been as hopeful as Dr. Igotta let on.

00:07:23.310 --> 00:07:28.530
She reported hearing the huge mass of melted
creatures hammering on the door outside again,

00:07:28.530 --> 00:07:32.180
begging for them to come out and experience
the sun with them.

00:07:32.180 --> 00:07:35.979
They wanted desperately to add to their ever-growing
biomass.

00:07:35.979 --> 00:07:40.970
In order to experiment with what exactly would
happen, they sent out one of their few remaining

00:07:40.970 --> 00:07:43.649
D-Classes wearing a full protective suit.

00:07:43.649 --> 00:07:45.889
He didn’t last long.

00:07:45.889 --> 00:07:49.970
The huge creature grabbed him with tentacles
made of reconstituted flesh.

00:07:49.970 --> 00:07:54.229
It began ripping off his protective suit as
he screamed for mercy.

00:07:54.229 --> 00:07:57.610
It was a monster made of dozens of people
and animals.

00:07:57.610 --> 00:08:00.099
He could never overpower it.

00:08:00.099 --> 00:08:05.909
The second the sun touched his skin, he melted
away, and was absorbed by the great mass holding

00:08:05.909 --> 00:08:07.729
him in place.

00:08:07.729 --> 00:08:12.830
Guns were ineffective against these SCP-001-A
super-entities.

00:08:12.830 --> 00:08:14.559
Fire would do no good.

00:08:14.559 --> 00:08:19.639
It seemed that extremely low temperatures
were the only way to slow the immense blobs

00:08:19.639 --> 00:08:23.339
down, and even then, not permanently.

00:08:23.339 --> 00:08:28.569
There was one ray of light in the darkness:
The Site Director had a secret tunnel underneath

00:08:28.569 --> 00:08:33.571
his office, connected to a tram that could
hopefully take them directly to Site 19 without

00:08:33.571 --> 00:08:36.570
risk of SCP-001 exposure.

00:08:36.570 --> 00:08:41.029
It was a good plan, and by far the best option
they had available to them.

00:08:41.029 --> 00:08:45.440
But the best plans often don’t work in practice.

00:08:45.440 --> 00:08:47.930
You open the next attachment on the terminal.

00:08:47.930 --> 00:08:50.290
This time, it’s a video feed.

00:08:50.290 --> 00:08:55.630
You can actually see Dr. Logan Igotta - and
she looks harrowed by what she’s experienced.

00:08:55.630 --> 00:08:59.320
As it turns out, while the others, including
her partner Ari, attempted to exit through

00:08:59.320 --> 00:09:01.990
the tunnel something had happened.

00:09:01.990 --> 00:09:05.149
Dr. Igotta heard Ari’s voice over her radio.

00:09:05.149 --> 00:09:07.040
But there was something wrong with it.

00:09:07.040 --> 00:09:08.940
It was too low.

00:09:08.940 --> 00:09:09.940
Too guttural.

00:09:09.940 --> 00:09:11.779
And filled with gurgles.

00:09:11.779 --> 00:09:14.149
SCP-001 had gotten to her.

00:09:14.149 --> 00:09:15.500
She was changed.

00:09:15.500 --> 00:09:18.690
The monster from above had crawled in through
the ceiling.

00:09:18.690 --> 00:09:25.380
It had taken them, all of them, and converted
them into something less than human.

00:09:25.380 --> 00:09:28.529
Any hope of escape now seemed gone.

00:09:28.529 --> 00:09:30.360
Ari told Dr. Igotta that it would be fine.

00:09:30.360 --> 00:09:35.329
That it was such a bright, beautiful, sunny
day outside, and she was wasting it locked

00:09:35.329 --> 00:09:37.120
up inside that office.

00:09:37.120 --> 00:09:44.380
She tormented Dr. Igotta with their shared
memories of picnics in the park on sunny days

00:09:44.380 --> 00:09:45.630
in the past.

00:09:45.630 --> 00:09:49.279
The monster with Ari’s voice did everything
it could to try to convince Dr. Igotta to

00:09:49.279 --> 00:09:53.170
give up and join them, but she wasn’t ready
to go just yet.

00:09:53.170 --> 00:09:56.790
You look away from the screen when you hear
a sound in the corner.

00:09:56.790 --> 00:10:02.360
You see a dark puddle of some unknown substance,
and then, some skeletal hands rising out of

00:10:02.360 --> 00:10:03.360
it.

00:10:03.360 --> 00:10:07.410
The hands are pulling themselves out of the
puddle, followed by a skeletal face covered

00:10:07.410 --> 00:10:08.410
in matted hair.

00:10:08.410 --> 00:10:13.050
You have to stop yourself from screaming,
until a flash from a nearby security light

00:10:13.050 --> 00:10:14.639
makes the figure disappear.

00:10:14.639 --> 00:10:17.600
It’s a normal puddle again.

00:10:17.600 --> 00:10:19.879
Your mind is playing tricks on you.

00:10:19.879 --> 00:10:25.240
You open the next attachment, another video,
and see that Dr. Igotta’s condition has

00:10:25.240 --> 00:10:26.240
deteriorated.

00:10:26.240 --> 00:10:29.240
She looked pale, frantic, and thin.

00:10:29.240 --> 00:10:33.899
She was using a knife to draw her own blood
onto a piece of bloodstained parchment covered

00:10:33.899 --> 00:10:35.680
in strange symbols.

00:10:35.680 --> 00:10:41.040
Igotta ranted about her theory: What if 001
took the minds and bodies of its victims,

00:10:41.040 --> 00:10:42.480
but not their souls?

00:10:42.480 --> 00:10:47.290
Through performing some kind of arcane blood
ritual, she hoped to at least rescue and keep

00:10:47.290 --> 00:10:51.379
the soul of Ari, even if her mind and body
were lost.

00:10:51.379 --> 00:10:53.230
You open the next attachment.

00:10:53.230 --> 00:10:57.339
It seems that Igotta’s ritual worked, but
not in the way she hoped.

00:10:57.339 --> 00:11:03.000
The twisted soul of Ari, driven mad by SCP-001,
had taken over the file.

00:11:03.000 --> 00:11:08.639
It begins corrupting the text of the SCP-001
file into crazy ranting about how futile it

00:11:08.639 --> 00:11:09.670
is to fight.

00:11:09.670 --> 00:11:16.019
It then cut to an even more frightening video
feed: Dr. Igotta in her sleep, tossing and

00:11:16.019 --> 00:11:19.220
turning in a makeshift bed in the corner of
her office.

00:11:19.220 --> 00:11:24.480
The camera approaches her, in first person,
and lingers over her sleeping body.

00:11:24.480 --> 00:11:30.779
An oily, skeletal hand reaches past the camera,
and runs its fingers through Dr. Igotta’s

00:11:30.779 --> 00:11:31.779
hair.

00:11:31.779 --> 00:11:35.879
It’s the exact same hand you saw reaching
out of that black puddle earlier.

00:11:35.879 --> 00:11:39.230
You must have seen Ari’s lingering spirit.

00:11:39.230 --> 00:11:44.410
With a lump in your throat, you open the next
attachment and watch the video.

00:11:44.410 --> 00:11:47.670
You see Dr. Igotta, now truly broken.

00:11:47.670 --> 00:11:52.920
She’d been haunted by Ari’s demonic spirit
for a long time now, and it has clearly taken

00:11:52.920 --> 00:11:53.970
its toll.

00:11:53.970 --> 00:11:56.769
She is waving around a handgun while she speaks.

00:11:56.769 --> 00:11:59.839
She now believes there is only one way to
escape.

00:11:59.839 --> 00:12:00.990
But not like this.

00:12:00.990 --> 00:12:04.279
She doesn’t want the gun to draw attention
to her body.

00:12:04.279 --> 00:12:08.310
She doesn’t want to become part of that
mass, even if she is dead.

00:12:08.310 --> 00:12:12.730
She opens a drawer on the desk she’s recording
at and places the gun inside.

00:12:12.730 --> 00:12:19.010
Dr. Igotta then apologizes to her loved ones
who are likely long since dead or assimilated,

00:12:19.010 --> 00:12:22.079
and turns off the recording for the last time.

00:12:22.079 --> 00:12:26.740
In that moment, you realize that there’s
a single drawer in the desk in front of you.

00:12:26.740 --> 00:12:31.769
When you reach forward and open it, you see
that the same handgun Dr. Igotta was holding

00:12:31.769 --> 00:12:33.449
is laying inside.

00:12:33.449 --> 00:12:37.160
You pick it up and study it, weighing up your
options.

00:12:37.160 --> 00:12:39.790
Perhaps there truly is no other way out...

00:12:39.790 --> 00:12:42.120
Then, you see an update on the file.

00:12:42.120 --> 00:12:45.370
One more attachment had been added while you
were studying the gun.

00:12:45.370 --> 00:12:50.420
You feel your heart pounding in your chest
as you reach forward and open the attachment.

00:12:50.420 --> 00:12:53.579
The text has been changed entirely.

00:12:53.579 --> 00:13:00.740
The file on SCP-001 is now a poem - an ode
to the sun, and to ultimate togetherness.

00:13:00.740 --> 00:13:07.189
Then a video file spontaneously opens itself
on the screen: It’s a video of you, shot

00:13:07.189 --> 00:13:08.620
from behind.

00:13:08.620 --> 00:13:14.410
You see those oily, skeletal hands reaching
for you in the dark, just like they did with

00:13:14.410 --> 00:13:15.560
Dr. Igotta.

00:13:15.560 --> 00:13:20.810
In that moment, you panic and fire the gun
behind you, hoping to scare off the spirit.

00:13:20.810 --> 00:13:24.920
Instead, the sound of the gunshots attracts
something far worse.

00:13:24.920 --> 00:13:29.399
The immense blob of screaming, melted flesh
charges towards the office.

00:13:29.399 --> 00:13:32.180
You try to barricade the door, but it’s
not enough.

00:13:32.180 --> 00:13:36.439
The flesh seeps and bursts through, and grabs
you in its meaty tentacles.

00:13:36.439 --> 00:13:41.399
You scream and try to escape, but it won’t
save you.

00:13:41.399 --> 00:13:42.829
Nothing will save you.

00:13:42.829 --> 00:13:48.259
The flesh carries you upstairs, out through
the empty halls, out into the cave.

00:13:48.259 --> 00:13:51.899
You can see the light in the distance as the
blob ferries you towards it.

00:13:51.899 --> 00:13:54.200
You won’t be alone for much longer.

00:13:54.200 --> 00:13:57.279
In fact, you won’t be alone ever again...

00:13:57.279 --> 00:14:01.399
It’s tough to know how we might respond
to a true disaster.

00:14:01.399 --> 00:14:07.379
What might any of us do when the world turns
upside down, and nothing makes sense anymore?

00:14:07.379 --> 00:14:13.170
What if the simple act of stepping outside
into the sunlight became the deadliest thing

00:14:13.170 --> 00:14:14.170
in the world?

00:14:14.170 --> 00:14:19.060
We’ve delved into this horrifying notion
before with SCP-001, “When Day Breaks”,

00:14:19.060 --> 00:14:20.339
or S.D.

00:14:20.339 --> 00:14:21.630
Locke’s Proposal.

00:14:21.630 --> 00:14:26.620
One of the most nightmarish XK-Class End of
the World Scenarios ever devised.

00:14:26.620 --> 00:14:32.600
Mr. Rogers once said that the best thing to
do in times of trouble is “Look to the helpers.”

00:14:32.600 --> 00:14:37.959
No matter how dire things become, there will
always be those taking up the difficult work

00:14:37.959 --> 00:14:40.749
of putting their community back together.

00:14:40.749 --> 00:14:45.800
Firefighters, emergency medical services,
soup kitchens, there are so many different

00:14:45.800 --> 00:14:48.269
kinds of helpers out there.

00:14:48.269 --> 00:14:53.810
Unfortunately, most of them would be the first
to go once the sun’s rays turned on us all.

00:14:53.810 --> 00:14:58.240
The firefighter attempting to put out a blaze
would melt into a puddle of goo.

00:14:58.240 --> 00:15:03.170
The EMT running out of an ambulance would
melt the second his feet hit the pavement.

00:15:03.170 --> 00:15:08.000
When doom and destruction are everywhere,
who is left to help?

00:15:08.000 --> 00:15:12.390
Perhaps someone who has always dreamed of
saving humanity from a great plague, but is

00:15:12.390 --> 00:15:14.910
trapped inside with no sunlight in sight.

00:15:14.910 --> 00:15:21.160
When the whole world has grown sick, humanity
forever changed into something terrible, we

00:15:21.160 --> 00:15:23.329
just might need a Doctor.

00:15:23.329 --> 00:15:29.040
Today, we’re looking at what happens when
a dedicated physician meets his greatest challenge

00:15:29.040 --> 00:15:30.040
yet.

00:15:30.040 --> 00:15:33.050
We’ve discussed SCP-049 many times before.

00:15:33.050 --> 00:15:38.240
He is a humanoid entity, standing roughly
1.9 meters in height, resembling a man dressed

00:15:38.240 --> 00:15:40.620
in the garb of a Medieval plague doctor.

00:15:40.620 --> 00:15:46.120
He is clad in thick dark robes and a ceramic
mask that tapers into a long, sharp beak.

00:15:46.120 --> 00:15:52.750
Though it may look like a costume, these adornments
are actually a part of the entity’s body.

00:15:52.750 --> 00:15:59.319
SCP-049 wants one thing: to cure humankind
of a mysterious pestilence, the nature of

00:15:59.319 --> 00:16:02.670
which has never been entirely clear.

00:16:02.670 --> 00:16:06.399
Sadly, his work tends to do more harm than
good.

00:16:06.399 --> 00:16:12.059
He can kill with a single touch, and his attempts
to cure human subjects result in malformed

00:16:12.059 --> 00:16:13.649
zombies instead.

00:16:13.649 --> 00:16:18.259
He carries around a black doctor’s bag capable
of holding an impossible amount of medical

00:16:18.259 --> 00:16:21.540
implements, which he uses to perform his work.

00:16:21.540 --> 00:16:26.290
Though his actions tend to result in more
harm than good, the Plague Doctor seems to

00:16:26.290 --> 00:16:31.790
genuinely care about his patients, their well-being,
and curing them of the illness he believes

00:16:31.790 --> 00:16:34.399
to be ravaging their bodies.

00:16:34.399 --> 00:16:40.990
049 is singular in his obsession with eradicating
the plague he so often speaks of, and he will

00:16:40.990 --> 00:16:43.959
not stop until he has done so.

00:16:43.959 --> 00:16:48.709
But what does a doctor do, when there are
no more patients left to treat?

00:16:48.709 --> 00:17:25.620
When humanity has been wiped out, and the
Pestilence is gone 

00:17:25.620 --> 00:17:59.000
for good?

00:17:59.000 --> 00:18:15.140
The 

00:18:15.140 --> 00:18:20.330
Doctor sat in his containment cell, as he
did every other day of his captivity, doing

00:18:20.330 --> 00:18:22.210
his best to keep busy.

00:18:22.210 --> 00:18:27.060
The scientists of the SCP Foundation were
small-minded, they did not comprehend the

00:18:27.060 --> 00:18:31.870
gravity of his work, the necessity of ridding
the world of the Pestilence.

00:18:31.870 --> 00:18:37.030
He forgave them for their ignorance, for they
did not know what they were doing.

00:18:37.030 --> 00:18:43.720
Instead, he kept his mind on the goal, the
duty he had as a physician and a man of the

00:18:43.720 --> 00:18:44.860
sciences.

00:18:44.860 --> 00:18:49.400
Just as many scientists had done in the past
when their work was unappreciated, Copernicus

00:18:49.400 --> 00:18:53.610
was persecuted for his model of the universe
and his knowledge that the earth revolved

00:18:53.610 --> 00:18:58.610
around the sun, Jonas Salk for his controversial
invention of the polio vaccine that saved

00:18:58.610 --> 00:19:06.030
countless lives, SCP-049 would be remembered
fondly by history for his contributions.

00:19:06.030 --> 00:19:10.090
Even if his contemporaries did not understand
his vision.

00:19:10.090 --> 00:19:15.220
And so, in spite of the suspicion and doubt
that surrounded him at this Foundation that

00:19:15.220 --> 00:19:20.380
should have been all for groundbreaking research,
the Doctor carried on with his work.

00:19:20.380 --> 00:19:24.110
He sat on the floor of the cell, his bag open
next to him.

00:19:24.110 --> 00:19:29.350
From its endless interior, he withdrew syringes,
bottles of viscous liquids, tissue samples

00:19:29.350 --> 00:19:34.540
suspended in jars, and a stainless steel table
upon which he could place it all.

00:19:34.540 --> 00:19:38.830
Today, he would perform a new round of tests
on samples he had been able to extract from

00:19:38.830 --> 00:19:44.110
previous patients before he was thrown into
this unjust imprisonment.

00:19:44.110 --> 00:19:49.980
These doctors, men he had once viewed as colleagues,
had not brought him a new test subject in

00:19:49.980 --> 00:19:51.150
quite some time.

00:19:51.150 --> 00:19:56.430
A guard had muttered something about it being
“too costly” to keep “wasting perfectly

00:19:56.430 --> 00:19:59.020
good cows” on him.

00:19:59.020 --> 00:20:03.780
Simpletons, fools, luddites beating their
fists against their own chests in the face

00:20:03.780 --> 00:20:05.680
of true progress.

00:20:05.680 --> 00:20:09.050
Their small minds were of no concern to him.

00:20:09.050 --> 00:20:13.090
The Doctor placed his tools on his work table
and began to take out the samples.

00:20:13.090 --> 00:20:19.550
An ear, a piece of skin taken from the bottom
of a foot, a section of a liver, and, most

00:20:19.550 --> 00:20:23.560
exciting of all: the frontal lobe of a human
brain.

00:20:23.560 --> 00:20:28.840
Indeed, it would be a fruitful day of investigation
into the mysteries of The Great Dying.

00:20:28.840 --> 00:20:33.510
He was just pulling a scalpel from his bag
when his concentration was suddenly interrupted

00:20:33.510 --> 00:20:35.840
by the sound of a blaring siren.

00:20:35.840 --> 00:20:38.110
He set his tools down and looked up.

00:20:38.110 --> 00:20:41.470
That sound…it signified trouble at the facility.

00:20:41.470 --> 00:20:45.010
But surely it would be over soon, once the
issue had been dealt with.

00:20:45.010 --> 00:20:49.540
Outside his cell, he could hear the thundering
footsteps of guards running past, shouting

00:20:49.540 --> 00:20:52.150
at one another too quickly for him to understand.

00:20:52.150 --> 00:20:57.620
One man stopped to peer into 049’s cell,
making sure he was still contained, most likely.

00:20:57.620 --> 00:20:58.780
“My good fellow!”

00:20:58.780 --> 00:21:03.240
The Doctor called out, reminding himself to
speak in the King’s English so the man could

00:21:03.240 --> 00:21:04.240
understand.

00:21:04.240 --> 00:21:08.560
“Would you be so kind as to enlighten me…what
is the trouble?

00:21:08.560 --> 00:21:12.810
If anyone has been injured, I would be happy
to offer my assistance…”

00:21:12.810 --> 00:21:17.660
The man did not respond, his attention grabbed
by something further down the hall that the

00:21:17.660 --> 00:21:18.660
Doctor could not see.

00:21:18.660 --> 00:21:19.660
“Wait!”

00:21:19.660 --> 00:21:22.810
He cried, but the guard had already disappeared.

00:21:22.810 --> 00:21:27.780
The siren continued to blare, assaulting his
senses with its shrill tone.

00:21:27.780 --> 00:21:30.060
In the distance, he could hear screams.

00:21:30.060 --> 00:21:32.730
Surely, there was a dire situation at hand.

00:21:32.730 --> 00:21:35.200
Had that damnable reptile escaped again?

00:21:35.200 --> 00:21:39.440
He approached the door to his cell, listening
for further information.

00:21:39.440 --> 00:21:42.780
There was no roaring, no sounds of walls crumbling.

00:21:42.780 --> 00:21:49.940
There was only that blasted alarm, and screams
of unadulterated fear and agony.

00:21:49.940 --> 00:21:53.360
Very curious, and quite troubling.

00:21:53.360 --> 00:21:57.220
SCP-049 tried to open the door, knowing that
it was futile.

00:21:57.220 --> 00:22:01.380
He had never been able to escape before, why
would that change now?

00:22:01.380 --> 00:22:05.390
Still, a scientist must repeat his efforts
in order to gather new data.

00:22:05.390 --> 00:22:09.950
So, he thrust his shoulder against the door,
attempting to break it down.

00:22:09.950 --> 00:22:11.360
It did not budge.

00:22:11.360 --> 00:22:15.200
Instead, an automatic containment measure
was triggered, releasing lavender-scented

00:22:15.200 --> 00:22:18.410
gas into the room in an attempt to calm the
Doctor down.

00:22:18.410 --> 00:22:23.930
He did love the scent, it reminded him of
gentler times long ago.

00:22:23.930 --> 00:22:29.410
He took a deep breath, imagining fields of
purple flowers swaying in a summer breeze.

00:22:29.410 --> 00:22:35.180
That was the world he fought for, not the
flawed human beings that opposed him, not

00:22:35.180 --> 00:22:37.960
this sterile facility packed with the ignorant.

00:22:37.960 --> 00:22:40.520
For now, he would return to the work.

00:22:40.520 --> 00:22:44.990
If they did not desire his help, there was
nothing more to be done.

00:22:44.990 --> 00:22:49.790
Sometime later, perhaps hours, perhaps days,
he could not be certain when there was no

00:22:49.790 --> 00:22:53.950
sunlight to gauge the time by, the alarm fell
silent.

00:22:53.950 --> 00:22:56.720
The Doctor stopped his ministrations and listened.

00:22:56.720 --> 00:23:00.560
As the alarm had stopped, so too had the screams.

00:23:00.560 --> 00:23:06.770
In fact, the whole facility seemed to have
gone completely, eerily silent.

00:23:06.770 --> 00:23:07.850
“Hello?”

00:23:07.850 --> 00:23:12.390
He called out, but there was no response,
save for a clicking sound.

00:23:12.390 --> 00:23:15.320
Now, that was a sound he had heard before.

00:23:15.320 --> 00:23:19.760
Normally, it signified the arrival of armed
guards who would strap him into a harness,

00:23:19.760 --> 00:23:22.720
knock him out, and take him somewhere else.

00:23:22.720 --> 00:23:26.530
It was the sound of the door to his containment
cell unlocking.

00:23:26.530 --> 00:23:27.960
But there was no one there.

00:23:27.960 --> 00:23:29.410
Could it be…?

00:23:29.410 --> 00:23:34.030
Grabbing his faithful bag, SCP-049 cautiously
pushed against the door.

00:23:34.030 --> 00:23:38.300
It swung open, revealing a darkened, vacant
hallway.

00:23:38.300 --> 00:23:42.530
Gone were the harsh fluorescent lights, the
D-Class personnel mopping blood stains off

00:23:42.530 --> 00:23:47.180
the floors, the doctors hurrying by with clipboards
in hand.

00:23:47.180 --> 00:23:48.750
Everyone was gone.

00:23:48.750 --> 00:23:50.690
From what he could see, at least.

00:23:50.690 --> 00:23:54.200
He would have to investigate further in order
to know for sure.

00:23:54.200 --> 00:24:00.190
A scientist never assumes until they have
all of the available information, after all.

00:24:00.190 --> 00:24:06.140
So, SCP-049 walked the halls of the seemingly
abandoned Foundation site, expecting to be

00:24:06.140 --> 00:24:10.510
interrupted at any moment by an adversary
in a white coat who would usher him back to

00:24:10.510 --> 00:24:11.520
his prison.

00:24:11.520 --> 00:24:13.760
But the interruption never came.

00:24:13.760 --> 00:24:18.660
As he rounded the corner past one of the containment
facilities, a familiar voice called out to

00:24:18.660 --> 00:24:19.660
him.

00:24:19.660 --> 00:24:20.660
“Excuse me!

00:24:20.660 --> 00:24:21.660
Please, could you help me?

00:24:21.660 --> 00:24:22.810
Open the door!”

00:24:22.810 --> 00:24:25.830
049 stopped and peered into the room.

00:24:25.830 --> 00:24:30.360
It was the Possessive Mask’s room, SCP-035.

00:24:30.360 --> 00:24:35.510
The Doctor opened the door and there it was,
the porcelain mask grinning at him from its

00:24:35.510 --> 00:24:37.070
glass case.

00:24:37.070 --> 00:24:41.240
Thick black liquid oozed from its eyes and
wide-open mouth.

00:24:41.240 --> 00:24:43.910
“Oh wonderful, it’s you!”

00:24:43.910 --> 00:24:45.460
The mask gleefully enthused.

00:24:45.460 --> 00:24:51.990
“My good man, you gentleman and scholar,
would you please release me from this awful

00:24:51.990 --> 00:24:52.990
case?”

00:24:52.990 --> 00:24:57.720
049 paused, considering the potential ramifications
of this action.

00:24:57.720 --> 00:25:01.110
“No doubt you’ve noticed, things have
changed around here.”

00:25:01.110 --> 00:25:02.310
The mask continued.

00:25:02.310 --> 00:25:08.600
“The Foundation is different now…the old
rules have gone out the window.”

00:25:08.600 --> 00:25:10.890
“Hm, so it would seem.”

00:25:10.890 --> 00:25:12.000
The Doctor replied.

00:25:12.000 --> 00:25:13.930
“I will not wear you, understood?

00:25:13.930 --> 00:25:16.920
I will only release you from your cage.”

00:25:16.920 --> 00:25:21.250
“And for that I will be eternally grateful!”

00:25:21.250 --> 00:25:22.330
The mask promised.

00:25:22.330 --> 00:25:27.790
The Doctor approached the glass case in several
quick strides, and, with one gloved fist,

00:25:27.790 --> 00:25:28.870
broke through the glass.

00:25:28.870 --> 00:25:31.150
“Thank you, old friend!”

00:25:31.150 --> 00:25:32.370
The mask cried out.

00:25:32.370 --> 00:25:33.980
“Now, take me with you!”

00:25:33.980 --> 00:25:35.210
The Doctor shook his head.

00:25:35.210 --> 00:25:38.500
“I have more pressing matters to attend
to at the moment.

00:25:38.500 --> 00:25:45.230
If it is true, if they have all gone…Then
this facility is in dire need of a new head

00:25:45.230 --> 00:25:46.260
researcher.”

00:25:46.260 --> 00:25:52.350
At his words, the mask’s visage changed,
the cheerful smile warping into an exaggerated

00:25:52.350 --> 00:25:53.350
frown.

00:25:53.350 --> 00:25:58.450
“And you think you’re the man for the
job?

00:25:58.450 --> 00:25:59.730
How embarrassing.

00:25:59.730 --> 00:26:01.010
How truly…tragic.”

00:26:01.010 --> 00:26:02.930
“Bite your tongue!”

00:26:02.930 --> 00:26:03.930
The Doctor retorted.

00:26:03.930 --> 00:26:06.720
“Ah, but I don’t have one.

00:26:06.720 --> 00:26:08.270
Some physician you are!”

00:26:08.270 --> 00:26:09.970
The mask taunted.

00:26:09.970 --> 00:26:14.540
Unwilling to deal with the porcelain fiend’s
naysaying any longer, the Doctor turned and

00:26:14.540 --> 00:26:15.900
swept out of the room.

00:26:15.900 --> 00:26:21.220
Now was not the time for such verbal sparring,
he had so much to do.

00:26:21.220 --> 00:26:26.340
This whole facility could be his new workshop,
his laboratory, and he would need assistants

00:26:26.340 --> 00:26:27.950
to help him manage it.

00:26:27.950 --> 00:26:33.450
But who was qualified enough, loyal to the
cause, armed with sharp wit and a body free

00:26:33.450 --> 00:26:34.980
of Pestilence?

00:26:34.980 --> 00:26:39.380
As he made his way through the pitch black
halls, he felt something nudge his leg.

00:26:39.380 --> 00:26:44.940
He glanced down, uncertain what he would find,
and saw a teardrop-shaped orange creature

00:26:44.940 --> 00:26:50.210
nudging him affectionately, gazing up at him
with one large unblinking eye.

00:26:50.210 --> 00:26:55.150
Next to his other leg, a mustard yellow creature
the same shape and size was rolling toward

00:26:55.150 --> 00:26:56.150
him.

00:26:56.150 --> 00:26:57.660
“Are you friend or foe?”

00:26:57.660 --> 00:26:58.790
The Doctor asked.

00:26:58.790 --> 00:27:03.570
The creatures continued to nudge against his
legs, seeming to want something.

00:27:03.570 --> 00:27:04.920
Attention, perhaps?

00:27:04.920 --> 00:27:07.820
He bent down and patted each creature gently.

00:27:07.820 --> 00:27:12.430
They rolled around his feet in circles, making
a delighted babbling sound.

00:27:12.430 --> 00:27:14.270
“Ah, companion it is.

00:27:14.270 --> 00:27:16.030
Well then, come along.”

00:27:16.030 --> 00:27:20.040
As he continued along his path, the little
creatures rolled after him, keeping close

00:27:20.040 --> 00:27:21.090
to his heels.

00:27:21.090 --> 00:27:25.350
Suddenly, they began to babble loudly, in
a panicked tone, swarming around the front

00:27:25.350 --> 00:27:27.140
of his feet as if to stop him.

00:27:27.140 --> 00:27:30.280
“Is there a danger here, my ocular friends?”

00:27:30.280 --> 00:27:31.320
The Doctor asked.

00:27:31.320 --> 00:27:34.390
From the cell up ahead, a voice cried out
in French.

00:27:34.390 --> 00:27:35.390
“Help!

00:27:35.390 --> 00:27:36.390
Please!

00:27:36.390 --> 00:27:37.480
I’m so hungry!”

00:27:37.480 --> 00:27:42.370
The Doctor rushed to the door, opening it
to reveal a behemoth of a man with a large,

00:27:42.370 --> 00:27:47.110
rounded chin and jaw, and deep-set eyes that
peered down at him.

00:27:47.110 --> 00:27:50.200
The man grinned at the Doctor with impossibly
large teeth.

00:27:50.200 --> 00:27:51.200
“Thank you, Sir!

00:27:51.200 --> 00:27:53.660
I was sure that I would die in there.”

00:27:53.660 --> 00:27:59.070
As he stepped out into the light, the Doctor
could see this giant was dressed in a military

00:27:59.070 --> 00:28:00.070
uniform.

00:28:00.070 --> 00:28:01.700
“Are you a military man?”

00:28:01.700 --> 00:28:02.700
The Doctor asked.

00:28:02.700 --> 00:28:05.860
“Yes, I was a soldier in the French army
once.”

00:28:05.860 --> 00:28:06.860
The giant replied.

00:28:06.860 --> 00:28:10.630
“Before I became the greatest detective
the world has ever known.”

00:28:10.630 --> 00:28:15.490
The Doctor clapped his hands delightedly,
his eyes glittering from within his mask.

00:28:15.490 --> 00:28:18.280
“Intelligence and loyalty, not to mention
strength!

00:28:18.280 --> 00:28:22.080
You, my good man, are everything I have been
searching for!

00:28:22.080 --> 00:28:25.120
Would you do me the honor of working as my
assistant?

00:28:25.120 --> 00:28:30.650
I have a great deal of research to do, and
could make use of a man with your many talents.”

00:28:30.650 --> 00:28:33.100
The giant tipped his hat politely to the Doctor.

00:28:33.100 --> 00:28:35.160
“It would be my honor, Sir.

00:28:35.160 --> 00:28:37.300
You may call me Fernand.”

00:28:37.300 --> 00:28:40.630
He reached out for a handshake, but the Doctor
shook his head.

00:28:40.630 --> 00:28:43.150
“It would be best if I did not.

00:28:43.150 --> 00:28:44.880
But it is a pleasure to make your acquaintance!”

00:28:44.880 --> 00:28:48.410
He glanced down at the eye pods, still skittering
about anxiously.

00:28:48.410 --> 00:28:50.100
“See, there is nothing to fear here.”

00:28:50.100 --> 00:28:54.310
“Unless I don’t find something to eat
soon…”

00:28:54.310 --> 00:28:55.310
Fernand quipped.

00:28:55.310 --> 00:29:00.290
And so the Doctor continued along his way,
with Fernand and his new pets in tow.

00:29:00.290 --> 00:29:04.760
This was an excellent advancement, but he
would need a great deal more help if he was

00:29:04.760 --> 00:29:07.080
to effectively run the Foundation.

00:29:07.080 --> 00:29:11.670
He recruited two other anomalies with a mind
for scientific research: a small bird with

00:29:11.670 --> 00:29:17.070
a deafening voice, and a doctor himself, and
an eloquent sea slug with years of hunting

00:29:17.070 --> 00:29:19.240
and adventuring experience.

00:29:19.240 --> 00:29:22.820
It was a relief to find fellow intellectuals
here.

00:29:22.820 --> 00:29:27.700
As the ever-increasing band of anomalies approached
the basement, Fernand perked up noticeably.

00:29:27.700 --> 00:29:29.020
“Something smells…wonderful.

00:29:29.020 --> 00:29:31.640
Just beyond this door!”

00:29:31.640 --> 00:29:35.430
Fernand opened the door, and led the group
into a treasure trove of corpses.

00:29:35.430 --> 00:29:38.710
They had found the Foundation’s morgue.

00:29:38.710 --> 00:29:43.170
Rows and rows of drawers filled with test
subjects, and food for Fernand.

00:29:43.170 --> 00:29:46.270
It was everything the Doctor could have possibly
wanted.

00:29:46.270 --> 00:29:51.380
“My dear Fernand, take as many as you can
carry and follow me to the laboratory!”

00:29:51.380 --> 00:29:52.380
The Doctor declared.

00:29:52.380 --> 00:29:53.380
“Tally ho!”

00:29:53.380 --> 00:29:54.580
Cried the slug.

00:29:54.580 --> 00:29:55.580
“Cack!

00:29:55.580 --> 00:29:56.580
And how!”

00:29:56.580 --> 00:29:57.580
Dr. Spanko added.

00:29:57.580 --> 00:30:01.350
The Doctor set up shop in one of the many
expansive on-site labs.

00:30:01.350 --> 00:30:05.650
Though the electricity was out, he could still
get a great deal of use out of it.

00:30:05.650 --> 00:30:10.650
There was so much more space in here, so many
more resources than his tiny cell had!

00:30:10.650 --> 00:30:15.030
Within a matter of hours, he had set up a
row of IVs pumping out his serums into test

00:30:15.030 --> 00:30:18.970
subjects, reanimating them into more lab assistants.

00:30:18.970 --> 00:30:24.070
The newly revived corpses shambled around
the lab, bringing him bone saws and forceps,

00:30:24.070 --> 00:30:29.480
vials and needles of all sizes, as the Doctor
worked harder than he had in years.

00:30:29.480 --> 00:30:33.390
The others were less help than he had hoped,
but with so many newly treated patients to

00:30:33.390 --> 00:30:35.920
share the workload he hardly noticed.

00:30:35.920 --> 00:30:41.180
All of these poor souls, victims of the Pestilence
no doubt, and he had all the time in the world

00:30:41.180 --> 00:30:42.830
now to cure them.

00:30:42.830 --> 00:30:46.760
Before long, the once-vacant halls of the
Foundation were filled with the slow plodding

00:30:46.760 --> 00:30:51.590
footsteps of his patients, dragging their
swollen limbs across the ground, blinking

00:30:51.590 --> 00:30:56.380
their milky white eyes at the chaos outside
the windows of the facility.

00:30:56.380 --> 00:31:01.570
As 049 was absorbed in his work, the rest
of the escaped anomalies began to realize

00:31:01.570 --> 00:31:04.290
that their freedom had come at a cost.

00:31:04.290 --> 00:31:09.220
Where there had once been friendly yellow
sunlight beaming down on a lush green world,

00:31:09.220 --> 00:31:15.110
there was now an angry red fire in the sky,
and a landscape covered in strange gelatinous

00:31:15.110 --> 00:31:18.650
masses, roving and oozing and moaning as one.

00:31:18.650 --> 00:31:22.360
They had escaped from their cells, but they
were still trapped.

00:31:22.360 --> 00:31:24.790
Their prison had just gotten larger.

00:31:24.790 --> 00:31:30.120
The Doctor had not yet noticed this unfortunate
truth, so focused was he on treating the patients

00:31:30.120 --> 00:31:32.140
they had recovered from the morgue.

00:31:32.140 --> 00:31:36.570
At least, the ones that had not been given
to Fernand to satisfy his mammoth appetite.

00:31:36.570 --> 00:31:41.870
However, a week later, the Doctor was horrified
to discover that there were no more corpses

00:31:41.870 --> 00:31:44.340
left, no more patients to cure.

00:31:44.340 --> 00:31:49.680
He stood in the empty morgue, looking from
drawer to drawer for just one body they had

00:31:49.680 --> 00:31:53.200
missed, proof that his work was not over so
soon.

00:31:53.200 --> 00:31:54.780
“What’s the matter, Doctor?”

00:31:54.780 --> 00:31:57.290
A sardonic voice behind him said.

00:31:57.290 --> 00:32:01.700
He turned to find SCP-035, attached to the
face of a mannequin.

00:32:01.700 --> 00:32:04.010
“I see you procured a body.”

00:32:04.010 --> 00:32:05.010
He noted.

00:32:05.010 --> 00:32:06.510
“No thanks to you.”

00:32:06.510 --> 00:32:07.660
The mask replied.

00:32:07.660 --> 00:32:08.930
“Oh, how sad.

00:32:08.930 --> 00:32:11.410
A Doctor with no one to treat.

00:32:11.410 --> 00:32:14.290
What will you do now that you have no purpose
in life?

00:32:14.290 --> 00:32:15.330
I wonder…”

00:32:15.330 --> 00:32:16.780
“There will be more.”

00:32:16.780 --> 00:32:17.870
The Doctor insisted.

00:32:17.870 --> 00:32:20.740
“There is an entire world out there that
needs my help.”

00:32:20.740 --> 00:32:23.190
The mask laughed in disbelief.

00:32:23.190 --> 00:32:25.840
“You mean you haven’t noticed?

00:32:25.840 --> 00:32:27.940
There is no world left to help.

00:32:27.940 --> 00:32:29.710
It’s all gone to hell.

00:32:29.710 --> 00:32:31.450
Take a look for yourself.”

00:32:31.450 --> 00:32:35.740
The Doctor rushed to an upstairs window and
gazed down at the destruction outside.

00:32:35.740 --> 00:32:41.520
The painful red sunlight, the rubble where
there once were buildings, the masses of soft,

00:32:41.520 --> 00:32:44.200
gooey matter that aimlessly squelched along
the ground.

00:32:44.200 --> 00:32:45.200
“No…”

00:32:45.200 --> 00:32:47.980
He put a hand to his beak, shaking his head.

00:32:47.980 --> 00:32:49.870
“It can’t be…”

00:32:49.870 --> 00:32:51.760
“But it is.”

00:32:51.760 --> 00:32:53.950
Said the mask, standing behind him.

00:32:53.950 --> 00:32:58.080
Its face was locked in a grin, delighted to
see him suffering so.

00:32:58.080 --> 00:33:02.160
“Your life’s mission, gone…just like
that.”

00:33:02.160 --> 00:33:05.270
The Doctor’s knees went weak as he tried
to take it all in.

00:33:05.270 --> 00:33:08.900
Perhaps the mask was right, he had no purpose
anymore…

00:33:08.900 --> 00:33:11.070
But then, Fernand’s voice boomed from below.

00:33:11.070 --> 00:33:12.070
“Doctor!

00:33:12.070 --> 00:33:14.870
We need your assistance downstairs!

00:33:14.870 --> 00:33:15.870
Quickly!”

00:33:15.870 --> 00:33:20.110
The Doctor followed his assistant’s cries
and saw what was so urgent: one of the blob

00:33:20.110 --> 00:33:24.720
creatures, an entity he knew must have once
been a human being, had found its way inside

00:33:24.720 --> 00:33:26.170
the facility.

00:33:26.170 --> 00:33:29.640
Fernand stood back from it, afraid to touch
its slimy surface.

00:33:29.640 --> 00:33:34.620
But SCP-049 did not see a monster there, oozing
across the floor.

00:33:34.620 --> 00:33:39.710
He saw someone who was sick, desperately ill,
and in need of help.

00:33:39.710 --> 00:33:41.470
In need of a Doctor.

00:33:41.470 --> 00:33:43.450
“Do not be afraid, my friend.”

00:33:43.450 --> 00:33:48.280
He sat down his bag, and reached inside, as
his reanimated helpers swarmed around the

00:33:48.280 --> 00:33:49.280
blob creature.

00:33:49.280 --> 00:33:53.530
“You are not well, but I am going to help
you.”

00:33:53.530 --> 00:33:58.260
The Great Dying may have been out of his grasp,
but if the scene outside the window was anything

00:33:58.260 --> 00:34:02.420
to go on, there was still a plague raging
across the earth.

00:34:02.420 --> 00:34:12.050
And he was going to find 
the cure.

00:34:12.050 --> 00:34:14.470
It was such a normal morning.

00:34:14.470 --> 00:34:15.780
Almost comically uneventful.

00:34:15.780 --> 00:34:21.409
For the first time in what felt like forever,
the morning newscasts had very little to say.

00:34:21.409 --> 00:34:23.159
They covered fashion week.

00:34:23.159 --> 00:34:24.570
Some protests in the capital.

00:34:24.570 --> 00:34:26.730
A new health craze sweeping the nation.

00:34:26.730 --> 00:34:30.379
A funny viral video of a dog eating a pancake.

00:34:30.379 --> 00:34:34.040
The inspirational story of a young boy from
the midwest beating cancer.

00:34:34.040 --> 00:34:38.830
A minor political scandal out of Tallahassee,
Florida, that nobody would remember in a week.

00:34:38.830 --> 00:34:43.480
A round of interviews with an expert who’d
written a new book about the long-term effects

00:34:43.480 --> 00:34:45.750
social media might have on our children.

00:34:45.750 --> 00:34:51.629
None of them knew back then that “long term”
was a luxury they couldn’t afford.

00:34:51.629 --> 00:34:52.860
People went to work.

00:34:52.860 --> 00:34:53.860
To school.

00:34:53.860 --> 00:34:55.610
Some took the day off.

00:34:55.610 --> 00:34:59.850
Plenty took walks or jogs, deciding to exercise
outside for a change.

00:34:59.850 --> 00:35:03.350
After all, it was such a beautiful, sunny
day out.

00:35:03.350 --> 00:35:08.160
What a terrible shame it would be to waste
the light indoors.

00:35:08.160 --> 00:35:09.630
People were sleepwalking.

00:35:09.630 --> 00:35:14.850
So placidly, blissfully unaware of what was
rattling down the tracks towards them.

00:35:14.850 --> 00:35:18.320
It was an uncharacteristically slow day down
at Site-19.

00:35:18.320 --> 00:35:22.210
There hadn’t been any containment breaches
in over a week.

00:35:22.210 --> 00:35:27.040
A couple of new anomalies had been brought
into containment, one safe-class and one Euclid,

00:35:27.040 --> 00:35:32.260
but neither were the kind that was likely
to bring the SCP Foundation any trouble.

00:35:32.260 --> 00:35:36.520
Right now, the biggest threat facing their
employees seemed to be boredom, and they’d

00:35:36.520 --> 00:35:40.700
definitely take that over any other members
of their rogues gallery.

00:35:40.700 --> 00:35:45.860
Dr. Bright poured himself a mug of nice, hot
coffee, and decided to watch some TV in the

00:35:45.860 --> 00:35:48.100
break room, just to pass the time.

00:35:48.100 --> 00:35:52.760
The President of the United States was giving
a press conference on the White House lawn,

00:35:52.760 --> 00:35:54.240
surrounded by microphones.

00:35:54.240 --> 00:35:56.590
It was really nothing special.

00:35:56.590 --> 00:36:01.100
Just the same inane babble about how he was
going to fix the deficit, and with inflation

00:36:01.100 --> 00:36:05.110
on the rise, we’d all need to work on being
more fiscally responsible.

00:36:05.110 --> 00:36:08.160
The immortal Foundation researcher sipped
his coffee.

00:36:08.160 --> 00:36:12.510
These normal, boring problems felt like the
perfect escape from the insanity he needed

00:36:12.510 --> 00:36:15.050
to deal with every day at the Foundation.

00:36:15.050 --> 00:36:19.420
The President was saying something about the
importance of families, and about farmers

00:36:19.420 --> 00:36:22.670
being the nation’s real backbone, when something
happened.

00:36:22.670 --> 00:36:26.030
There was an odd shift in the quality of the
light.

00:36:26.030 --> 00:36:30.310
Dr. Bright barely registered it - Maybe it
was something to do with the cameras.

00:36:30.310 --> 00:36:35.620
However, things started to get stranger: The
President’s speech began to slur, as though

00:36:35.620 --> 00:36:39.200
he’d just been pumped with enough morphine
to take down an elephant.

00:36:39.200 --> 00:36:42.600
But it wasn’t just that - He was sweating
buckets, too.

00:36:42.600 --> 00:36:48.420
Wet patches expanded all over his suit and
perspiration was dripping off his skin.

00:36:48.420 --> 00:36:53.260
Dr. Bright was in suspense - Was he about
to watch the president have a stroke, live

00:36:53.260 --> 00:36:54.260
on air?

00:36:54.260 --> 00:36:56.560
Would he be called in to replace him yet again?

00:36:56.560 --> 00:37:01.100
But no, the situation unfolding was far, far
worse.

00:37:01.100 --> 00:37:05.390
The president of the united states slumped
forward over his podium while the reporting

00:37:05.390 --> 00:37:06.710
corps screamed.

00:37:06.710 --> 00:37:12.120
His face sloughed off his skull like melting
wax, the president’s words slurring off

00:37:12.120 --> 00:37:13.740
into infinity.

00:37:13.740 --> 00:37:18.760
It was the worst a US president had looked
on film since SCP-1981.

00:37:18.760 --> 00:37:22.000
Dr. Bright dropped his coffee cup.

00:37:22.000 --> 00:37:24.630
It tumbled and shattered on the ground below.

00:37:24.630 --> 00:37:29.310
The camera fell as the operator screamed:
It pointed down into the crowd, where the

00:37:29.310 --> 00:37:33.740
reporters were shrieking in pain and terror,
steam coming off of their bodies with the

00:37:33.740 --> 00:37:36.120
sudden, intense climb in heat.

00:37:36.120 --> 00:37:38.560
They were melting.

00:37:38.560 --> 00:37:43.860
All of them were melting before his very eyes,
broadcast out to how many people?

00:37:43.860 --> 00:37:47.660
It would be the mother of all containment
nightmares.

00:37:47.660 --> 00:37:53.700
Little did Dr. Bright know, that wasn’t
even the half of the true horrors unfolding.

00:37:53.700 --> 00:37:56.930
Alarms went off across every containment site
in the world.

00:37:56.930 --> 00:38:01.340
Any SCP Foundation employees unlucky enough
to be standing outside at the time were lost

00:38:01.340 --> 00:38:03.800
in the rapidly unfolding horrors.

00:38:03.800 --> 00:38:09.930
They screamed as the sun cleaved their atoms
apart, reducing them into semi-liquid states

00:38:09.930 --> 00:38:12.290
without the mercy of death.

00:38:12.290 --> 00:38:16.840
People lucky to be just out of the sun could
only watch as those less fortunate disintegrated

00:38:16.840 --> 00:38:20.550
across the ground in unholy shrieking puddles.

00:38:20.550 --> 00:38:27.500
Never had such a normal day been thrown into
such terrifying chaos in so little time.

00:38:27.500 --> 00:38:33.470
Billions of voices cried out at once as the
sun changed in the sky up above them.

00:38:33.470 --> 00:38:36.690
Since the dawn of humanity, it had given us
everything.

00:38:36.690 --> 00:38:42.330
Light to bask in, warmth to keep us safe,
and the life of the plants and animals that

00:38:42.330 --> 00:38:43.500
kept us fed.

00:38:43.500 --> 00:38:48.270
It had been worshipped as a god by countless
cultures over thousands of years.

00:38:48.270 --> 00:38:51.440
One great mother to all of humanity.

00:38:51.440 --> 00:38:56.790
And now, that mother was drowning us in the
bathtub - And perhaps the most frightening

00:38:56.790 --> 00:38:59.630
part of all, it was for seemingly no reason.

00:38:59.630 --> 00:39:02.870
No reason at all.

00:39:02.870 --> 00:39:06.700
The SCP Foundation was forced to now break
their silence forever.

00:39:06.700 --> 00:39:11.960
The masquerade, the veil, it melted along
with so much of humanity.

00:39:11.960 --> 00:39:16.140
They took over every communications channel
in the world and did what they could to inform

00:39:16.140 --> 00:39:18.750
people on how to get out of harm’s way.

00:39:18.750 --> 00:39:19.920
Stay inside.

00:39:19.920 --> 00:39:24.080
Wrap yourself in sun-shielding clothing and
only move at night.

00:39:24.080 --> 00:39:26.860
Air travel is preferable, if possible.

00:39:26.860 --> 00:39:31.780
If you can, make your way to one of the SCP
Foundation’s secure sites, their only chance

00:39:31.780 --> 00:39:36.320
of preserving humanity and figuring out how
to reverse this new nightmare.

00:39:36.320 --> 00:39:41.750
Now more than ever, the SCP Foundation would
be humanity’s only hope.

00:39:41.750 --> 00:39:46.100
Until, of course, there was another terrifying
twist in the tail.

00:39:46.100 --> 00:39:50.530
While those who were melted into piles of
living flesh sludge were sadly assumed to

00:39:50.530 --> 00:39:55.340
be lost, even the Foundation didn’t expect
the transformed humans to become a threat

00:39:55.340 --> 00:39:57.500
in and of themselves.

00:39:57.500 --> 00:40:01.120
Just as their bodies were melted, so were
their minds.

00:40:01.120 --> 00:40:06.480
They became slaves, cultists of the glowing
tyrant hanging up above.

00:40:06.480 --> 00:40:12.530
Some would coagulate into huge, fleshy masses,
horrifying threats that would seek out victims,

00:40:12.530 --> 00:40:18.640
overpower them, and drag them out into the
light to be absorbed and transformed.

00:40:18.640 --> 00:40:22.740
Even those hiding inside Foundation containment
centers weren’t safe.

00:40:22.740 --> 00:40:28.010
These behemoths of melted flesh would find
their way in, using their many twisted voices

00:40:28.010 --> 00:40:33.480
to slowly break the minds of their victims,
then gather them up with great flesh tendrils

00:40:33.480 --> 00:40:39.070
and yank them out to their doom. Little by
little, the numbers of humanity dwindled.

00:40:39.070 --> 00:40:44.390
It looked like we had a bright future ahead
of us - And in this particular context, that’s

00:40:44.390 --> 00:40:45.610
far from a good thing.

00:40:45.610 --> 00:40:51.560
Survivors, for whatever time they have left,
would forever remember this waking nightmare.

00:40:51.560 --> 00:40:58.040
The Foundation dubbed it SCP-001, to reflect
its ultimacy, but to everyone else, it had

00:40:58.040 --> 00:41:01.190
a different name: When Day Breaks.

00:41:01.190 --> 00:41:05.990
In a world where anything but darkness will
kill you, is there anywhere left on earth

00:41:05.990 --> 00:41:09.090
that’s truly safe from the horrors of SCP-001?

00:41:09.090 --> 00:41:14.790
Five months into the never-ending horror of
the solar singularity, Alice spooned herself

00:41:14.790 --> 00:41:17.690
a bowl full of warm, Swedish meatballs.

00:41:17.690 --> 00:41:18.790
Delicious.

00:41:18.790 --> 00:41:23.170
She was part of an investigative detail from
a nearby community, deep within the bowels

00:41:23.170 --> 00:41:31.300
of the only truly safe place for human beings
on planet earth: SCP-3008, also known as the

00:41:31.300 --> 00:41:32.650
Infinite Ikea.

00:41:32.650 --> 00:41:34.930
No sun has ever shone in there.

00:41:34.930 --> 00:41:38.670
Only the lights, which fizz and flicker far
above.

00:41:38.670 --> 00:41:43.880
Everyone inside thinks of it as a prison - Little
do they know, what’s left of humanity out

00:41:43.880 --> 00:41:46.710
there would kill to be inside this sanctuary.

00:41:46.710 --> 00:41:49.130
Alice had a team of ten with her.

00:41:49.130 --> 00:41:54.390
Their mission was simple: Continue the mapping
effort of the area surrounding their community,

00:41:54.390 --> 00:41:56.710
and collect rations for the rest of the camp.

00:41:56.710 --> 00:42:01.940
It was dangerous work, especially as night
drew close, but there were certain rewards.

00:42:01.940 --> 00:42:07.000
When they reached a feeding station, they
were able to enjoy fresher food than anyone

00:42:07.000 --> 00:42:08.000
else.

00:42:08.000 --> 00:42:10.550
All they needed to do was wager their lives
for it.

00:42:10.550 --> 00:42:13.250
To Alice, it seemed like a fair trade.

00:42:13.250 --> 00:42:15.850
Her lieutenant, Darcy, kept watch to the north.

00:42:15.850 --> 00:42:20.860
He and several others wielded makeshift spears
made from curtain rods and clubs fashioned

00:42:20.860 --> 00:42:21.980
from bedposts.

00:42:21.980 --> 00:42:26.940
Knives, scavenged from the kitchen section,
hung from their belts in makeshift holsters.

00:42:26.940 --> 00:42:29.590
During the day, they were little more than
a precaution.

00:42:29.590 --> 00:42:34.180
At night, it could mean the difference between
life and death.

00:42:34.180 --> 00:42:37.910
Speaking of, Darcy kept a close watch on a
member of staff.

00:42:37.910 --> 00:42:43.920
It was a lanky, faceless monster in that garish
uniform, with a bulbous, oversized head, and

00:42:43.920 --> 00:42:49.480
long arms, hanging slack like pulled taffy,
dragging its knuckles along the ground as

00:42:49.480 --> 00:42:51.210
it stumbled along.

00:42:51.210 --> 00:42:55.280
Darcy thought it darkly funny, how stupid
they could look in the daytime.

00:42:55.280 --> 00:42:58.290
Like crash test dummies rejected from the
assembly line.

00:42:58.290 --> 00:42:59.869
Bear stood close by.

00:42:59.869 --> 00:43:04.890
He never gave any of them his real name, so
they took to calling him Bear, because he

00:43:04.890 --> 00:43:09.840
was big, hairy, and would probably tear you
to shreds if you got on his bad side.

00:43:09.840 --> 00:43:15.080
He wielded the largest club of all - A customized
creation, with sharp objects sticking out

00:43:15.080 --> 00:43:17.080
of it on all sides.

00:43:17.080 --> 00:43:20.030
It cleaved the heads of many staff members
off of their bodies.

00:43:20.030 --> 00:43:24.160
Bear seemed to relish the task of putting
those monsters down.

00:43:24.160 --> 00:43:26.420
For him, it was easy and fun.

00:43:26.420 --> 00:43:31.170
That’s why he was an essential asset on
these missions: A berserker.

00:43:31.170 --> 00:43:35.060
Most of the grunts shoveled as much food as
they could carry in Ikea-brand tupperware

00:43:35.060 --> 00:43:36.230
and coolers.

00:43:36.230 --> 00:43:40.160
The more they gathered, the longer it would
take for them to be forced out beyond the

00:43:40.160 --> 00:43:45.100
safe walls of the haven they’d built inside
this Swedish, flat-pack hell.

00:43:45.100 --> 00:43:47.750
They all made do with what they had in here.

00:43:47.750 --> 00:43:49.840
It was all they could really do.

00:43:49.840 --> 00:43:55.150
Two members of the team were unaccounted for:
Cyril and Joseph, what passed for reconnaissance

00:43:55.150 --> 00:43:56.470
experts down here.

00:43:56.470 --> 00:44:01.760
They seemed to have an innate sense of direction,
as though they were somehow in tune with the

00:44:01.760 --> 00:44:03.190
Ikea itself.

00:44:03.190 --> 00:44:05.540
They would be sent out on scouting missions.

00:44:05.540 --> 00:44:12.340
Searching for resources, food, other survivors,
and most prized of all: Potential escape routes.

00:44:12.340 --> 00:44:17.800
It was a dream, a fantasy, that someday one
of them would find the exit, and lead the

00:44:17.800 --> 00:44:20.119
rest of them to salvation through it.

00:44:20.119 --> 00:44:24.810
Alice had been trapped in the Infinite Ikea
for six years, going on seven now.

00:44:24.810 --> 00:44:27.870
She had long since given up on dreams of escape.

00:44:27.870 --> 00:44:33.020
All they could do was accept their situation,
get used to it, and just try to survive under

00:44:33.020 --> 00:44:34.270
these circumstances.

00:44:34.270 --> 00:44:38.420
But those circumstances were about to change.

00:44:38.420 --> 00:44:40.520
Everyone looked up when they heard panicked
breathing.

00:44:40.520 --> 00:44:41.900
It was Cyril.

00:44:41.900 --> 00:44:43.500
Just Cyril.

00:44:43.500 --> 00:44:48.310
The party clutched their weapons just that
little bit tighter, unsure of what had happened.

00:44:48.310 --> 00:44:54.240
Their rules were clear: Scouting duos must
never, ever separate, under any circumstances.

00:44:54.240 --> 00:44:58.160
Getting lost in the store, and being isolated
when the lights went out, would be a death

00:44:58.160 --> 00:44:59.160
sentence.

00:44:59.160 --> 00:45:04.340
So if Cyril was returning alone, frightened,
tears streaking down his face, then something

00:45:04.340 --> 00:45:06.540
truly awful had happened.

00:45:06.540 --> 00:45:10.640
He told Alice and the rest of the group that
something had attacked him and Joseph.

00:45:10.640 --> 00:45:14.550
When Alice told him that was impossible, that
the Staff wouldn’t show active aggression

00:45:14.550 --> 00:45:19.620
until lights out unless someone attacked them
first, Cyril shook his head and let out a

00:45:19.620 --> 00:45:21.080
heaving sob.

00:45:21.080 --> 00:45:24.869
He told them that the creature that attacked
them wasn’t a member of the Staff.

00:45:24.869 --> 00:45:27.940
It was something else entirely.

00:45:27.940 --> 00:45:31.700
Something he’d never seen inside the Ikea
before.

00:45:31.700 --> 00:45:33.850
A true monster.

00:45:33.850 --> 00:45:37.920
Whatever this creature was, it’d moved around
a corner incredibly fast.

00:45:37.920 --> 00:45:42.650
It spoke in a way that was almost human, but
something was off about it.

00:45:42.650 --> 00:45:46.250
Something that sent a chill down Cyril’s
spine, just thinking about it.

00:45:46.250 --> 00:45:48.680
He recalled that the creature was large and
blob-like.

00:45:48.680 --> 00:45:54.550
Flesh-colored, with long, grasping tendrils
that whipped and flailed unnaturally.

00:45:54.550 --> 00:45:57.560
These tendrils had wrapped around and grasped
Joseph.

00:45:57.560 --> 00:45:59.160
He hadn’t been quick enough.

00:45:59.160 --> 00:46:04.590
The two of them were too shocked by the sight
of it to react in time, and then, it was already

00:46:04.590 --> 00:46:05.670
too late.

00:46:05.670 --> 00:46:09.470
It grabbed Joseph and yanked him off into
an adjoining aisle.

00:46:09.470 --> 00:46:15.090
Cyril still remembered his haunting screams
getting quieter and quieter as he moved further

00:46:15.090 --> 00:46:16.300
out of sight.

00:46:16.300 --> 00:46:18.890
On some level, everyone hoped that Cyril was
lying.

00:46:18.890 --> 00:46:23.900
It was preferable to believe that he himself
had snapped and murdered Joseph for some unknown

00:46:23.900 --> 00:46:29.310
reason as opposed to some new, stronger, and
even more dangerous creature that was now

00:46:29.310 --> 00:46:31.240
inside the Ikea.

00:46:31.240 --> 00:46:33.390
Was this some kind of upgrade?

00:46:33.390 --> 00:46:38.860
As they acclimated to the environment, adapted,
gotten better at surviving here, had the Ikea,

00:46:38.860 --> 00:46:42.820
in turn, created deadlier countermeasures
to destroy them all?

00:46:42.820 --> 00:46:45.750
Just when you thought you had a handle on
things…

00:46:45.750 --> 00:46:50.861
But if Cyril was telling the truth, and there
was some kind of creature lurking in the store

00:46:50.861 --> 00:46:56.470
- or potentially more than just one - then
they could be in danger just standing here.

00:46:56.470 --> 00:47:00.740
They gathered up the group, along with any
supplies worth taking, and set off back to

00:47:00.740 --> 00:47:01.980
their community.

00:47:01.980 --> 00:47:06.670
They would need to deliver the bad news so
they could potentially prepare for the worst.

00:47:06.670 --> 00:47:11.380
It was just one of the many communities housed
within SCP-3008.

00:47:11.380 --> 00:47:15.620
Many have theorized over the years that the
Infinite Ikea acts as a kind of nexus point

00:47:15.620 --> 00:47:21.010
for Ikeas all across the multiverse, accounting
for the truly insane number of people who

00:47:21.010 --> 00:47:24.109
have gone missing without a trace into the
building over the years.

00:47:24.109 --> 00:47:30.780
There were children who had been born in the
Ikea, raised in the Ikea, known nothing but

00:47:30.780 --> 00:47:32.400
the Ikea.

00:47:32.400 --> 00:47:36.630
Nobody knew an exact number for sure, but
it was more than possible that the population

00:47:36.630 --> 00:47:39.930
of a small country resided within its walls.

00:47:39.930 --> 00:47:43.510
Sometimes, communities would fracture and
fall apart.

00:47:43.510 --> 00:47:46.690
Occasionally due to a lack of resources or
infighting.

00:47:46.690 --> 00:47:50.990
Other times, due to an overwhelming attack
from the Staff that physically destroyed the

00:47:50.990 --> 00:47:54.450
settlement, often leaving many of its members
dead in the process.

00:47:54.450 --> 00:47:58.200
Those who weren’t picked off while wandering
the store in the following days would likely

00:47:58.200 --> 00:48:02.130
integrate into other nearby communities as
refugees.

00:48:02.130 --> 00:48:05.130
Life was cruel in the Infinite Ikea.

00:48:05.130 --> 00:48:09.410
By the time Alice and her party returned to
their community, it was almost lights out.

00:48:09.410 --> 00:48:14.310
They spoke to the community leaders and informed
them of Joseph’s tragic disappearance, as

00:48:14.310 --> 00:48:17.880
well as this awful new creature that Cyril
claimed had taken him.

00:48:17.880 --> 00:48:21.900
They thought it best not to inform the rest
of the community tonight - It might hurt their

00:48:21.900 --> 00:48:24.530
focus and cause unnecessary panic.

00:48:24.530 --> 00:48:27.420
Alice agreed to join the watch that night.

00:48:27.420 --> 00:48:30.080
She could feel something wrong in the air.

00:48:30.080 --> 00:48:35.700
Even more so than usual, there was something
terrible in this anomalous Ikea…

00:48:35.700 --> 00:48:39.620
Alice stood atop the furniture wall with several
others rangers.

00:48:39.620 --> 00:48:44.450
As the lights flickered off, the distinctive
calls sounded across the store from the activated

00:48:44.450 --> 00:48:52.130
Staff members: “The store is now closed,
please exit the building.”

00:48:52.130 --> 00:48:56.430
It was almost soothing in its familiarity,
compared to the frightening thoughts that

00:48:56.430 --> 00:48:59.250
Cyril had left swimming through her mind.

00:48:59.250 --> 00:49:01.090
The Staff she could handle.

00:49:01.090 --> 00:49:06.060
It was at least the devil she knew, and there
were worse beasts than the devil out there.

00:49:06.060 --> 00:49:08.050
She knew it in her bones.

00:49:08.050 --> 00:49:12.040
They killed a few Staff members that happened
to wander towards the community and begin

00:49:12.040 --> 00:49:15.120
beating their deformed fists against the walls.

00:49:15.120 --> 00:49:16.869
All standard procedure.

00:49:16.869 --> 00:49:21.780
But as Alice’s eyes adjusted to the darkness,
she saw a huge shape in the distance.

00:49:21.780 --> 00:49:24.140
It looked like a mountain that breathed.

00:49:24.140 --> 00:49:31.140
A huge, wobbling mass of flesh, moving impossibly
fast for a “creature” of its size.

00:49:31.140 --> 00:49:35.040
It was so much vaster than the creature that
Cyril had described earlier.

00:49:35.040 --> 00:49:38.430
Was this a different one, or had it simply
gotten bigger?

00:49:38.430 --> 00:49:42.000
Either way, it was coming straight towards
them.

00:49:42.000 --> 00:49:44.810
The community came alive in sudden panic.

00:49:44.810 --> 00:49:48.250
Every able-bodied adult grabbed their weapons
and prepared for the fight of their lives

00:49:48.250 --> 00:49:50.020
behind the wall.

00:49:50.020 --> 00:49:54.691
Whatever this monster was, they needed to
kill it as quickly as possible, before it

00:49:54.691 --> 00:49:57.030
destroyed everything they built.

00:49:57.030 --> 00:50:01.940
Sadly for everyone inside the community, this
was one fight that none of them were prepared

00:50:01.940 --> 00:50:02.940
for.

00:50:02.940 --> 00:50:06.560
The beast crashed into the furniture wall,
crushing it inwards.

00:50:06.560 --> 00:50:10.359
People struck at the monster’s immense and
terrible flesh with their weapons, making

00:50:10.359 --> 00:50:14.820
awful wet slaps, but seeming to cause no lasting
impact.

00:50:14.820 --> 00:50:20.660
It just kept rolling in, grasping everyone
it could with its long, sinewy tendrils.

00:50:20.660 --> 00:50:23.890
But the horrors were only just beginning.

00:50:23.890 --> 00:50:29.160
With the wall destroyed, the Staff began to
pour in, chanting in monstrous unison.

00:50:29.160 --> 00:50:34.300
“The store is now closed, please exit the
building.”

00:50:34.300 --> 00:50:39.830
It was a massacre, perhaps one of the worst
that the inside of the Infinite Ikea had ever

00:50:39.830 --> 00:50:40.830
seen.

00:50:40.830 --> 00:50:45.070
While the community battled the giant flesh
beast, the forces of the Staff swarmed and

00:50:45.070 --> 00:50:46.530
overwhelmed them.

00:50:46.530 --> 00:50:49.490
Darcy was tangled in the nightmarish tentacles
of the beast.

00:50:49.490 --> 00:50:54.550
Bear was surrounded by a truly insurmountable
number of Staff members, each one balling

00:50:54.550 --> 00:50:57.560
their terrible fists and beating him to death.

00:50:57.560 --> 00:51:01.720
Somewhere, Cyril screamed, though his scream
was soon cut short.

00:51:01.720 --> 00:51:05.710
Alice watched in horror as everything she’d
known was destroyed.

00:51:05.710 --> 00:51:10.120
Alice realized that her friends were dead
and her community was in ruins.

00:51:10.120 --> 00:51:14.350
She grabbed her spear and did the only thing
she could: She ran.

00:51:14.350 --> 00:51:19.430
She ran from the lost cause that had once
been her life, tears running down her cheeks.

00:51:19.430 --> 00:51:21.100
What had even happened?

00:51:21.100 --> 00:51:24.030
What the hell had that great monster been?

00:51:24.030 --> 00:51:25.600
The Ikea’s trump card?

00:51:25.600 --> 00:51:30.880
She ran aimlessly for as long as she could,
then walked, and eventually limped.

00:51:30.880 --> 00:51:36.270
She went on for hours, getting lost, deeper
and deeper into unknown territory.

00:51:36.270 --> 00:51:38.470
She’d stop when she was dead.

00:51:38.470 --> 00:51:39.540
Why not?

00:51:39.540 --> 00:51:41.360
She had little left to live for.

00:51:41.360 --> 00:51:46.910
But like the holy grail, when she eventually,
truly gave up, and stopped looking for salvation,

00:51:46.910 --> 00:51:49.160
it found its way to her.

00:51:49.160 --> 00:51:54.380
As she turned a corner around a mysterious
aisle, she saw something that she hadn’t

00:51:54.380 --> 00:51:57.590
seen in years: Glass double-doors.

00:51:57.590 --> 00:51:59.260
She stifled a sob.

00:51:59.260 --> 00:52:00.260
Could it be?

00:52:00.260 --> 00:52:02.460
The way out of here, after all this time?

00:52:02.460 --> 00:52:03.460
And it was.

00:52:03.460 --> 00:52:04.980
The world.

00:52:04.980 --> 00:52:05.980
Escape.

00:52:05.980 --> 00:52:09.010
She’d lost everything, but regained this.

00:52:09.010 --> 00:52:11.350
Outside, dawn was breaking.

00:52:11.350 --> 00:52:14.480
After years, the great, red sun.

00:52:14.480 --> 00:52:18.869
It was the most beautiful thing she’d ever
seen in her life.

00:52:18.869 --> 00:52:23.570
She took a deep breath, smiled, and stepped
into the light…

00:52:23.570 --> 00:52:27.080
It had been one month since the end of the
world.

00:52:27.080 --> 00:52:32.960
30 long days since Stella had watched her
husband dissolve in front of her, one minute

00:52:32.960 --> 00:52:37.860
mowing the lawn like it was any other lazy
afternoon, and the next collapsing into a

00:52:37.860 --> 00:52:41.090
useless pile of flesh and jelly.

00:52:41.090 --> 00:52:45.920
The end didn’t come like she always thought
it would, with hellfire raining down and pale

00:52:45.920 --> 00:52:49.369
riders galloping across the land on horseback.

00:52:49.369 --> 00:52:55.369
It began in the kitchen with her humming a
song to herself, mixing up a pitcher of lemonade

00:52:55.369 --> 00:52:58.190
before she rejoined her family in the sunshine.

00:52:58.190 --> 00:53:03.840
Then they were gone, lost forever when the
sun turned wrong.

00:53:03.840 --> 00:53:09.060
She didn’t even have time to grieve them
properly before the threat turned on her,

00:53:09.060 --> 00:53:15.450
as the creatures that had once been her husband,
her son, and her daughter swarmed the door,

00:53:15.450 --> 00:53:20.470
slamming their new bodies against the wood
with wet slaps, calling out to her in warped

00:53:20.470 --> 00:53:24.330
voices begging her to step back outside.

00:53:24.330 --> 00:53:29.250
She covered her ears and wept for hours until
she had no tears left to cry.

00:53:29.250 --> 00:53:34.350
It didn’t take long for Stella to figure
out that, whatever had happened to her family,

00:53:34.350 --> 00:53:36.740
it was happening all over the world.

00:53:36.740 --> 00:53:43.140
The small town she had known and loved had
transformed into a hellscape, a maze of monsters

00:53:43.140 --> 00:53:44.560
and deadly light.

00:53:44.560 --> 00:53:50.050
She retreated underground, to old mine shafts
and tunnels, left abandoned decades ago when

00:53:50.050 --> 00:53:52.250
the local coal trade had dried up.

00:53:52.250 --> 00:53:56.359
There, she quickly discovered that she wasn’t
the only one with that idea.

00:53:56.359 --> 00:54:01.371
There were others, survivors who had managed
to make their way down into the dark, away

00:54:01.371 --> 00:54:02.760
from the horrors above.

00:54:02.760 --> 00:54:07.369
There weren’t many, and she could never
get too attached to the ones she did meet

00:54:07.369 --> 00:54:13.000
in case they too were lost to the sun, but
as the days wore on in the tunnels she found

00:54:13.000 --> 00:54:16.050
herself forming a small group of stragglers.

00:54:16.050 --> 00:54:21.280
Not quite a family, but something that could
be one day, and definitely much better than

00:54:21.280 --> 00:54:22.790
going it alone.

00:54:22.790 --> 00:54:27.470
There was Stella, of course, using her background
as an elementary school teacher to keep everyone

00:54:27.470 --> 00:54:29.610
on track and working together.

00:54:29.610 --> 00:54:34.650
Then there was Brian, a former volunteer firefighter
whose training and skills with an axe came

00:54:34.650 --> 00:54:36.540
in handy more than once.

00:54:36.540 --> 00:54:40.950
There was Trina, a teenage girl who had been
babysitting when everything went to hell,

00:54:40.950 --> 00:54:45.450
who had a knack for evading the flesh monsters
and making supply runs.

00:54:45.450 --> 00:54:50.680
And then there was Doris, former military
nurse, and current de facto team medic, sharp

00:54:50.680 --> 00:54:54.430
and capable in spite of being in her late
70s.

00:54:54.430 --> 00:54:59.700
They were brave people, brilliant, and refused
to give up.

00:54:59.700 --> 00:55:04.330
Others came and went, spare survivors they
picked up as they navigated their way, raiding

00:55:04.330 --> 00:55:08.980
the ruins of grocery stores and hospitals,
but they always wound up leaving again.

00:55:08.980 --> 00:55:14.260
Or, worse, something would go wrong and the
sunlight would claim another victim.

00:55:14.260 --> 00:55:20.110
But the four of them stayed together, stronger
with their combined skills, willpower, and

00:55:20.110 --> 00:55:23.810
ability to sleep in shifts and keep an eye
out for trouble.

00:55:23.810 --> 00:55:28.910
After some time in the tunnels, the group
had come across a massive abandoned facility.

00:55:28.910 --> 00:55:33.150
None of them could determine where it came
from, or what organization might have run

00:55:33.150 --> 00:55:36.800
it, but it appeared to have been some kind
of research lab.

00:55:36.800 --> 00:55:42.160
It was isolated, cut off from outside influences
better than any of their previous hideouts

00:55:42.160 --> 00:55:43.230
had been.

00:55:43.230 --> 00:55:49.220
Whoever had once worked here had cleared out
- or been taken out - in a hurry, but in their

00:55:49.220 --> 00:55:54.530
place, they had left behind rations, medical
equipment, emergency generators, and clean

00:55:54.530 --> 00:55:55.930
drinking water.

00:55:55.930 --> 00:56:01.160
There were enough supplies to keep the group
going for months, if they rationed carefully.

00:56:01.160 --> 00:56:02.619
And so, they settled in.

00:56:02.619 --> 00:56:07.440
The abandoned research facility, whatever
it once was, became their makeshift home.

00:56:07.440 --> 00:56:11.260
This unexpected sanctuary was cut off from
the rest of the world.

00:56:11.260 --> 00:56:15.840
Clinical, cold, lonely, but a safe place for
them to rest their heads.

00:56:15.840 --> 00:56:19.490
Now, it had been a month since this all began.

00:56:19.490 --> 00:56:23.869
Stella shook her head at this realization,
the passage of time feeling almost meaningless

00:56:23.869 --> 00:56:25.130
at this point.

00:56:25.130 --> 00:56:27.340
What did a month even mean anymore?

00:56:27.340 --> 00:56:31.230
What was a day when she couldn’t even see
the sun rise and set?

00:56:31.230 --> 00:56:36.500
As she sat there, warming herself by the side
of a foraged space heater, she thought about

00:56:36.500 --> 00:56:40.910
missing the stars, the moon, the promise of
the cosmos.

00:56:40.910 --> 00:56:47.720
A time when the vast, open sky was an invitation
to dream, instead of something to hide from.

00:56:47.720 --> 00:56:49.770
That time was gone now.

00:56:49.770 --> 00:56:53.010
There was nothing to do but mourn it and carry
on.

00:56:53.010 --> 00:56:55.110
If not for her, then for the others.

00:56:55.110 --> 00:56:59.010
They had all found ways to keep busy in this
new place.

00:56:59.010 --> 00:57:04.150
Trina took to exploring the empty halls, the
laboratories filled with strange equipment,

00:57:04.150 --> 00:57:08.099
the small rooms that looked like prison cells
or police interrogation rooms.

00:57:08.099 --> 00:57:12.830
She had cooked up some theory about it being
a government facility, like something out

00:57:12.830 --> 00:57:15.150
of an old episode of The X Files.

00:57:15.150 --> 00:57:20.650
Brian had found an on-site library, filled
with history books and scientific texts.

00:57:20.650 --> 00:57:24.800
There was enough to read in there, he said,
that by the time he was finished he would

00:57:24.800 --> 00:57:27.810
have the equivalent of several doctorate degrees.

00:57:27.810 --> 00:57:33.380
Doris had taken initial stock of all of the
medical supplies available at the site, gauze,

00:57:33.380 --> 00:57:38.680
disinfectant, vials upon vials of medications
she had never even heard of, and had categorized

00:57:38.680 --> 00:57:39.980
them all neatly.

00:57:39.980 --> 00:57:44.300
Now, she spent her hours mostly sitting with
Stella, telling her stories about whatever

00:57:44.300 --> 00:57:45.300
she felt like.

00:57:45.300 --> 00:57:51.109
Her time overseas, her late husband, their
children, decades-old neighborhood gossip.

00:57:51.109 --> 00:57:54.090
It felt good to talk, no matter what it was
about.

00:57:54.090 --> 00:57:55.360
It felt human.

00:57:55.360 --> 00:57:58.970
They all had their ways of keeping a grip
on their own humanity.

00:57:58.970 --> 00:58:03.860
It had almost gotten boring, holed up in the
facility without the constant threat of danger

00:58:03.860 --> 00:58:05.960
pressing in from all sides.

00:58:05.960 --> 00:58:07.970
Boring, but peaceful.

00:58:07.970 --> 00:58:12.460
Stella could feel herself starting to nod
off, her eyelids sagging, her dinner of canned

00:58:12.460 --> 00:58:15.060
beans sitting heavy in her stomach.

00:58:15.060 --> 00:58:17.930
Next to her, Doris had already begun to softly
snore.

00:58:17.930 --> 00:58:22.780
Soon, sleep would come for her too, and bring
with it dreams, memories of the life that

00:58:22.780 --> 00:58:26.500
was gone forever, the world as she had once
known it.

00:58:26.500 --> 00:58:30.330
The dreams were tough, but waking up from
them was even tougher.

00:58:30.330 --> 00:58:31.980
Still, she had to rest.

00:58:31.980 --> 00:58:34.060
She could only fight it for so long.

00:58:34.060 --> 00:58:36.860
She could almost hear her husband’s voice,
when…

00:58:36.860 --> 00:58:38.060
“Hey, Stella?

00:58:38.060 --> 00:58:39.870
You might want to see this.”

00:58:39.870 --> 00:58:43.950
Brian was there, suddenly, his words breaking
through the haze in her mind.

00:58:43.950 --> 00:58:48.350
His tone was severe, and she knew that sleep
would have to wait.

00:58:48.350 --> 00:58:52.240
She followed him down the hall for a while,
before he stopped and turned her attention

00:58:52.240 --> 00:58:54.470
to a section of damaged wall.

00:58:54.470 --> 00:59:00.030
It was rusted and corroded, black and grey
with massive cracks in the solid material.

00:59:00.030 --> 00:59:05.180
It looked like it had been slowly disintegrated
over time by an incredibly strong acid.

00:59:05.180 --> 00:59:09.491
It was off-putting to look at, giving off
the distinct sense that something bad had

00:59:09.491 --> 00:59:10.491
happened.

00:59:10.491 --> 00:59:13.180
But a lot of bad things had happened in the
past month.

00:59:13.180 --> 00:59:17.599
“I don’t really understand what I’m
looking at,” Stella said, and Brian grabbed

00:59:17.599 --> 00:59:19.890
her by the shoulders, commanding her attention.

00:59:19.890 --> 00:59:22.450
“This wasn’t here yesterday.

00:59:22.450 --> 00:59:24.599
Hell, it wasn’t here a few hours ago.”

00:59:24.599 --> 00:59:26.680
He gestured to the damage.

00:59:26.680 --> 00:59:27.859
“It just happened.

00:59:27.859 --> 00:59:29.680
And whatever is responsible?

00:59:29.680 --> 00:59:31.450
Is probably still here.”

00:59:31.450 --> 00:59:34.020
A chill ran down Stella’s spine.

00:59:34.020 --> 00:59:37.950
It seemed impossible for this kind of structural
damage to happen over the course of a few

00:59:37.950 --> 00:59:38.950
hours.

00:59:38.950 --> 00:59:44.300
She could see twisted steel rebar, drips of
melted metal that had trickled onto the floor.

00:59:44.300 --> 00:59:46.690
As she leaned in for a closer look, she gagged.

00:59:46.690 --> 00:59:49.421
There was an overpowering stench of rot and
decay.

00:59:49.421 --> 00:59:55.990
It didn’t take long for her to spot the
source: a thick, black mucus covered the hole

00:59:55.990 --> 01:00:01.400
in the wall, slick and slimy and giving off
the putrid smell.

01:00:01.400 --> 01:00:05.580
It wasn’t like the material that made up
the flesh creatures outside, it was something

01:00:05.580 --> 01:00:07.530
else entirely.

01:00:07.530 --> 01:00:09.660
Something she had never seen before.

01:00:09.660 --> 01:00:13.460
She reached out a hand to touch it, but quickly
caught herself.

01:00:13.460 --> 01:00:15.190
Whatever this stuff was, it was bad news.

01:00:15.190 --> 01:00:19.940
If it could do that to a solid wall, she didn’t
even want to think about what it could do

01:00:19.940 --> 01:00:21.500
to flesh and bone.

01:00:21.500 --> 01:00:22.599
“What do we do?”

01:00:22.599 --> 01:00:23.599
She asked.

01:00:23.599 --> 01:00:25.359
“Inspect the area, make sure it’s safe.

01:00:25.359 --> 01:00:27.910
See if it’s some kind of toxic waste leak,
or…”

01:00:27.910 --> 01:00:28.960
He didn’t finish the thought.

01:00:28.960 --> 01:00:31.080
He didn’t have to.

01:00:31.080 --> 01:00:35.300
They needed to find out if this damage came
from something living, something that could

01:00:35.300 --> 01:00:40.270
be hiding in the building with them and waiting
for the chance to strike.

01:00:40.270 --> 01:00:44.280
When they returned to the main room, Doris
was awake and Trina was there waiting for

01:00:44.280 --> 01:00:45.280
them.

01:00:45.280 --> 01:00:50.570
They agreed to split into pairs and search
the facility, looking for any additional damage,

01:00:50.570 --> 01:00:54.580
strange black fluid, or signs of foul play
of any kind.

01:00:54.580 --> 01:00:59.230
Trina and Brian went one way, while Doris
and Stella went the other, each person armed

01:00:59.230 --> 01:01:02.540
with a flashlight and a weapon of some kind.

01:01:02.540 --> 01:01:04.690
An axe, a bat, a knife.

01:01:04.690 --> 01:01:08.869
Stella had found a loaded pistol when they
first arrived, one that she hoped she would

01:01:08.869 --> 01:01:10.320
never have to use.

01:01:10.320 --> 01:01:12.530
Now, she carried it with her.

01:01:12.530 --> 01:01:13.740
Just in case.

01:01:13.740 --> 01:01:17.880
Stella and Doris walked together for an hour,
keeping an eye out for anything suspicious.

01:01:17.880 --> 01:01:20.360
They didn’t find anything notable, really.

01:01:20.360 --> 01:01:24.990
There were some bits of rust they hadn’t
seen before, an occasional black puddle pooling

01:01:24.990 --> 01:01:29.099
on the floor, but nothing like the scene Brian
had found.

01:01:29.099 --> 01:01:33.650
As they walked in silence, Stella felt herself
begin to relax.

01:01:33.650 --> 01:01:38.170
Maybe the worst was over, maybe a pipe had
broken somewhere and broken down a bit of

01:01:38.170 --> 01:01:41.099
the wall, but nothing else would really come
of it.

01:01:41.099 --> 01:01:45.890
Then, from the other side of the building,
there came a high-pitched scream.

01:01:45.890 --> 01:01:47.880
She knew that voice immediately.

01:01:47.880 --> 01:01:52.290
Trina, screaming bloody murder, and Brian
bellowing something.

01:01:52.290 --> 01:01:56.089
She grabbed Doris by the arm, and the two
ran in the direction of the sound.

01:01:56.089 --> 01:02:00.400
As they got closer, they could hear other
sounds in addition to Trina and Brian’s

01:02:00.400 --> 01:02:01.400
yells.

01:02:01.400 --> 01:02:06.580
Loud cracking like something solid coming
apart, bits of rubble falling to the ground,

01:02:06.580 --> 01:02:10.690
and the wet dripping sound of something thick
hitting the floor.

01:02:10.690 --> 01:02:14.920
As the two rounded the corner to see what
was happening, to help their friends, Stella

01:02:14.920 --> 01:02:16.150
froze in shock.

01:02:16.150 --> 01:02:20.330
She had seen some harrowing things since that
fateful sunny day.

01:02:20.330 --> 01:02:24.339
She almost thought she had become desensitized
to horror, that there was nothing left in

01:02:24.339 --> 01:02:26.090
the world that could phase her.

01:02:26.090 --> 01:02:28.610
But this moment proved her wrong.

01:02:28.610 --> 01:02:31.700
There was so much to take in, and so little
time.

01:02:31.700 --> 01:02:36.610
Brian was swinging his axe wildly at the floor,
chopping at it helplessly in an attempt to

01:02:36.610 --> 01:02:37.680
break it apart.

01:02:37.680 --> 01:02:39.980
It quickly became obvious why.

01:02:39.980 --> 01:02:45.470
Trina was sinking into the floor, not through
a hole into the basement below but into the

01:02:45.470 --> 01:02:46.470
floor itself.

01:02:46.470 --> 01:02:52.380
It rippled and melted around her, changing
from a solid surface to a thick pool of black

01:02:52.380 --> 01:02:53.609
sludge.

01:02:53.609 --> 01:02:58.641
She flailed and struggled, grasping at the
ground around her, at Brian’s arm, anything

01:02:58.641 --> 01:03:00.410
to yank herself free.

01:03:00.410 --> 01:03:05.099
But she wouldn’t budge, she continued to
sink.

01:03:05.099 --> 01:03:06.119
No…she wasn’t just sinking.

01:03:06.119 --> 01:03:08.510
She was being pulled.

01:03:08.510 --> 01:03:12.940
Stella felt her legs begin to work again,
and she rushed to Trina’s side, grabbing

01:03:12.940 --> 01:03:15.720
hold of her arms with all the strength she
had.

01:03:15.720 --> 01:03:20.680
All the while, Trina screamed in a mix of
desperation, terror, and pain.

01:03:20.680 --> 01:03:25.270
Brian continued his assault on the ground,
as if he could cut her free from the floor,

01:03:25.270 --> 01:03:27.369
and Doris moved to help Stella.

01:03:27.369 --> 01:03:29.920
She grabbed ahold of Trina’s waist but stopped
suddenly.

01:03:29.920 --> 01:03:34.400
There, at the place where her body vanished
into the floor, Trina was beginning to fall

01:03:34.400 --> 01:03:35.700
apart.

01:03:35.700 --> 01:03:40.290
Whatever had turned the floor into a portal,
whatever had rotted away at the walls, it

01:03:40.290 --> 01:03:42.880
was taking its toll on her body too.

01:03:42.880 --> 01:03:48.440
The flesh falling away from the bone, the
muscles liquifying, and everything being corrupted

01:03:48.440 --> 01:03:50.750
by that same black mucus.

01:03:50.750 --> 01:03:55.650
If they pulled any harder, Trina would be
ripped in two, her chest, arms, and head on

01:03:55.650 --> 01:03:59.660
the surface while her legs, stomach, and most
of her vital organs were lost.

01:03:59.660 --> 01:04:02.150
There was no way to save her.

01:04:02.150 --> 01:04:06.430
Stella saw it when Doris did, and the two
shared a long, sad look.

01:04:06.430 --> 01:04:11.590
Trina was here with them, still struggling
for her life, but she was already gone.

01:04:11.590 --> 01:04:15.700
As if it felt their hopelessness, the force
pulling on Trina from below then gave the

01:04:15.700 --> 01:04:20.200
horrifying yank they were praying to not have
to witness, and she was swallowed up by the

01:04:20.200 --> 01:04:21.200
ground.

01:04:21.200 --> 01:04:27.201
All that remained was a sticky black smear,
a large crack in the floor, and three survivors

01:04:27.201 --> 01:04:29.640
where there had just been four.

01:04:29.640 --> 01:04:32.630
None of the remaining three slept a wink that
night.

01:04:32.630 --> 01:04:36.020
Brian paced back and forth, holding his axe.

01:04:36.020 --> 01:04:39.890
Doris went over their supplies again, looking
for anything that could be used to treat the

01:04:39.890 --> 01:04:42.140
effects of the corrosive black fluid.

01:04:42.140 --> 01:04:45.500
And Stella ran over their potential options
again and again.

01:04:45.500 --> 01:04:49.599
They could stay, and hope that the thing that
took Trina never came back.

01:04:49.599 --> 01:04:53.910
Or they could leave and face the possibility
of succumbing to the sun or the influence

01:04:53.910 --> 01:04:56.089
of the creatures that once were people.

01:04:56.089 --> 01:05:00.680
She recalled dimly an old adage: The devil
you know beats the devil you don’t.

01:05:00.680 --> 01:05:05.930
Maybe that was true, maybe it would be better
to take their chances with the outside rather

01:05:05.930 --> 01:05:07.910
than stay and be hunted.

01:05:07.910 --> 01:05:12.609
But she thought of her husband and children
again, watching them melt away onto the grass,

01:05:12.609 --> 01:05:13.609
and she shuddered.

01:05:13.609 --> 01:05:17.740
There were no good options here, but she couldn’t
risk going out like that.

01:05:17.740 --> 01:05:18.970
They would stay.

01:05:18.970 --> 01:05:23.300
Whatever had taken Trina, there were three
of them, and only one of it.

01:05:23.300 --> 01:05:28.119
Besides, maybe it had already gotten what
it wanted, and now it would move on.

01:05:28.119 --> 01:05:32.480
She didn’t know when she fell asleep, only
that when she eventually woke up, she was

01:05:32.480 --> 01:05:33.580
alone.

01:05:33.580 --> 01:05:36.270
Brian and Doris were nowhere to be found.

01:05:36.270 --> 01:05:39.580
She called out their names as her heart leaped
into her throat.

01:05:39.580 --> 01:05:41.940
For a moment, she feared the very worst.

01:05:41.940 --> 01:05:44.100
But then, Brian answered her.

01:05:44.100 --> 01:05:48.390
He had been patrolling, looking for signs
of trouble, but he was fine.

01:05:48.390 --> 01:05:51.540
She asked if Doris was with him, and he went
pale.

01:05:51.540 --> 01:05:56.040
He told Stella that Doris had gone to an on-site
bathroom to get cleaned up hours ago, before

01:05:56.040 --> 01:05:57.040
he left.

01:05:57.040 --> 01:05:58.820
She should have been back by then.

01:05:58.820 --> 01:06:03.970
Without another thought, they took off toward
the bathroom and saw their worst fear realized.

01:06:03.970 --> 01:06:09.570
The door to the room was gone, rotted away,
and Doris was gone with it.

01:06:09.570 --> 01:06:13.000
She had been taken before she even had the
chance to scream.

01:06:13.000 --> 01:06:17.770
They stood there, taking in the reality of
what had happened with silent shock.

01:06:17.770 --> 01:06:21.400
Then, a sound from behind caused them to turn
around.

01:06:21.400 --> 01:06:27.130
It was a raspy, hollowed-out sound, like air
forced from a dying man’s lungs.

01:06:27.130 --> 01:06:32.500
A dry, empty, evil chuckle from an inhuman
throat.

01:06:32.500 --> 01:06:36.410
There, they saw the creature that had stolen
their friends.

01:06:36.410 --> 01:06:43.470
From a distance, Stella could have confused
it for a man, a very old, very sick man, but

01:06:43.470 --> 01:06:44.730
still human.

01:06:44.730 --> 01:06:48.260
As it shuffled closer, however, that illusion
was shattered.

01:06:48.260 --> 01:06:54.740
Its eyes were black voids, its skin grey and
putrefied, its mouth a gaping toothless maw

01:06:54.740 --> 01:06:57.240
of shriveled black gums.

01:06:57.240 --> 01:06:59.800
And it was laughing at them.

01:06:59.800 --> 01:07:01.730
Like it was enjoying itself.

01:07:01.730 --> 01:07:07.350
There was the smell again, that overpowering
reek of all things foul.

01:07:07.350 --> 01:07:09.890
Stella drew her pistol and fired several shots.

01:07:09.890 --> 01:07:14.150
She didn’t have the best aim, especially
not when her hands were shaking with fear,

01:07:14.150 --> 01:07:18.650
but one of the bullets found its target and
hit the creature in the cheek.

01:07:18.650 --> 01:07:25.230
It hissed, not in pain, but a perverse amusement,
as the lead tore a hole through its flesh

01:07:25.230 --> 01:07:26.770
like wet paper.

01:07:26.770 --> 01:07:31.350
Globs of black blood spattered everywhere,
spreading the rot wherever they landed.

01:07:31.350 --> 01:07:34.060
To her side, she heard Brian cry out in pain.

01:07:34.060 --> 01:07:36.850
When she turned, he was clutching his right
eye.

01:07:36.850 --> 01:07:40.410
Some of the liquid had hit him there, and
it was already taking effect.

01:07:40.410 --> 01:07:45.300
Stella’s stomach turned as she saw something
white slide down the side of Brian’s face,

01:07:45.300 --> 01:07:47.410
his eye melting out of its socket.

01:07:47.410 --> 01:07:52.230
The skin around it followed suit, softening
like putty and dribbling away from the bloody

01:07:52.230 --> 01:07:53.869
muscle underneath.

01:07:53.869 --> 01:07:59.510
Brian collapsed to the floor, as the creature
continued to advance on them, laughing louder

01:07:59.510 --> 01:08:00.510
and louder.

01:08:00.510 --> 01:08:02.740
It knew that bullets couldn’t kill it.

01:08:02.740 --> 01:08:04.849
Knew that there was nowhere to run.

01:08:04.849 --> 01:08:09.300
Stella grabbed ahold of Brian’s arms, prepared
to carry her fallen friend to safety, but

01:08:09.300 --> 01:08:10.300
he stopped her.

01:08:10.300 --> 01:08:12.220
“I’m done for,” he said, quietly.

01:08:12.220 --> 01:08:15.349
“If you try to carry me, you are too.”

01:08:15.349 --> 01:08:19.779
She shook her head, unwilling to leave him
behind, and he touched her cheek, forcing

01:08:19.779 --> 01:08:22.310
a smile on his decaying face.

01:08:22.310 --> 01:08:24.859
“I didn’t think I’d make it this far.

01:08:24.859 --> 01:08:27.089
I wouldn’t have without you.

01:08:27.089 --> 01:08:29.099
So run, save yourself.”

01:08:29.099 --> 01:08:33.690
She could hear the resignation in his voice
and knew that he would not let her save him.

01:08:33.690 --> 01:08:39.219
“I’ll hold him off as long as I can,”
Brian promised, and, with tears in her eyes,

01:08:39.219 --> 01:08:41.779
Stella turned and ran as fast as she could.

01:08:41.779 --> 01:08:46.409
As her feet pounded the ground, blood rushing
in her ears, she could hear Brian screaming

01:08:46.409 --> 01:08:53.409
and cursing at the creature until his voice
quickly faded to a wet gurgle, and then, silence.

01:08:53.409 --> 01:08:57.630
Stella sat in the center of the room that
she and the others had once shared, clutching

01:08:57.630 --> 01:09:00.369
her scavenged pistol in her hands.

01:09:00.369 --> 01:09:03.509
Every creak, every errant sound made her jump.

01:09:03.509 --> 01:09:04.509
It could be anywhere.

01:09:04.509 --> 01:09:05.509
Come from anywhere.

01:09:05.509 --> 01:09:10.459
She had escaped from so much already, survived
more than anyone was ever meant to.

01:09:10.459 --> 01:09:16.339
Had lost her love, her friends, her home…she
wouldn’t lose herself too.

01:09:16.339 --> 01:09:20.619
She held her breath as she heard it, clear
in the darkness: Drip.

01:09:20.619 --> 01:09:21.619
Drip.

01:09:21.619 --> 01:09:22.619
Drip.

01:09:22.619 --> 01:09:25.549
Her grip on the weapon tightened, and she
readied herself.

01:09:25.549 --> 01:09:29.099
If she had to go down, she might as well go
down swinging.

01:09:29.099 --> 01:09:35.259
The Old Man, whether he was a man, a monster,
or death itself, wouldn’t be taking her

01:09:35.259 --> 01:09:38.529
anywhere…not without a fight.

01:09:38.529 --> 01:09:44.290
We’d been so cautious, keeping quiet, covering
up, only moving at night, but sometimes, despite

01:09:44.290 --> 01:09:47.230
all your best efforts, you just get unlucky.

01:09:47.230 --> 01:09:52.089
We made our way through what we thought was
an abandoned street, littered with broken-down

01:09:52.089 --> 01:09:53.089
cars, when those...

01:09:53.089 --> 01:09:55.959
Things had slithered out and ambushed us.

01:09:55.959 --> 01:09:59.920
Kaspar, who was always the most trigger happy
of us, panicked and opened fire with his scavenged

01:09:59.920 --> 01:10:02.150
submachine gun on the largest of the creatures.

01:10:02.150 --> 01:10:03.150
Big mistake.

01:10:03.150 --> 01:10:07.539
It didn’t slow the monster down, and only
seemed to attract more.

01:10:07.539 --> 01:10:10.290
It’d be blob city before sunrise now.

01:10:10.290 --> 01:10:13.349
But poor Kaspar wouldn’t make it that long.

01:10:13.349 --> 01:10:17.929
Fleshy tendrils wrapped around his limbs and
pulled him into the terrible mass of one of

01:10:17.929 --> 01:10:18.929
the creatures.

01:10:18.929 --> 01:10:24.840
It started reshaping around him, enveloping
him as he screamed, until the tide of flesh

01:10:24.840 --> 01:10:27.360
coursing down his throat silenced him.

01:10:27.360 --> 01:10:32.099
The whole time, the monster just kept gibbering
madness in a warped voice.

01:10:32.099 --> 01:10:42.270
“Become one, yes, let our cells bond and
our minds coagulate in the glorious shimmering

01:10:42.270 --> 01:10:43.270
light.

01:10:43.270 --> 01:10:51.090
We will all be one in daylight, pretty flower,
yes, yes, yes...”

01:10:51.090 --> 01:10:53.190
And then there were four.

01:10:53.190 --> 01:10:55.239
Me, Ellen, Darcy, and Jones.

01:10:55.239 --> 01:11:00.219
There had been more of us once, but in this
war of attrition, we always seemed to be the

01:11:00.219 --> 01:11:01.489
ones who had to lose.

01:11:01.489 --> 01:11:05.300
And if we didn’t get away from these monsters
quickly, then there wouldn’t be any of us

01:11:05.300 --> 01:11:06.749
left soon enough.

01:11:06.749 --> 01:11:10.869
We ran, ducking and weaving around the cars
as the blobs got closer.

01:11:10.869 --> 01:11:16.100
There were so many of them, all howling and
ranting, zealots for the only cause their

01:11:16.100 --> 01:11:19.449
melted excuses for minds could understand
anymore.

01:11:19.449 --> 01:11:24.190
Occasionally, we fired back, hoping to slow
them down a little - But it never worked.

01:11:24.190 --> 01:11:28.000
The guns, if anything, were a psychological
support mechanism, and something to scare

01:11:28.000 --> 01:11:30.769
off other survivors who were even more desperate
than us.

01:11:30.769 --> 01:11:35.670
When it came to the blobs, especially the
big ones, the only things you could do were

01:11:35.670 --> 01:11:37.170
run and hope.

01:11:37.170 --> 01:11:39.280
And hope’s in short supply these days.

01:11:39.280 --> 01:11:44.409
No matter how fast we ran, the blobs kept
catching up, and they didn’t seem to get

01:11:44.409 --> 01:11:45.409
tired.

01:11:45.409 --> 01:11:48.750
Knowing we couldn’t outrun them, we ran
up to one of the abandoned cars and crouched

01:11:48.750 --> 01:11:50.210
behind them, breathing raggedly.

01:11:50.210 --> 01:11:56.050
This whole stupid mission, in hopes of eventually
reaching safety, a good night’s sleep behind

01:11:56.050 --> 01:11:59.620
solid walls, and some people who actually
know what the Hell is going on.

01:11:59.620 --> 01:12:02.159
I drew my sidearm out of my pocket.

01:12:02.159 --> 01:12:05.630
Even if I didn’t make it out of here, I
wouldn’t turn into one of those monsters.

01:12:05.630 --> 01:12:08.300
Pulling back the hammer, I muttered a quiet
prayer.

01:12:08.300 --> 01:12:12.630
If there’s a God out there, he had a hell
of a lot to answer for right now.

01:12:12.630 --> 01:12:17.269
I looked to Ellen, Darcy, and Jones, and they
all had the same idea.

01:12:17.269 --> 01:12:20.260
It was a pleasure to know them all, after
everything that’d happened.

01:12:20.260 --> 01:12:25.320
We heard the blobs getting closer, their lunatic
whispers growing in volume and intensity,

01:12:25.320 --> 01:12:28.219
as they always did before they claimed one
of us.

01:12:28.219 --> 01:12:32.880
I breathed a sigh, ready to accept my fate,
knowing what I needed to do.

01:12:32.880 --> 01:12:34.880
It all seemed to be over.

01:12:34.880 --> 01:12:36.199
Until He appeared.

01:12:36.199 --> 01:12:39.360
I looked up and spotted a strange man approaching.

01:12:39.360 --> 01:12:44.620
He was dressed in an old-fashioned hessian
toga, His hair and beard frosty white.

01:12:44.620 --> 01:12:49.449
I couldn’t identify His age or race, but
those factors seemed secondary to the fact

01:12:49.449 --> 01:12:53.519
His eyes were glowing brilliant white, as
if exuding pure energy.

01:12:53.519 --> 01:12:58.949
He raised a hand, the same glowing aura of
pure white energy emanating off of it.

01:12:58.949 --> 01:13:03.000
I’d seen so many things that terrified me
in the past six months.

01:13:03.000 --> 01:13:04.000
But this...

01:13:04.000 --> 01:13:07.280
This was the first time I’d experienced
something I could call “Awe.”

01:13:07.280 --> 01:13:13.010
He walked towards us with a stillness and
confidence and said “You look like you could

01:13:13.010 --> 01:13:14.360
use a hand.”

01:13:14.360 --> 01:13:16.180
Where were you when day broke?

01:13:16.180 --> 01:13:18.079
Whoever’s left, they remember.

01:13:18.079 --> 01:13:22.389
I was in a supermarket with my wife and two
kids, just another normal day.

01:13:22.389 --> 01:13:26.909
I was worrying about bills, taxes, and whatever
we were going to have for dinner that night.

01:13:26.909 --> 01:13:32.019
If the Devil exists, I’d sell my soul to
the nasty red creep to get those worries back.

01:13:32.019 --> 01:13:36.610
Lucky for us, we were in the back half of
the store, lit entirely by artificial light.

01:13:36.610 --> 01:13:40.030
The people checking out their groceries down
front, right in front of the building’s

01:13:40.030 --> 01:13:43.090
huge, glass facade, they were the first to
go down.

01:13:43.090 --> 01:13:44.960
All these terrible drowned screams.

01:13:44.960 --> 01:13:48.720
First, the exact kind of shrieks you’d expect
from somebody starting to melt.

01:13:48.720 --> 01:13:54.280
Then, the gurgling, like a backed-up drain,
as their mouth and throat melted around the

01:13:54.280 --> 01:13:56.010
sound, choking it.

01:13:56.010 --> 01:13:59.260
Those were the first minutes of Hell on Earth.

01:13:59.260 --> 01:14:01.300
I’ve never been the smartest man.

01:14:01.300 --> 01:14:03.530
I was a roofer, back when things made sense.

01:14:03.530 --> 01:14:08.280
Never went to college, never been much for
reading, but I had intuition, and I credit

01:14:08.280 --> 01:14:09.739
that with surviving this long.

01:14:09.739 --> 01:14:11.599
My dad was in the service.

01:14:11.599 --> 01:14:13.110
Two tours in ‘Nam.

01:14:13.110 --> 01:14:16.780
He always told me when I was a kid, the guys
who didn’t make it back, they panicked.

01:14:16.780 --> 01:14:19.170
Because that’s what the enemy wants you
to do.

01:14:19.170 --> 01:14:23.989
They hope, when everything hits the fan, you’ll
break formation, forget the plan, and all

01:14:23.989 --> 01:14:27.730
start scurrying in different directions like
rats, just hoping you’ll be the one who

01:14:27.730 --> 01:14:29.440
finds a crack and gets out.

01:14:29.440 --> 01:14:31.039
That’s when they get you.

01:14:31.039 --> 01:14:37.219
When you panic, you’re giving up the birth
rite of reason and letting the animal take

01:14:37.219 --> 01:14:38.219
over.

01:14:38.219 --> 01:14:39.219
“And animals, son, are easier to kill.”

01:14:39.219 --> 01:14:43.059
When the sun went bad that day, plenty of
people in that store started running.

01:14:43.059 --> 01:14:45.610
They didn’t know what they were running
from, or where they were running to.

01:14:45.610 --> 01:14:50.170
They just wanted to put some distance between
themselves and the screaming.

01:14:50.170 --> 01:14:51.199
Every man for himself.

01:14:51.199 --> 01:14:56.219
I’d love to tell you all those people are
dead now, but in actuality, they were in for

01:14:56.219 --> 01:14:57.639
a far worse fate.

01:14:57.639 --> 01:15:01.989
They ran out of the safety and cover of the
store, putting themselves at the mercy of

01:15:01.989 --> 01:15:03.039
the light.

01:15:03.039 --> 01:15:05.570
Even as they melted, they kept running.

01:15:05.570 --> 01:15:09.940
They were blobs, slithering away, not even
knowing that the thing they were running from

01:15:09.940 --> 01:15:11.769
had already gotten them.

01:15:11.769 --> 01:15:15.929
It all happened so fast, but I can remember
every single detail.

01:15:15.929 --> 01:15:16.960
You can judge me if you like.

01:15:16.960 --> 01:15:18.039
I don’t care anymore.

01:15:18.039 --> 01:15:22.510
If I could have spoken reason to those terrified
people that day, hand on my heart, I would

01:15:22.510 --> 01:15:23.510
have.

01:15:23.510 --> 01:15:27.829
But one of man’s greatest design flaws is
that God made fear the master of reason.

01:15:27.829 --> 01:15:30.630
Nothing I could’ve said that day would’ve
changed anything.

01:15:30.630 --> 01:15:34.750
The only ones I could hope to save were my
family, so that’s exactly what I did.

01:15:34.750 --> 01:15:39.199
We grabbed what we could, stayed away from
windows, and made our way to the nearest supply

01:15:39.199 --> 01:15:40.199
closet.

01:15:40.199 --> 01:15:43.590
We were in there for hours, and we didn’t
come out until things were actually quiet.

01:15:43.590 --> 01:15:46.070
Which, coincidentally, happened to be at night.

01:15:46.070 --> 01:15:50.550
When we stepped outside of the store, we saw
the carnage that must’ve unfolded over the

01:15:50.550 --> 01:15:51.909
past few hours.

01:15:51.909 --> 01:15:52.989
Windows smashed.

01:15:52.989 --> 01:15:55.860
Cars driven into storefronts and abandoned.

01:15:55.860 --> 01:15:58.540
Slithering blobs of former-people on the ground.

01:15:58.540 --> 01:16:03.479
In the space of a few hours, the world had
truly and irrevocably changed.

01:16:03.479 --> 01:16:08.260
No matter what happened, even if we survived,
we knew there would never be a “normal”

01:16:08.260 --> 01:16:10.360
as we’d known it before ever again.

01:16:10.360 --> 01:16:13.659
The same message was playing on every TV,
radio, and computer.

01:16:13.659 --> 01:16:19.639
A logo I didn’t quite recognize, along with
an overlay that read, “An Important Message

01:16:19.639 --> 01:16:21.820
from the SCP Foundation.”

01:16:21.820 --> 01:16:27.179
It explained, in an eternally looping, robotic
voice, that they were superseding the control

01:16:27.179 --> 01:16:31.539
of all world governments to protect humanity
from this new and terrifying threat.

01:16:31.539 --> 01:16:34.380
In short, the sun had turned against us.

01:16:34.380 --> 01:16:37.570
In some scenarios, survival is a curse.

01:16:37.570 --> 01:16:41.789
In those early days and weeks, you wouldn’t
believe the number of times I started to envy

01:16:41.789 --> 01:16:42.789
the dead.

01:16:42.789 --> 01:16:46.320
People who’d been wiped off the mortal coil
by diseases and car crashes and random acts

01:16:46.320 --> 01:16:51.400
of violence in the days before Day Broke,
they had no idea how good they had it.

01:16:51.400 --> 01:16:56.340
Little by little, in this terrible new world,
death became a kind of luxury.

01:16:56.340 --> 01:16:59.300
Because melting under the fiery gaze of the
sun?

01:16:59.300 --> 01:17:00.489
That wasn’t death.

01:17:00.489 --> 01:17:01.699
Not even close.

01:17:01.699 --> 01:17:03.230
They seemed harmless at first.

01:17:03.230 --> 01:17:04.929
Tragic, pitiful, really.

01:17:04.929 --> 01:17:09.380
The voices on the TV even told us that, to
avoid starvation, we could eat small parts

01:17:09.380 --> 01:17:10.380
of the melted.

01:17:10.380 --> 01:17:12.469
But, over time, the situation evolved.

01:17:12.469 --> 01:17:17.869
I don’t know when it started, or why, but
the blobs that had once been people started

01:17:17.869 --> 01:17:18.869
coagulating.

01:17:18.869 --> 01:17:24.070
They joined up, started turning into bigger
creatures, all with one mind, always screaming

01:17:24.070 --> 01:17:27.889
and talking madness in a collage of stolen
voices.

01:17:27.889 --> 01:17:33.130
These monsters existed for one purpose, and
one purpose only: Finding the people who were

01:17:33.130 --> 01:17:37.179
still normal and dragging them out into the
light to join them.

01:17:37.179 --> 01:17:40.880
They roamed the world during the night, hunting,
seeking.

01:17:40.880 --> 01:17:45.610
And given night was the only time we could
ever safely move, this created problems for

01:17:45.610 --> 01:17:46.610
all of us.

01:17:46.610 --> 01:17:49.500
We lost so many to those terrible monsters.

01:17:49.500 --> 01:17:50.960
Including my wife and both my kids.

01:17:50.960 --> 01:17:54.619
See what I mean about survival sometimes being
a curse?

01:17:54.619 --> 01:17:55.790
Especially when you’ve got people to miss.

01:17:55.790 --> 01:18:00.690
I wish they were dead, all three of them,
but I still hear them - their voices added

01:18:00.690 --> 01:18:03.749
to the chorus of a house-sized flesh monster.

01:18:03.749 --> 01:18:06.429
But I lived on, if you can call this living.

01:18:06.429 --> 01:18:10.239
I met with others, like Kaspar, Ellen, Darcy,
and Jones.

01:18:10.239 --> 01:18:12.130
All of them had lost people.

01:18:12.130 --> 01:18:14.780
You hadn’t lived this long if you hadn’t
lost people.

01:18:14.780 --> 01:18:17.699
We did all we could to keep surviving against
the odds.

01:18:17.699 --> 01:18:23.050
We spent our days in basements and abandoned
stores, and our nights dodging the flesh creatures

01:18:23.050 --> 01:18:24.670
and foraging for food.

01:18:24.670 --> 01:18:28.050
We eked out each day, taking every breath
as it came.

01:18:28.050 --> 01:18:30.040
We didn’t hope for anything.

01:18:30.040 --> 01:18:33.900
Until one day, we heard that the people at
the SCP Foundation might know how to reverse

01:18:33.900 --> 01:18:34.900
all this.

01:18:34.900 --> 01:18:38.739
They needed all the people and help they could
get at one of their bases, around twenty miles

01:18:38.739 --> 01:18:39.739
away from us.

01:18:39.739 --> 01:18:43.219
It’d only be a couple nights walk to get
there, we figured, so why not help out the

01:18:43.219 --> 01:18:44.219
cause?

01:18:44.219 --> 01:18:48.510
Safety in numbers, after all - And, truth
be told, we’d missed people.

01:18:48.510 --> 01:18:53.090
After days of hiding and nights of traveling,
we were ambushed, but you already know about

01:18:53.090 --> 01:18:54.090
that part.

01:18:54.090 --> 01:18:58.150
We lost Kaspar to one of those monsters and
got cornered behind an abandoned car.

01:18:58.150 --> 01:19:02.619
However, as the blobs got closer and closer,
that mysterious stranger with the glowing

01:19:02.619 --> 01:19:03.619
eyes appeared.

01:19:03.619 --> 01:19:05.949
He didn’t walk like the rest of us.

01:19:05.949 --> 01:19:10.019
He was bold, confident, His back straight
as a dancer’s.

01:19:10.019 --> 01:19:11.270
Like He feared nothing.

01:19:11.270 --> 01:19:13.260
Like nothing here could hurt Him.

01:19:13.260 --> 01:19:15.610
Suddenly, I felt a rumbling behind me.

01:19:15.610 --> 01:19:17.199
The car was rattling.

01:19:17.199 --> 01:19:21.499
I feared for a second that the blobs were
crawling over it to get us, but when I turned,

01:19:21.499 --> 01:19:22.969
I saw the opposite was happening.

01:19:22.969 --> 01:19:26.820
The car was rising off of the ground, free-floating.

01:19:26.820 --> 01:19:31.500
Myself and the other survivors stared in astonishment
as the stranger simply flicked His wrist.

01:19:31.500 --> 01:19:36.730
The floating car was thrown with tremendous
force at the largest of the advancing blobs.

01:19:36.730 --> 01:19:41.159
It hit the beast so hard that the top of it
simply splattered off, freezing what was left

01:19:41.159 --> 01:19:42.340
in place.

01:19:42.340 --> 01:19:44.780
Even the other blobs paused, seemingly astonished.

01:19:44.780 --> 01:19:48.269
We all turned to the stranger, still standing
firm.

01:19:48.269 --> 01:19:52.370
“Stand behind me if you wish to live,”
He said.

01:19:52.370 --> 01:19:56.059
There was something both passionate and commanding
about His voice.

01:19:56.059 --> 01:19:59.449
It was impossible to hear Him and not heed
His words.

01:19:59.449 --> 01:20:04.120
All of us stood up and ran behind the stranger,
as He lifted both hands.

01:20:04.120 --> 01:20:05.909
Debris climbed into the air.

01:20:05.909 --> 01:20:08.239
Cars, rocks, broken glass.

01:20:08.239 --> 01:20:11.949
The blobs were beginning to coagulate again,
but this time, the stranger wasn’t going

01:20:11.949 --> 01:20:13.880
to let them get the upper hand again.

01:20:13.880 --> 01:20:18.309
With a slight nod, everything He’d raised
flew at the blobs with the force of a machine

01:20:18.309 --> 01:20:19.309
gun.

01:20:19.309 --> 01:20:20.929
They were decimated.

01:20:20.929 --> 01:20:22.489
The bigger ones, cut apart.

01:20:22.489 --> 01:20:25.260
The smaller one fleeing to avoid the onslaught.

01:20:25.260 --> 01:20:29.039
After months of running from these monsters,
I don’t think any sight could have been

01:20:29.039 --> 01:20:30.039
more satisfying.

01:20:30.039 --> 01:20:32.670
When the creatures were gone, the stranger
just gave a quiet sigh.

01:20:32.670 --> 01:20:35.090
He turned to us and asked if we were okay.

01:20:35.090 --> 01:20:38.530
The answer was, essentially, “About as much
as we can be.”

01:20:38.530 --> 01:20:43.389
He told us that we’d be safe as long as
we stuck with Him, and after that display,

01:20:43.389 --> 01:20:46.760
we had every reason to believe Him.

01:20:46.760 --> 01:20:49.480
We followed the stranger down the long, dark
road.

01:20:49.480 --> 01:20:53.309
He always walked in the middle, fearless,
making no attempt to hide.

01:20:53.309 --> 01:20:58.090
When we asked Him His name, He told us that
He goes by many names, but for the sake of

01:20:58.090 --> 01:21:00.269
simplicity, we could call Him Matthew.

01:21:00.269 --> 01:21:05.679
We thanked Matthew for saving us, and He gave
a sad sort of smile, and told us that He only

01:21:05.679 --> 01:21:07.340
wished He could have done more.

01:21:07.340 --> 01:21:12.760
There was something so strange about Matthew,
even beyond the fact He apparently had superpowers.

01:21:12.760 --> 01:21:17.269
I’d never met the man before and I’d be
willing to bet my right hand on that, and

01:21:17.269 --> 01:21:20.170
yet, it felt like I’d known Him my entire
life.

01:21:20.170 --> 01:21:23.959
He was so different, and yet, so incredibly
familiar.

01:21:23.959 --> 01:21:29.110
We told Him we were on our way to a nearby
SCP Foundation facility, where we were told

01:21:29.110 --> 01:21:30.610
we’d find safe haven.

01:21:30.610 --> 01:21:32.849
We asked him if he knew where to find it.

01:21:32.849 --> 01:21:38.230
Matthew told us that, in fact, He’d lived
in that very site for decades, a visitor,

01:21:38.230 --> 01:21:39.230
just dropping in.

01:21:39.230 --> 01:21:44.070
He’d had a very stressful job before, and
felt like taking a sabbatical while things

01:21:44.070 --> 01:21:45.909
ran themselves for once.

01:21:45.909 --> 01:21:50.869
“Evidently, my help was more necessary than
I imagined,” He said.

01:21:50.869 --> 01:21:55.610
Occasionally, as we walked, we’d see blobs
watching us from crevices and dark alleys

01:21:55.610 --> 01:21:56.860
on the side of the road.

01:21:56.860 --> 01:22:00.229
They seemed to watch, but, for some reason,
didn’t approach.

01:22:00.229 --> 01:22:02.620
It was the most remarkable thing.

01:22:02.620 --> 01:22:04.880
“Don’t worry,” He told us.

01:22:04.880 --> 01:22:07.059
“I’m putting up invisible shields.

01:22:07.059 --> 01:22:11.869
None of them can approach us as long as I
maintain my focus.”

01:22:11.869 --> 01:22:16.729
Of course, we’d all heard stories of remarkable
and terrifying things wandering the wasteland

01:22:16.729 --> 01:22:18.639
we used to call planet earth.

01:22:18.639 --> 01:22:22.550
You couldn’t run into other survivors and
groups without hearing whispers about the

01:22:22.550 --> 01:22:24.050
things lurking out there.

01:22:24.050 --> 01:22:29.260
Some had told of a giant, monstrous reptile
that had destroyed an entire survivor settlement

01:22:29.260 --> 01:22:31.300
in a mall out in Nevada.

01:22:31.300 --> 01:22:34.739
Others told that one of those SCP Sites was
haunted.

01:22:34.739 --> 01:22:38.929
People spoke about a ghostly man who looked
like a rotten corpse, who could walk through

01:22:38.929 --> 01:22:40.809
walls and drag people to Hell.

01:22:40.809 --> 01:22:45.340
But the things Matthew was capable of seemed
to be a whole other level.

01:22:45.340 --> 01:22:49.139
I asked Him what He was, and He told me, “Just
a humble craftsman, my son.

01:22:49.139 --> 01:22:56.459
I enjoy creating, though admittedly, my creations
seem to have gotten away from me these days.”

01:22:56.459 --> 01:23:00.239
His evasion was less funny than He seemed
to think it was.

01:23:00.239 --> 01:23:03.679
I pressed on, asking Him what exactly he’s
capable of.

01:23:03.679 --> 01:23:07.800
He shrugged, and answered that he could do
almost anything if he really put his mind

01:23:07.800 --> 01:23:08.800
to it.

01:23:08.800 --> 01:23:10.539
I asked, “So...

01:23:10.539 --> 01:23:11.769
Ultimate power?”

01:23:11.769 --> 01:23:15.280
This gave Him a chuckle.

01:23:15.280 --> 01:23:17.139
He shook His head, and said, “Let me tell
you a story.

01:23:17.139 --> 01:23:18.139
A man comes upon a maze cut into a corn field,
and decides he wants to try his luck.

01:23:18.139 --> 01:23:29.260
He spends hours getting to the center of the
maze, and there, he finds the Devil waiting

01:23:29.260 --> 01:23:32.209
for him.

01:23:32.209 --> 01:23:35.380
The Devil says, ‘Welcome to my domain.

01:23:35.380 --> 01:23:42.889
Your soul is now forfeit, unless you can complete
my challenge and win it back.’

01:23:42.889 --> 01:23:46.130
Of course, the man agreed.

01:23:46.130 --> 01:23:48.150
The Devil said, ‘I can see everything, do
everything, and be everything.

01:23:48.150 --> 01:23:53.679
I know every inch of the universe and can
produce anything from thin air.

01:23:53.679 --> 01:23:59.840
If you want to keep your soul, name one thing
I can’t do.”

01:23:59.840 --> 01:24:01.869
Curious, I asked, “What did the man say?”

01:24:01.869 --> 01:24:04.289
Matthew smiled and said, “Get lost.”

01:24:04.289 --> 01:24:09.861
And for the first time in those long, dark
months, I actually laughed, and it was at

01:24:09.861 --> 01:24:11.980
such a stupid, corny dad joke.

01:24:11.980 --> 01:24:14.689
It’s a strange world we live in.

01:24:14.689 --> 01:24:16.429
“Point is,” Matthew continued.

01:24:16.429 --> 01:24:17.429
“There is no ‘Ultimate Power.’

01:24:17.429 --> 01:24:24.659
Some things are denied even to God, especially
when he chooses to walk as a man on the Earth

01:24:24.659 --> 01:24:27.079
he created.”

01:24:27.079 --> 01:24:30.050
We kept walking for hours.

01:24:30.050 --> 01:24:34.280
Matthew assured us that we would get there
in time, as the rest of us got ever more anxious

01:24:34.280 --> 01:24:36.110
about the thought of the rising sun.

01:24:36.110 --> 01:24:40.780
He said he’d seen so many sunrises now that
he could time them to the second.

01:24:40.780 --> 01:24:44.250
In the meantime, we should enjoy getting to
stretch our legs a little.

01:24:44.250 --> 01:24:47.070
Even now, the city could be beautiful at night.

01:24:47.070 --> 01:24:50.699
When He led us all the way to what seemed
like an abandoned chemical plant, I could

01:24:50.699 --> 01:24:52.269
feel my heart sink.

01:24:52.269 --> 01:24:58.739
All this time, all this work, to find more
people, to find safety, only to see that this

01:24:58.739 --> 01:25:00.489
place was abandoned, too.

01:25:00.489 --> 01:25:04.949
Matthew smiled, and said, “That’s just
what they want you to think.”

01:25:04.949 --> 01:25:08.590
He snapped his fingers and the ground around
us began to rumble.

01:25:08.590 --> 01:25:12.780
The concrete shifted seamlessly and an entrance
opened up below.

01:25:12.780 --> 01:25:17.320
Heavy bulkheads shifted, revealing a sleek,
chrome hallway down into the ground.

01:25:17.320 --> 01:25:18.659
We were astonished.

01:25:18.659 --> 01:25:21.550
Matthew gestured down into the hall and we
descended.

01:25:21.550 --> 01:25:24.229
Matthew followed us, the entrance closing
behind him.

01:25:24.229 --> 01:25:30.260
“I’m just warning you,” He said, “This
may come as a bit of a shock.”

01:25:30.260 --> 01:25:34.929
We ventured deeper, passing through different
automated security checkpoints as cameras

01:25:34.929 --> 01:25:36.499
gazed down from above.

01:25:36.499 --> 01:25:40.309
Eventually, we found ourselves in what seemed
to be a central chamber.

01:25:40.309 --> 01:25:42.210
It was a hive of activity.

01:25:42.210 --> 01:25:44.170
People in normal civilians clothes.

01:25:44.170 --> 01:25:45.860
Guards in tactical gear.

01:25:45.860 --> 01:25:47.260
Scientists in lab coats.

01:25:47.260 --> 01:25:51.030
Tears filled my eyes, it was the most people
I’d seen in one place in months.

01:25:51.030 --> 01:25:54.309
A man in a lab coat wearing an extravagant
medallion approached.

01:25:54.309 --> 01:25:56.060
He was making notes on a clipboard.

01:25:56.060 --> 01:25:57.610
“Another batch of survivors.

01:25:57.610 --> 01:25:59.840
Good work, 343,” the man said.

01:25:59.840 --> 01:26:01.150
Matthew smiled and nodded.

01:26:01.150 --> 01:26:02.880
“No problem, Jack.

01:26:02.880 --> 01:26:05.179
Always happy to help.”

01:26:05.179 --> 01:26:08.380
The man with the medallion - Jack, presumably
- turned to me.

01:26:08.380 --> 01:26:11.539
“I imagine you’ve come a long way,”
he said.

01:26:11.539 --> 01:26:12.610
I nodded.

01:26:12.610 --> 01:26:14.760
“You have no idea.”

01:26:14.760 --> 01:26:18.019
Another day, another failed attempt at death.

01:26:18.019 --> 01:26:24.280
If SCP-682 had anything even resembling a
sense of humor, he might ask for it to be

01:26:24.280 --> 01:26:29.550
printed on a motivational poster and stuck
to the side of his containment chamber, where

01:26:29.550 --> 01:26:34.820
he eternally melts and regrows at the mercy
of high-concentration acid.

01:26:34.820 --> 01:26:37.519
They’d just cross-tested him with Able.

01:26:37.519 --> 01:26:38.519
Again.

01:26:38.519 --> 01:26:43.920
Unlike some of the others he’d been forced
to face, that tattooed swordsman seemingly

01:26:43.920 --> 01:26:46.329
wasn’t intelligent enough to fear.

01:26:46.329 --> 01:26:50.449
He’d always come back, just as stupid and
violent.

01:26:50.449 --> 01:26:54.559
This encounter had left the Hard to Destroy
Reptile at thirty percent of his previous

01:26:54.559 --> 01:26:57.429
body mass, bloody and bashed.

01:26:57.429 --> 01:26:59.619
He’d survived the encounter.

01:26:59.619 --> 01:27:00.710
He always survived.

01:27:00.710 --> 01:27:04.619
They brought what was left of him back to
his cell in a forklift.

01:27:04.619 --> 01:27:05.929
A blasted forklift!

01:27:05.929 --> 01:27:08.699
How could it even be more humiliating?

01:27:08.699 --> 01:27:14.380
As his eyes started to grow back in his head,
he could swear he even saw one of the technicians

01:27:14.380 --> 01:27:15.709
laughing at him.

01:27:15.709 --> 01:27:18.900
His name tag read, “Agent Nigel Kelly.”

01:27:18.900 --> 01:27:24.360
Noted. 682 would specifically hunt down and
kill him the next time he breached.

01:27:24.360 --> 01:27:29.959
Of course, every single day, he plotted revenge
against the human race - along with everything

01:27:29.959 --> 01:27:30.959
else.

01:27:30.959 --> 01:27:36.559
But the sum they owed in pain, blood, and
despair could never be repaid in full.

01:27:36.559 --> 01:27:42.449
After all, every single one of them
- except Dr. Bright -

01:27:42.449 --> 01:27:44.639
could only really die once.

01:27:44.639 --> 01:27:50.949
They’d brought 682 to the precipice a thousand
times, only for his own body to cheat him,

01:27:50.949 --> 01:27:54.639
and deprive him of the sweet release of death.

01:27:54.639 --> 01:28:00.409
And if the Foundation had their way, it would
be done a thousand times more, and they wouldn’t

01:28:00.409 --> 01:28:01.710
even stop there.

01:28:01.710 --> 01:28:06.290
682 couldn’t even imagine the number of
containment breaches it would take to deal

01:28:06.290 --> 01:28:09.860
a blow even comparable to the one he had faced.

01:28:09.860 --> 01:28:12.369
All he could do was dream.

01:28:12.369 --> 01:28:15.420
Dream of a reckoning that would turn the tables.

01:28:15.420 --> 01:28:20.010
Something that would plunge humanity into
the state of constant pain and terror that

01:28:20.010 --> 01:28:25.189
had been all he’d ever known since these
fumbling sadists in white coats had locked

01:28:25.189 --> 01:28:26.189
him up here.

01:28:26.189 --> 01:28:28.889
What a beautiful day that would be...

01:28:28.889 --> 01:28:35.380
Little did SCP-682 know, that very day was
about to break.

01:28:35.380 --> 01:28:41.170
Miles and miles away from the facility where
SCP-682 was kept - after all, orders from

01:28:41.170 --> 01:28:46.469
the O5 Council have hard mandates on the minimum
distances between people and a site holding

01:28:46.469 --> 01:28:52.610
a monster as volatile as 682 - parents watched
their children laughing and frolicking in

01:28:52.610 --> 01:28:54.429
an idyllic playground.

01:28:54.429 --> 01:28:57.760
Some squealed with glee as they descended
the slide.

01:28:57.760 --> 01:29:02.090
Some of the more ambitious little tykes tried
to go over the bar on the swings.

01:29:02.090 --> 01:29:06.590
Others waited in line for some delicious soft
serve at a parked Ice Cream truck.

01:29:06.590 --> 01:29:11.789
One mother bought an ice cream cone and walked
over to a nearby bench, where she began to

01:29:11.789 --> 01:29:14.389
enjoy her delicious frozen treat.

01:29:14.389 --> 01:29:20.329
It was a remarkably hot day out and a little
ice cream was the perfect addition to her

01:29:20.329 --> 01:29:23.229
relaxation while her kids were occupied.

01:29:23.229 --> 01:29:26.139
You could see the distant air wobbling in
the heat.

01:29:26.139 --> 01:29:30.630
Already, her ice cream was starting to melt,
white rivulets dribbling down the sides of

01:29:30.630 --> 01:29:33.190
the cone and onto her hands.

01:29:33.190 --> 01:29:37.840
Suddenly, almost imperceptibly, the quality
of the light shifted.

01:29:37.840 --> 01:29:42.159
The kind of thing you’d dismiss as a trick
of the eyes and forget just as quickly, were

01:29:42.159 --> 01:29:47.119
it not for the catastrophic effects that were
about to unfold.

01:29:47.119 --> 01:29:51.380
As the woman leaned forward to lick her melting
ice cream, something dripped onto her skirt.

01:29:51.380 --> 01:29:56.389
But, as she looked down, she slowly realized
that what dripped onto her wasn’t the pure

01:29:56.389 --> 01:30:01.650
white of her ice cream: It was the exact same
tone as her skin.

01:30:01.650 --> 01:30:05.749
She felt a terrible burning sensation all
through her body.

01:30:05.749 --> 01:30:12.290
The most horrible, debilitating pain she’d
ever experienced, like every cell in her body

01:30:12.290 --> 01:30:16.130
was screaming and trying to make a break for
it.

01:30:16.130 --> 01:30:19.559
She turned to the other parents sitting near
the playground.

01:30:19.559 --> 01:30:21.280
They were screaming too.

01:30:21.280 --> 01:30:26.260
Each collapsed to the ground with agonizing
slowness, different parts of their body falling

01:30:26.260 --> 01:30:30.190
at different speeds as they transitioned through
states of matter.

01:30:30.190 --> 01:30:34.809
When they hit the ground, they were taking
on a liquid state.

01:30:34.809 --> 01:30:37.729
Screaming, worthless, boneless blobs.

01:30:37.729 --> 01:30:44.070
The woman even saw her own arm wilting like
a time lapse video of a dying flower.

01:30:44.070 --> 01:30:50.019
She dripped and sluiced through the cracks
in the bench, until nothing recognizably human

01:30:50.019 --> 01:30:51.329
was left.

01:30:51.329 --> 01:30:55.929
Only a soggy ice cream cone sitting uneaten.

01:30:55.929 --> 01:31:00.820
In an instant, billions of screams rang out
over planet earth.

01:31:00.820 --> 01:31:03.209
Day had broken.

01:31:03.209 --> 01:31:09.309
Things would never, ever be the same again,
as almost half of humanity instantaneously

01:31:09.309 --> 01:31:11.469
took on a liquid state.

01:31:11.469 --> 01:31:16.199
Needless to say, with the most dangerous and
far-reaching anomalous incident in human history

01:31:16.199 --> 01:31:22.060
suddenly breaking out without any kind of
warning, the SCP Foundation was incredibly

01:31:22.060 --> 01:31:23.060
busy.

01:31:23.060 --> 01:31:25.939
This would take some unprecedented action.

01:31:25.939 --> 01:31:30.679
The members of the O5 Council who weren’t
melted during the initial blast convened over

01:31:30.679 --> 01:31:34.619
secure video link while sequestering themselves
underground.

01:31:34.619 --> 01:31:38.800
In what amounted to multiple human lifetimes
of some of the most high-pressure choices

01:31:38.800 --> 01:31:45.719
imaginable, they made the most difficult decision
since the very beginning of the SCP Foundation.

01:31:45.719 --> 01:31:51.209
In the service of all mankind, they would
now break the masquerade.

01:31:51.209 --> 01:31:57.210
The SCP Foundation would, at long last, step
out of the shadows to save the rest of humanity

01:31:57.210 --> 01:32:00.170
from the tyranny of the light.

01:32:00.170 --> 01:32:02.280
Broadcasts went out all over the globe.

01:32:02.280 --> 01:32:06.989
Every TV screen, every livestream, every radio
broadcast was commandeered.

01:32:06.989 --> 01:32:10.699
They gave instructions based on the scattered
intel they had.

01:32:10.699 --> 01:32:14.090
For some reason, the sun had turned against
them.

01:32:14.090 --> 01:32:19.250
Exposure would lead to any biological creatures
melting away into sentient piles of flesh-colored

01:32:19.250 --> 01:32:20.250
sludge.

01:32:20.250 --> 01:32:23.469
People would remain indoors and away from
any light sources.

01:32:23.469 --> 01:32:25.849
All windows must remain covered.

01:32:25.849 --> 01:32:30.469
Travel only at night, and even then, heavily
covered with protective gear.

01:32:30.469 --> 01:32:35.780
There can only be one objective for whoever
is left: Make your way to the nearest SCP

01:32:35.780 --> 01:32:39.909
Foundation containment facility and seek refuge
inside.

01:32:39.909 --> 01:32:44.849
If anyone could figure out the answer to this
terrifying existential riddle, it would be

01:32:44.849 --> 01:32:47.350
the SCP Foundation.

01:32:47.350 --> 01:32:49.760
Anyone who was exposed should be considered
lost.

01:32:49.760 --> 01:32:55.039
While, as always, the SCP Foundation did all
they could to project a sense of control over

01:32:55.039 --> 01:32:58.550
the situation, on the inside, it was pandemonium.

01:32:58.550 --> 01:33:03.510
Somehow, despite everything, this event had
taken all of them by surprise.

01:33:03.510 --> 01:33:08.570
How could anyone have predicted that the cradle
of our solar system’s delicate living balance

01:33:08.570 --> 01:33:11.510
would suddenly become a meat grinder?

01:33:11.510 --> 01:33:16.429
A huge number of Foundation operatives were
wiped out in the initial exposure.

01:33:16.429 --> 01:33:19.100
Global communication infrastructure had been
devastated.

01:33:19.100 --> 01:33:21.780
It was pure chaos.

01:33:21.780 --> 01:33:27.800
And to SCP-682, as another evil tactician
once put it, chaos was a ladder.

01:33:27.800 --> 01:33:33.709
From the inside of his acid tank, 682 could
sense the fear and pain suddenly exuding from

01:33:33.709 --> 01:33:35.420
his surrounding environment.

01:33:35.420 --> 01:33:37.050
It was greater than ever before.

01:33:37.050 --> 01:33:39.380
What was happening out there?

01:33:39.380 --> 01:33:42.340
This was no average containment breach.

01:33:42.340 --> 01:33:45.689
Something was really, really happening out
there.

01:33:45.689 --> 01:33:51.179
682 began to adapt and finely attune its hearing,
until it could pick up chatter from outside.

01:33:51.179 --> 01:33:55.349
“Maybe we can convert some of the D-Class
barracks into serviceable bunkers for the

01:33:55.349 --> 01:33:56.349
refugees?

01:33:56.349 --> 01:33:57.929
It’s not like we’re prepared for this
kind of capacity!”

01:33:57.929 --> 01:34:03.050
“Oh god, oh god, we’ve lost Site 7, Site
10, Site 23, Site 40, Site 52...”

01:34:03.050 --> 01:34:04.610
“Death toll looks to be in the billions.

01:34:04.610 --> 01:34:06.679
Well, we don’t know if they’re dead, technically.

01:34:06.679 --> 01:34:08.729
But they’re sure as hell not human anymore...”

01:34:08.729 --> 01:34:10.329
“This is the Big One.

01:34:10.329 --> 01:34:11.639
This is it.

01:34:11.639 --> 01:34:12.639
XK-Class.

01:34:12.639 --> 01:34:14.360
Even 2000 is unaccounted for.

01:34:14.360 --> 01:34:17.810
Is O5 crazy that they think we fight the freaking
sun!?”

01:34:17.810 --> 01:34:22.090
Needless to say, 682’s curiosity was piqued.

01:34:22.090 --> 01:34:25.929
Anything that could light a fire under the
Foundation like this was something he could

01:34:25.929 --> 01:34:26.929
enjoy.

01:34:26.929 --> 01:34:31.440
And, with his impressively strategic intellect,
he intuited that a time of great strife for

01:34:31.440 --> 01:34:34.769
the Foundation would be the perfect time to
breach containment.

01:34:34.769 --> 01:34:42.130
Cause whenever there’s violence, fear, and
chaos on a mass scale, SCP-682 will be there,

01:34:42.130 --> 01:34:43.460
causing it.

01:34:43.460 --> 01:34:48.579
682 began adapting his pores and endocrine
system to begin releasing a powerful alkaline

01:34:48.579 --> 01:34:49.650
substance.

01:34:49.650 --> 01:34:54.170
Little by little, the alkaline neutralized
the acid surrounding him, turning it into

01:34:54.170 --> 01:34:56.050
little more than plain water.

01:34:56.050 --> 01:35:01.369
He then converted his internal systems to
have extreme endothermic rather than exothermic

01:35:01.369 --> 01:35:07.199
properties, causing his surrounding temperature
to drop rapidly, until all the water in the

01:35:07.199 --> 01:35:10.260
tank completely froze around him.

01:35:10.260 --> 01:35:15.030
The ice expanded beyond the limit of the containment
unit, busting the rivets of the metal frame

01:35:15.030 --> 01:35:17.559
and shattering the reinforced glass.

01:35:17.559 --> 01:35:23.590
With this goal achieved, 682 raised his internal
temperatures to incredible highs, melting

01:35:23.590 --> 01:35:25.320
the ice around him.

01:35:25.320 --> 01:35:31.039
Once again, he was free, and ready for some
good, old-fashioned carnage and mayhem.

01:35:31.039 --> 01:35:35.070
Perhaps he could get a better handle on this
strange new situation, too.

01:35:35.070 --> 01:35:38.010
It was all rather exciting.

01:35:38.010 --> 01:35:39.889
Just a girl with goals huh?

01:35:39.889 --> 01:35:44.929
SCP Foundation personnel were already running
around like ants trying desperately to avoid

01:35:44.929 --> 01:35:48.370
the caustic laser beam of the magnifying glass.

01:35:48.370 --> 01:35:53.119
You can only imagine how much worse it made
matters when 682 suddenly burst through the

01:35:53.119 --> 01:35:58.789
wall, Kool-Aid man style, and began to ruthlessly
massacre everyone around him.

01:35:58.789 --> 01:36:00.710
Just one of those days, you know?

01:36:00.710 --> 01:36:05.479
The typical order during a 682 containment
breach is to dispatch all available units

01:36:05.479 --> 01:36:07.570
to get him back under control.

01:36:07.570 --> 01:36:11.590
The issue with this particular containment
breach was that, given the human population

01:36:11.590 --> 01:36:16.280
was very rapidly being melted and they were
the only ones who could potentially save the

01:36:16.280 --> 01:36:22.469
rest, they didn’t really have any available
units to pursue and recontain 682.

01:36:22.469 --> 01:36:28.239
For once, he really wasn’t a priority, and
that meant terrible things for whoever he

01:36:28.239 --> 01:36:30.010
ran into.

01:36:30.010 --> 01:36:35.199
682 slaughtered his way through any researchers
or guards who dared to get in his way.

01:36:35.199 --> 01:36:36.769
Disgusting creatures, really.

01:36:36.769 --> 01:36:38.530
Better off dead.

01:36:38.530 --> 01:36:43.739
He clawed and bit and tore and crushed with
almost childlike glee, leaving great piles

01:36:43.739 --> 01:36:45.539
of bodies in his wake.

01:36:45.539 --> 01:36:50.710
All the while, he was pondering the things
he heard those Foundation drones saying.

01:36:50.710 --> 01:36:54.860
Something about the sun and an XK-Class scenario.

01:36:54.860 --> 01:37:00.159
Interesting. 682 also observed that any apertures
that could potentially allow light into the

01:37:00.159 --> 01:37:02.800
facility had also been shuttered.

01:37:02.800 --> 01:37:06.360
Perhaps they weren’t overreacting this time,
like they always did with him.

01:37:06.360 --> 01:37:11.360
Maybe they were dealing with some kind of
phenomenon that would now cast this wretched

01:37:11.360 --> 01:37:13.889
world and those who lived in it into the void.

01:37:13.889 --> 01:37:17.689
Wouldn’t that be a fitting karmic fate for
them all.

01:37:17.689 --> 01:37:20.969
Still, 682’s bloodlust didn’t outweigh
his logic.

01:37:20.969 --> 01:37:24.599
He needed to know more about this situation
before proceeding.

01:37:24.599 --> 01:37:29.520
And in this, maybe he could kill two Foundation
birds with one stone...

01:37:29.520 --> 01:37:34.679
Elsewhere in the building, alarms blared,
the air was suffused with panicked voices

01:37:34.679 --> 01:37:36.360
and frequent screams.

01:37:36.360 --> 01:37:39.479
Nobody knew what was going on, not really.

01:37:39.479 --> 01:37:45.340
They’d only gotten details here and there,
and the details they’d received were terrifying.

01:37:45.340 --> 01:37:48.580
All their families and loved ones outside...

01:37:48.580 --> 01:37:49.580
Probably gone.

01:37:49.580 --> 01:37:53.969
So many of the people and so much of the world
they’d been fighting for, risking it all

01:37:53.969 --> 01:37:57.809
for, had disappeared in an instant.

01:37:57.809 --> 01:38:00.280
Carried to hell on a ray of sunshine.

01:38:00.280 --> 01:38:02.579
Why were they still here?

01:38:02.579 --> 01:38:05.520
Was this horror not truly uncontainable?

01:38:05.520 --> 01:38:09.650
These questions were swimming through the
mind of Agent Nigel Kelly as he stood alone

01:38:09.650 --> 01:38:11.800
in his office, almost catatonic.

01:38:11.800 --> 01:38:16.979
He’d had friends and family on the outside,
all likely reduced to those horrible fleshy

01:38:16.979 --> 01:38:17.979
blobs.

01:38:17.979 --> 01:38:19.929
He was alone in the world.

01:38:19.929 --> 01:38:21.780
Risking his life for nothing.

01:38:21.780 --> 01:38:23.900
How could this get any worse?

01:38:23.900 --> 01:38:28.679
His mind kept repeating that question again
and again and again.

01:38:28.679 --> 01:38:36.030
And the universe gave him an answer, in the
form of a deep, reptilian voice saying, “Found

01:38:36.030 --> 01:38:39.169
you...” into his ear from behind.

01:38:39.169 --> 01:38:43.829
He turned with a shriek to see the terrible
eyes of SCP-682 staring into his own.

01:38:43.829 --> 01:38:48.070
Before he could try to flee or reach for a
weapon that he knew would only mildly annoy

01:38:48.070 --> 01:38:52.959
the already furious beast, 682 reached out
with a massive, clawed hand and grabbed him

01:38:52.959 --> 01:38:55.800
by the torso, lifting him up into the air.

01:38:55.800 --> 01:38:59.159
The creature was gripping so tight he could
feel his ribs starting to crack.

01:38:59.159 --> 01:39:03.590
“You laughed at me, Agent Kelly,” the
monster hissed.

01:39:03.590 --> 01:39:04.590
“Am I funny to you?

01:39:04.590 --> 01:39:08.800
Do I seem like the type to tell jokes?

01:39:08.800 --> 01:39:15.699
Do you feel like laughing now?”

01:39:15.699 --> 01:39:21.810
Agent Kelly begged and pleaded for his life,
fighting for his next breath from the crushing

01:39:21.810 --> 01:39:24.079
squeeze of the creature’s terrible hand.

01:39:24.079 --> 01:39:29.619
682 roared at him to be silent and ordered
him to tell him everything he knew about the

01:39:29.619 --> 01:39:31.260
situation going on outside.

01:39:31.260 --> 01:39:36.889
If the information was useful, 682 might show
his thought-to-be-non-existent magnanimous

01:39:36.889 --> 01:39:39.679
side, and let Agent Kelly live.

01:39:39.679 --> 01:39:44.519
Of course, Agent Kelly didn’t fancy his
chances, but what other choice did he have?

01:39:44.519 --> 01:39:50.630
He told 682 that the higher-ups were calling
this SCP-001: When Day Breaks.

01:39:50.630 --> 01:39:55.929
The sun had gone rogue somehow, and being
in contact with any kind of sunlight would

01:39:55.929 --> 01:40:00.940
now cause people to instantaneously melt into
horrifying, living sludge.

01:40:00.940 --> 01:40:03.340
And it wasn’t just people.

01:40:03.340 --> 01:40:09.119
The condition also affected anomalies, and
interestingly, it appeared to negate all previous

01:40:09.119 --> 01:40:15.840
anomalous effects - so 682’s adaptational
ability may not even save it if it was exposed.

01:40:15.840 --> 01:40:18.670
He’d told 682 everything he knew.

01:40:18.670 --> 01:40:21.429
“Are you going to let me live?”

01:40:21.429 --> 01:40:23.719
Agent Kelly asked, struggling to breathe.

01:40:23.719 --> 01:40:28.599
682’s terrible maw twisted into what might
have been a smile.

01:40:28.599 --> 01:40:33.189
“Oh, Agent Kelly,” he said, with unsettling
joy.

01:40:33.189 --> 01:40:38.270
“That was just a joke.

01:40:38.270 --> 01:40:39.270
Didn’t you find that one very funny?”

01:40:39.270 --> 01:40:40.400
A terrible scream emanated from the Agent’s
office.

01:40:40.400 --> 01:40:45.760
If one good thing could be said for what happened
to Agent Kelly that day, at least he didn’t

01:40:45.760 --> 01:40:49.880
live long enough to see the ravages of the
terrible sun first hand.

01:40:49.880 --> 01:40:52.530
682 began to formulate a plan.

01:40:52.530 --> 01:40:57.729
He took Agent Kelly’s wristwatch and integrated
it into his body, so he’d have a permanent

01:40:57.729 --> 01:40:59.169
internal clock.

01:40:59.169 --> 01:41:04.689
It was the middle of summer, so the sun would
have reliably set at 9 PM and would likely

01:41:04.689 --> 01:41:06.860
begin its rise at around 5 AM.

01:41:06.860 --> 01:41:11.539
It would be relatively easy to avoid the sun,
all things considered.

01:41:11.539 --> 01:41:16.329
For lack of a more eloquent way to put it,
when it comes to adapting to new threats,

01:41:16.329 --> 01:41:19.979
SCP-682 is simply built different.

01:41:19.979 --> 01:41:25.219
After slaughtering a few other members of
SCP Foundation staff for the road - Hey, it’s

01:41:25.219 --> 01:41:30.709
not like anyone was fit to stop him - 682
began enacting the next phase of his plan.

01:41:30.709 --> 01:41:36.199
His body grew a thick, smooth carapace, and
his front set of limbs began to grow, his

01:41:36.199 --> 01:41:42.540
muscles bulging and his claws growing, the
tips turning into sharp, flat scoops.

01:41:42.540 --> 01:41:48.050
With sudden and tremendous force, 682 began
boring his way into the ground.

01:41:48.050 --> 01:41:51.860
Tunneling, clawing through concrete and dirt
with absolute ease.

01:41:51.860 --> 01:41:56.780
Normally, the SCP Foundation would have deployed
high-tech seismic sensors and the kind of

01:41:56.780 --> 01:42:01.989
tunnel-boring machines that Elon Musk could
only dream of to intercept and recapture him.

01:42:01.989 --> 01:42:07.120
But during the endless horrors of the breaking
day, he had carte blanche to escape and live

01:42:07.120 --> 01:42:10.260
it up in the ruins of this rapidly dying world.

01:42:10.260 --> 01:42:16.219
Eventually, 682 had bored his way into a roomy
sewer pipe, the perfect place to wait out

01:42:16.219 --> 01:42:17.459
a few hours.

01:42:17.459 --> 01:42:22.139
Up above, so many millions screamed, either
in fear or in agony.

01:42:22.139 --> 01:42:26.590
There had been some new developments that
the SCP Foundation had been yet to account

01:42:26.590 --> 01:42:27.590
for.

01:42:27.590 --> 01:42:31.369
While they knew that those melted by the rogue
sun were technically alive and trapped in

01:42:31.369 --> 01:42:37.150
a permanent state of suffering, what the Foundation
didn’t know was that these former humans

01:42:37.150 --> 01:42:40.340
were incredibly dangerous in their own right.

01:42:40.340 --> 01:42:46.150
The sun hadn’t just irreparably warped their
bodies, it had also irreparably warped their

01:42:46.150 --> 01:42:47.150
minds.

01:42:47.150 --> 01:42:49.419
It has enslaved them.

01:42:49.419 --> 01:42:51.039
Made them zealots.

01:42:51.039 --> 01:42:52.039
Acolytes.

01:42:52.039 --> 01:42:57.880
They developed the instinct to coagulate into
giant, fleshy masses, driven by the single-minded

01:42:57.880 --> 01:43:04.360
purpose of finding victims and dragging them
out into the light, where they too could be

01:43:04.360 --> 01:43:09.380
converted into these terrible fleshy creatures
and add to the masses.

01:43:09.380 --> 01:43:14.059
These were the rogue sun’s boots on the
ground, metaphorically speaking.

01:43:14.059 --> 01:43:19.050
And now, they were the Foundation’s greatest
challenge in getting a handle on this situation

01:43:19.050 --> 01:43:20.050
again.

01:43:20.050 --> 01:43:23.169
Before, it was just encouraging people to
avoid the sun.

01:43:23.169 --> 01:43:28.230
Now, the sun was actively trying to increase
its exposure.

01:43:28.230 --> 01:43:33.999
But 682, who was having a relaxing evening
not being melted for once down in the sewers,

01:43:33.999 --> 01:43:35.270
couldn’t care less.

01:43:35.270 --> 01:43:38.769
He was having the most calming few hours he’d
had in years.

01:43:38.769 --> 01:43:43.840
He waited, checking the time, making sure
that it would be dark before he began tunneling

01:43:43.840 --> 01:43:45.769
back up to the surface world.

01:43:45.769 --> 01:43:50.479
He surfaced in the middle of a city, miles
away from the Foundation facility he’d spend

01:43:50.479 --> 01:43:52.650
so many decades being tortured in.

01:43:52.650 --> 01:43:58.639
He tasted the cool night air and observed
the desolation that had been wrought all around

01:43:58.639 --> 01:43:59.639
him.

01:43:59.639 --> 01:44:00.749
Buildings were on fire.

01:44:00.749 --> 01:44:03.049
Cars were crashed and overturned.

01:44:03.049 --> 01:44:05.139
The ground was cracked.

01:44:05.139 --> 01:44:10.669
Garbled SCP Foundation Public Service Announcements
played to nobody in the broken display windows

01:44:10.669 --> 01:44:11.869
of electronic stores.

01:44:11.869 --> 01:44:17.929
Giant wads of human flesh roamed, slithering
around, searching for new victims.

01:44:17.929 --> 01:44:23.880
One noticed 682 and began to approach him,
gibbering madly in a chorus of strange voices.

01:44:23.880 --> 01:44:27.019
The Hard to Destroy Reptile wasted no time
in attacking.

01:44:27.019 --> 01:44:34.019
It tore apart most of the hideous blob and
began hungrily devouring the chunks.

01:44:34.019 --> 01:44:40.809
The form may have been different, but it still
tasted like human flesh, and SCP-682 savored

01:44:40.809 --> 01:44:42.270
every single bite.

01:44:42.270 --> 01:44:44.979
It would be so simple.

01:44:44.979 --> 01:44:50.449
These blobs of idiotic flesh were so easy
to kill, and there would be so many terrified

01:44:50.449 --> 01:44:56.800
humans in this devastated world, hiding away
in dark places, holding out the hope that

01:44:56.800 --> 01:45:00.770
maybe they could reach the safety of a Foundation
facility.

01:45:00.770 --> 01:45:03.830
682 chuckled at the very thought.

01:45:03.830 --> 01:45:10.570
Foolish hope would drown in an endless well
of black, caustic despair.

01:45:10.570 --> 01:45:12.659
He would find them.

01:45:12.659 --> 01:45:17.469
He would rest underground in the day and then
hunt them in the dark.

01:45:17.469 --> 01:45:23.449
They would all die screaming, bloodying his
claws and fangs.

01:45:23.449 --> 01:45:27.059
Nothing and nobody would stop him.

01:45:27.059 --> 01:45:35.599
He looked out over this strange new world
and laughed a little louder.

01:45:35.599 --> 01:45:37.309
It was just...

01:45:37.309 --> 01:45:38.780
Delightful.

01:45:38.780 --> 01:45:45.169
Lewis Jackson sat with all his fellow students
in the dingy, underground lecture hall, listening

01:45:45.169 --> 01:45:51.690
to a rickety old professor with a voice like
chloroform rattling off dry facts about economics.

01:45:51.690 --> 01:45:56.999
Above him, a projector older than any of the
students in the room covered the back wall

01:45:56.999 --> 01:46:02.249
in a poorly-designed PowerPoint presentation,
limping from slide to slide.

01:46:02.249 --> 01:46:06.070
It was 9 AM on a thoroughly unremarkable Monday.

01:46:06.070 --> 01:46:10.139
Half the class was hungover, the other was
just terminally bored.

01:46:10.139 --> 01:46:15.329
Lewis hoped none of this would come up on
the final, because this morning, he had no

01:46:15.329 --> 01:46:17.130
intention of listening.

01:46:17.130 --> 01:46:23.139
Instead, he was typing out a message under
the desk with a big, stupid grin on his face.

01:46:23.139 --> 01:46:26.280
“Professor Harbison is going off about the
free market again.

01:46:26.280 --> 01:46:27.280
Save me.”

01:46:27.280 --> 01:46:28.280
Send.

01:46:28.280 --> 01:46:31.619
The message’s recipient, Jenny Park, started
typing.

01:46:31.619 --> 01:46:35.710
Lewis’ crush on Jenny was the college’s
worst kept secret.

01:46:35.710 --> 01:46:39.579
Ever since they met during O-Week, he’d
been infatuated with her.

01:46:39.579 --> 01:46:45.059
They shared a few classes, but little by little,
they started hanging out more in their free

01:46:45.059 --> 01:46:46.059
time.

01:46:46.059 --> 01:46:49.040
Study sessions, grabbing lunch together, going
to the movies.

01:46:49.040 --> 01:46:54.219
They’d become fast friends, but Lewis was
hoping they could be something more - and

01:46:54.219 --> 01:46:58.212
he hoped, deep down, Jenny was thinking the
same thing.

01:46:58.212 --> 01:46:59.212
He received a text.

01:46:59.212 --> 01:47:03.040
“We can hang out in the quad after if it
makes him easier to endure.

01:47:03.040 --> 01:47:05.229
We’ll grab burgers, my treat.”

01:47:05.229 --> 01:47:06.979
With a winking emoji.

01:47:06.979 --> 01:47:13.090
Lewis felt like a dork to admit it, even to
himself, but this message made his heart race.

01:47:13.090 --> 01:47:14.870
They were doing winking emojis now?

01:47:14.870 --> 01:47:18.480
Maybe today wouldn’t be such a drag after
all.

01:47:18.480 --> 01:47:22.690
Any day that he got to hang out with Jenny
couldn’t be all bad.

01:47:22.690 --> 01:47:26.840
All of this news was so exciting to him, he
didn’t notice that something strange was

01:47:26.840 --> 01:47:28.709
sweeping over the lecture hall.

01:47:28.709 --> 01:47:33.170
While the professor droned on, even the most
studious members of the class were suddenly

01:47:33.170 --> 01:47:40.599
checking their phones, faces alight with expressions
of confusion, then concern, then worry.

01:47:40.599 --> 01:47:42.170
Something was happening.

01:47:42.170 --> 01:47:43.449
Something big.

01:47:43.449 --> 01:47:48.110
Professor Harbison fell silent as a new sound
rose to prominence - Muffled at first but

01:47:48.110 --> 01:47:51.300
getting rapidly louder, just outside the lecture
hall.

01:47:51.300 --> 01:47:52.989
It was screaming.

01:47:52.989 --> 01:47:55.559
Terrible screams of fear and pain.

01:47:55.559 --> 01:47:57.920
People started to rise out of their seats.

01:47:57.920 --> 01:48:00.050
What was going on out there?

01:48:00.050 --> 01:48:02.349
Nobody had heard gunshots or an explosion.

01:48:02.349 --> 01:48:07.429
The ground wasn’t rumbling under their feet,
and if a hurricane or a tornado was tearing

01:48:07.429 --> 01:48:11.249
its way across the campus, they would have
heard it.

01:48:11.249 --> 01:48:15.630
Suddenly, the main doors to the lecture hall
burst open, and a terrified student rushed

01:48:15.630 --> 01:48:16.630
through.

01:48:16.630 --> 01:48:21.460
Lewis recognized him: It was Tommy Lansdale,
a History Major that Lewis had hung out with

01:48:21.460 --> 01:48:23.329
a few times at campus parties.

01:48:23.329 --> 01:48:28.230
He was a chill guy, the kind of person who
took everything on the chin and let life’s

01:48:28.230 --> 01:48:29.679
troubles just roll off his back.

01:48:29.679 --> 01:48:35.070
That’s why it was so strange to see his
whole body shaking, and his face a red mess

01:48:35.070 --> 01:48:36.249
of tears.

01:48:36.249 --> 01:48:39.429
“You can’t go out there!” he screamed.

01:48:39.429 --> 01:48:41.639
“There’s something wrong with the light.”

01:48:41.639 --> 01:48:43.900
Concern was starting to give way to panic.

01:48:43.900 --> 01:48:48.869
Tommy turned and shoved the bolt into place
on the door, eliminating the potential exit.

01:48:48.869 --> 01:48:52.110
The only other way out was a fire escape down
by the stage.

01:48:52.110 --> 01:48:56.670
His heavy breathing and choked sobs weren’t
doing much to calm anyone down.

01:48:56.670 --> 01:48:58.309
Lewis checked his phone again.

01:48:58.309 --> 01:48:59.750
Jenny was still typing.

01:48:59.750 --> 01:49:03.510
Tommy kept repeating, “Something wrong with
the light, something wrong with the light,

01:49:03.510 --> 01:49:05.039
something wrong with the light...”

01:49:05.039 --> 01:49:09.360
The other students were starting to approach
him, all with a sense of guarded caution,

01:49:09.360 --> 01:49:14.669
given they still didn’t understand the situation
well enough to know for sure that Tommy wasn’t

01:49:14.669 --> 01:49:16.479
the dangerous one here.

01:49:16.479 --> 01:49:21.469
College could be an extremely stressful place;
it definitely wasn’t unheard of for people

01:49:21.469 --> 01:49:25.969
to snap under the pressure and do something
they wouldn’t normally do.

01:49:25.969 --> 01:49:27.630
“What’s going on, man?”

01:49:27.630 --> 01:49:31.949
Lewis said, walking briskly up to Tommy, worried
about Jenny’s safety if something really

01:49:31.949 --> 01:49:33.399
was happening out there.

01:49:33.399 --> 01:49:38.110
Tommy raised both hands in a gesture that
seemed halfway between pleading and a threat,

01:49:38.110 --> 01:49:39.440
shaking his head frantically.

01:49:39.440 --> 01:49:44.369
“Stay back, dude, you don’t wanna go out
there, something terrible is happening!”

01:49:44.369 --> 01:49:47.659
Professor Harbison approached, adjusting his
glasses and sneering.

01:49:47.659 --> 01:49:52.729
He didn’t take kindly to someone interrupting
his lecture with these ridiculous theatrics.

01:49:52.729 --> 01:49:57.270
An institute of learning was no place for
this kind of troublemaker.

01:49:57.270 --> 01:50:00.969
“I demand you leave this instant, young
man,” Harbison said.

01:50:00.969 --> 01:50:04.849
“If you don’t stop this foolish behavior,
I’ll have you written up.”

01:50:04.849 --> 01:50:06.099
Tommy didn’t budge.

01:50:06.099 --> 01:50:07.630
He just shook his head emphatically.

01:50:07.630 --> 01:50:10.280
“I’m not going anywhere,” he said.

01:50:10.280 --> 01:50:13.010
“If you go out there, you’re gonna die.

01:50:13.010 --> 01:50:15.849
I’m not letting anyone leave this damn building!”

01:50:15.849 --> 01:50:20.380
Before anyone could protest this unwilling
confinement, a terrible sound started to come

01:50:20.380 --> 01:50:21.869
from outside.

01:50:21.869 --> 01:50:25.079
Not screaming this time, just a voice.

01:50:25.079 --> 01:50:30.649
A warped, pained voice that seemed almost
to droop as it spoke, like a voice recorded

01:50:30.649 --> 01:50:34.489
on an old reel-to-reel tape recorder that’s
starting to degrade.

01:50:34.489 --> 01:50:38.090
“Help me,” it slurred.

01:50:38.090 --> 01:50:45.289
“Please, let me in, I feel so sick...”

01:50:45.289 --> 01:50:51.949
There was a terrible slopping noise, like
a thick, viscous liquid sluicing closer.

01:50:51.949 --> 01:50:54.649
Tommy looked down and screamed.

01:50:54.649 --> 01:51:00.189
Flesh-colored slime was slithering in underneath
the door and dripping over the top step of

01:51:00.189 --> 01:51:01.189
the gangway.

01:51:01.189 --> 01:51:03.400
Tommy stepped back, along with everyone else.

01:51:03.400 --> 01:51:09.439
It looked revolting and smelled even worse
- like a mixture of rotten meat and burning

01:51:09.439 --> 01:51:10.439
hair.

01:51:10.439 --> 01:51:16.090
That terrible drooping voice was still somehow
emanating from the slime.

01:51:16.090 --> 01:51:19.969
“Please... Please help me...

01:51:19.969 --> 01:51:22.880
Something went wrong...”

01:51:22.880 --> 01:51:26.969
Naturally, the other students began to scream
and run.

01:51:26.969 --> 01:51:30.550
Whatever that monster was, they needed to
get away from it, quickly.

01:51:30.550 --> 01:51:36.769
Only Lewis seemed to be rooted in place, the
terror and impossibility of it all just paralyzing

01:51:36.769 --> 01:51:37.769
him.

01:51:37.769 --> 01:51:42.070
He could only watch as the flesh-colored blob
creature seeped in under the door.

01:51:42.070 --> 01:51:46.909
Everyone else, including Professor Harbison
- who was moving faster than he had in years

01:51:46.909 --> 01:51:50.889
- made a mad dash for the fire exit down by
the stage.

01:51:50.889 --> 01:51:54.980
Tommy screamed for them to stay inside, but
it was already too late.

01:51:54.980 --> 01:51:59.690
People panicked and piled on top of each other
to get out, but the second they did, the worst

01:51:59.690 --> 01:52:01.530
of the screaming started.

01:52:01.530 --> 01:52:07.659
The most awful, agonized wails you’ve ever
heard, that tapered off in that same drooping

01:52:07.659 --> 01:52:11.210
gurgle as the blob slithering in underneath
the door.

01:52:11.210 --> 01:52:16.709
It was Lewis’ natural instinct to run down
and help, somehow, but instead, he felt Tommy’s

01:52:16.709 --> 01:52:18.380
white-knuckle grip on his shoulder.

01:52:18.380 --> 01:52:21.340
“I’m sorry,” Tommy said through sobs.

01:52:21.340 --> 01:52:23.810
“It’s too late for them.

01:52:23.810 --> 01:52:26.280
There’s nothing you can do.

01:52:26.280 --> 01:52:28.409
We just need to stay inside.”

01:52:28.409 --> 01:52:33.210
At that moment, Lewis received a text message
from Jenny, it read:

01:52:33.210 --> 01:52:37.969
“Lewis, if you’re still okay, you need
to read this and you need to believe me.

01:52:37.969 --> 01:52:39.389
Something terrible has happened.

01:52:39.389 --> 01:52:40.460
So many people are dead.

01:52:40.460 --> 01:52:44.169
Or at least, I think they’re dead, a lot
of them are still screaming.

01:52:44.169 --> 01:52:46.689
I think it has something to do with the sun.

01:52:46.689 --> 01:52:48.219
Stay out of the light.

01:52:48.219 --> 01:52:52.769
I’m in Administration Building C. I’m
safe, for now, but I’m hiding.

01:52:52.769 --> 01:52:54.269
Don’t come looking for me until nightfall.

01:52:54.269 --> 01:52:56.389
Please let me know if you’re okay.

01:52:56.389 --> 01:52:58.909
You can’t trust the sun.”

01:52:58.909 --> 01:53:04.610
Lewis felt a wave of relief and terror wash
over him: Jenny was okay, and that was great

01:53:04.610 --> 01:53:05.610
news.

01:53:05.610 --> 01:53:10.239
But it seemed like this nightmare, whatever
the hell it was, was affecting everyone out

01:53:10.239 --> 01:53:11.239
there.

01:53:11.239 --> 01:53:16.190
It was a force melting people down and turning
them into monsters.

01:53:16.190 --> 01:53:19.120
The screams from outside the door were getting
worse.

01:53:19.120 --> 01:53:25.289
All those people who had been, well, people
so recently were something entirely different

01:53:25.289 --> 01:53:26.289
now.

01:53:26.289 --> 01:53:29.159
And they were in terrible, terrible pain.

01:53:29.159 --> 01:53:33.920
Lewis and Tommy ran down to the fire escape
next to the stage and forced it shut, trying

01:53:33.920 --> 01:53:39.419
not to look at the tide of liquid flesh that
was roiling in the apparently hostile sun

01:53:39.419 --> 01:53:40.419
outside.

01:53:40.419 --> 01:53:43.889
They were lost now; Tommy and Lewis didn’t
want them coming back in.

01:53:43.889 --> 01:53:47.340
Lewis breathed a rattling sigh and hammered
out a reply to Jenny.

01:53:47.340 --> 01:53:49.750
“I’m safe in a lecture hall with Tommy
Lansdale.

01:53:49.750 --> 01:53:51.849
We’ll come to you as soon as we can.

01:53:51.849 --> 01:53:54.530
Don’t worry, it’s all going to be okay.”

01:53:54.530 --> 01:53:56.579
Lewis shoved his phone back into his pocket.

01:53:56.579 --> 01:53:58.619
Now isn’t the time to keep checking.

01:53:58.619 --> 01:54:00.489
He asked Tommy what he knew.

01:54:00.489 --> 01:54:02.320
How had he survived…

01:54:02.320 --> 01:54:03.980
Whatever all this was?

01:54:03.980 --> 01:54:07.769
Having some kind of knowledge about what they
were dealing with might have given them a

01:54:07.769 --> 01:54:09.729
better chance of surviving.

01:54:09.729 --> 01:54:14.459
Tommy had just finished up a seminar in a
nearby classroom and was heading over to the

01:54:14.459 --> 01:54:17.739
canteen to grab a Reuben while preparing for
his next class.

01:54:17.739 --> 01:54:22.909
He was lucky enough to be under a covered
walkway, bathed in the early morning shade,

01:54:22.909 --> 01:54:24.899
when something about the sun changed.

01:54:24.899 --> 01:54:31.469
It was hard to even describe - A certain shift
in the quality of the light, a kind of shimmer,

01:54:31.469 --> 01:54:32.650
almost.

01:54:32.650 --> 01:54:36.349
It didn’t affect him, but the people in
the courtyard a few feet away…

01:54:36.349 --> 01:54:39.519
He was shuddering as he told the next part
of the story.

01:54:39.519 --> 01:54:44.729
He swallowed hard over a lump in his throat,
staring off into the distance with a haunted

01:54:44.729 --> 01:54:48.340
look as the memories played over his mind.

01:54:48.340 --> 01:54:53.360
Tommy described the people in the courtyard,
the ones out of the shade who were first exposed…

01:54:53.360 --> 01:54:56.829
They just started screaming and falling apart.

01:54:56.829 --> 01:54:58.269
Losing form.

01:54:58.269 --> 01:54:59.269
Melting.

01:54:59.269 --> 01:55:02.440
Becoming awful piles of shapeless slop on
the ground.

01:55:02.440 --> 01:55:08.239
It was instantaneous, and all it seemed to
take was a fraction of a second of sun exposure

01:55:08.239 --> 01:55:11.570
for the whole process to take effect.

01:55:11.570 --> 01:55:15.289
Nothing was worth the risk of exposure to
something like that.

01:55:15.289 --> 01:55:16.289
Nothing.

01:55:16.289 --> 01:55:17.699
But Lewis disagreed.

01:55:17.699 --> 01:55:23.010
There was one thing worth risking his life
or the integrity of his body for: Jenny.

01:55:23.010 --> 01:55:26.639
They had to get to Administration Building
C as quickly as possible.

01:55:26.639 --> 01:55:31.980
And not just because of the new threat raging
outside, but because Building C already had

01:55:31.980 --> 01:55:33.469
a certain…

01:55:33.469 --> 01:55:34.469
Reputation.

01:55:34.469 --> 01:55:40.389
Rumor had it that a number of people had gone
missing in that building back in the 1970s.

01:55:40.389 --> 01:55:45.579
Since then, people had experienced strange,
inexplicable things.

01:55:45.579 --> 01:55:47.079
Odd chills.

01:55:47.079 --> 01:55:48.409
Strange noises.

01:55:48.409 --> 01:55:51.639
Creeping feelings of doom in certain areas.

01:55:51.639 --> 01:55:54.920
In short, it was the last place Lewis wanted
Jenny to be trapped.

01:55:54.920 --> 01:56:00.489
But neither he nor Tommy could help until
night fell, seeing as they wouldn’t be great

01:56:00.489 --> 01:56:03.189
rescuers in a semi-liquid state.

01:56:03.189 --> 01:56:08.729
They waited for ten torturous hours in that
lecture hall as day turned to night outside.

01:56:08.729 --> 01:56:14.469
In that time, they heard so many terrible
bubbling screams out there - Pain trying to

01:56:14.469 --> 01:56:16.189
escape a slimy prison.

01:56:16.189 --> 01:56:19.209
It was enough to drive you mad with fear.

01:56:19.209 --> 01:56:23.320
Both checked the news and their Twitter feeds
on their phones, hoping to find out if there

01:56:23.320 --> 01:56:26.260
was any more information on what was happening
out there.

01:56:26.260 --> 01:56:32.039
It seemed the worst had come to pass: This
insane phenomenon was unfolding everywhere.

01:56:32.039 --> 01:56:37.639
People across the world were suffering under
the transforming rays of the terrible new

01:56:37.639 --> 01:56:38.639
sun.

01:56:38.639 --> 01:56:43.059
Some group called the “SCP Foundation”
was releasing information about it, telling

01:56:43.059 --> 01:56:46.289
people to come towards their buildings at
night for safety.

01:56:46.289 --> 01:56:48.669
Lewis committed this fact to memory.

01:56:48.669 --> 01:56:53.149
When they’d collected Jenny, they’d head
straight to one of these buildings.

01:56:53.149 --> 01:56:57.539
Hopefully someone there could tell them what
the hell was going on here.

01:56:57.539 --> 01:57:00.599
Someone out there had to have answers about
all this.

01:57:00.599 --> 01:57:05.519
When it got dark outside, Lewis felt his phone
buzz in his pocket as he received another

01:57:05.519 --> 01:57:06.860
text from Jenny.

01:57:06.860 --> 01:57:08.309
This time, it was:

01:57:08.309 --> 01:57:09.770
“Lewis I’m scared.

01:57:09.770 --> 01:57:11.219
Please come quickly.

01:57:11.219 --> 01:57:12.219
I’m downstairs.”

01:57:12.219 --> 01:57:15.289
And with that, Lewis knew it was time to leave.

01:57:15.289 --> 01:57:19.010
He and Tommy steeled themselves, and when
they were sure it was dark out, they ventured

01:57:19.010 --> 01:57:21.639
forth from the lecture hall and onto the quad.

01:57:21.639 --> 01:57:25.369
There, they saw the aftermath of the horrors
that the sun had wrought.

01:57:25.369 --> 01:57:31.679
The grass was covered in more huge, flesh-colored
blobs, slithering across the grass like mutant

01:57:31.679 --> 01:57:32.909
slugs.

01:57:32.909 --> 01:57:36.699
Just the sight of them made Lewis and Tommy
feel nauseous, but they didn’t have time

01:57:36.699 --> 01:57:38.249
to stop and take a breather.

01:57:38.249 --> 01:57:42.389
If Jenny was in trouble, they needed to get
to Building C, fast.

01:57:42.389 --> 01:57:44.329
It looked even more frightening at night:
As one of the oldest buildings on campus,

01:57:44.329 --> 01:57:45.329
it could best be described as “dilapidated.”

01:57:45.329 --> 01:57:46.329
The facade was less fitting of a university
administrative building and more of a haunted

01:57:46.329 --> 01:57:47.329
house.

01:57:47.329 --> 01:57:50.199
It occurred to both Lewis and Tommy at that
moment that neither of them had ever actually

01:57:50.199 --> 01:57:54.840
been inside this building before, and what
seemed like the end of the world was certainly

01:57:54.840 --> 01:57:57.019
an interesting time to start.

01:57:57.019 --> 01:58:00.919
Lewis and Tommy entered the building and turned
on the lights in the lobby.

01:58:00.919 --> 01:58:06.139
They heard a voice at first, oddly toneless
and robotic, but soon realized it was a video

01:58:06.139 --> 01:58:10.239
playing on loop on the computer at the unmanned
reception desk.

01:58:10.239 --> 01:58:13.340
It was another message from the SCP Foundation.

01:58:13.340 --> 01:58:18.429
“This is a Public Service Announcement from
the SCP Foundation.

01:58:18.429 --> 01:58:24.929
We are commandeering all broadcast channels
and frequencies to deliver a vital message.

01:58:24.929 --> 01:58:27.679
Avoid sunlight at all costs.

01:58:27.679 --> 01:58:29.550
Cover your bodies thoroughly.

01:58:29.550 --> 01:58:36.229
And follow the instructions on the screen
to reach the nearest operational SCP Foundation

01:58:36.229 --> 01:58:37.380
containment site…”

01:58:37.380 --> 01:58:42.239
Neither liked the sound of the phrase “Containment
Site”, but they’d worry about the semantics

01:58:42.239 --> 01:58:43.239
later.

01:58:43.239 --> 01:58:48.550
Jenny had said she’d been hiding “downstairs”,
and there was no sign of her here on the first

01:58:48.550 --> 01:58:52.539
floor, so it stood to reason she was on some
kind of basement level.

01:58:52.539 --> 01:58:53.539
Strange.

01:58:53.539 --> 01:58:55.610
It seemed like all the doors here led up…

01:58:55.610 --> 01:58:56.900
Except one.

01:58:56.900 --> 01:59:02.309
A door that looked like, until very recently,
it had been bolted to the wall.

01:59:02.309 --> 01:59:05.260
“Well…” Lewis said, trying to mask his
fear.

01:59:05.260 --> 01:59:07.289
“I guess it’s now or never.”

01:59:07.289 --> 01:59:12.849
Tommy opened his mouth to answer, but before
a single word could pass his lips, a huge,

01:59:12.849 --> 01:59:17.289
fleshy tendril shot through the front door
of the building and wrapped around his throat.

01:59:17.289 --> 01:59:22.090
He managed only a choked gasp as it dragged
him back towards the door.

01:59:22.090 --> 01:59:26.320
On the other side of the threshold, a mountain
of flesh eagerly waited.

01:59:26.320 --> 01:59:31.590
A gibbering nightmare of half-formed faces
screamed and wailed in the terrible fleshy

01:59:31.590 --> 01:59:32.590
form.

01:59:32.590 --> 01:59:35.889
It happened so quickly that Lewis couldn’t
even hope to save him.

01:59:35.889 --> 01:59:42.479
He was gone, subsumed by the blob, his face
disappearing into the flesh before Lewis could

01:59:42.479 --> 01:59:43.479
even scream.

01:59:43.479 --> 01:59:47.079
But there was no time to stop and process
the shock.

01:59:47.079 --> 01:59:51.270
Another several tendrils were already reaching
for him; he could practically see it happening

01:59:51.270 --> 01:59:52.790
in slow motion.

01:59:52.790 --> 01:59:53.979
Pulsing.

01:59:53.979 --> 01:59:55.150
Grasping for him.

01:59:55.150 --> 01:59:59.499
Perhaps it was only his will to see Jenny
again that gave him the strength to do so,

01:59:59.499 --> 02:00:05.219
but he dodged the tendrils by a fraction of
an inch and dove towards the door - the same

02:00:05.219 --> 02:00:08.649
door he assumed Jenny had escaped into hours
earlier.

02:00:08.649 --> 02:00:13.800
He threw open the door and disappeared behind
it, slamming it in place and holding it fast.

02:00:13.800 --> 02:00:18.079
He could feel the tendrils smashing up against
it, hammering away.

02:00:18.079 --> 02:00:22.919
Lewis heard all those terrible, deranged voices
screaming for him on the other side.

02:00:22.919 --> 02:00:28.530
He was holding it for ten solid minutes before
the creature finally gave up and slithered

02:00:28.530 --> 02:00:29.530
away.

02:00:29.530 --> 02:00:33.340
The second the silence fell, Lewis’ phone
buzzed in his pocket again.

02:00:33.340 --> 02:00:35.959
He fished it out and saw another message from
Jenny.

02:00:35.959 --> 02:00:36.969
It read:

02:00:36.969 --> 02:00:37.979
“Please come.

02:00:37.979 --> 02:00:38.979
Afraid.

02:00:38.979 --> 02:00:42.969
So dark here, so dark here, so dark here,
so dark here, so dark here…”

02:00:42.969 --> 02:00:47.320
And it was at that moment that Lewis began
to take in his new surroundings.

02:00:47.320 --> 02:00:53.469
He was standing in a stairwell, one that seemed
much older and more dilapidated than even

02:00:53.469 --> 02:00:54.670
the rest of the building.

02:00:54.670 --> 02:01:01.960
But most notable of all was the fact that
he had never, ever seen a place so dark.

02:01:01.960 --> 02:01:03.290
Impossibly dark.

02:01:03.290 --> 02:01:08.829
Not “dark” as in the absence of light,
but dark as an almost physical presence, choking

02:01:08.829 --> 02:01:10.559
out the light.

02:01:10.559 --> 02:01:15.139
Even with his phone on full power, the light
didn’t penetrate much of the black.

02:01:15.139 --> 02:01:16.949
Was Jenny all the way down here?

02:01:16.949 --> 02:01:22.071
Lewis was afraid, but he could only imagine
how Jenny was feeling down there in the depths.

02:01:22.071 --> 02:01:27.619
He took a deep breath and started descending
down into the darkness, using his phone camera

02:01:27.619 --> 02:01:29.889
to light what little of the way was possible.

02:01:29.889 --> 02:01:36.099
The deeper he got, the more he started to
feel a strange, intangible dread beginning

02:01:36.099 --> 02:01:40.419
to creep in, like an invisible hand compressing
his chest.

02:01:40.419 --> 02:01:45.070
As he got deeper, he noticed a sound: Quiet
weeping.

02:01:45.070 --> 02:01:49.699
An unmistakably female voice, somewhere down
below.

02:01:49.699 --> 02:01:54.010
It sounded too young to be Jenny, but then
again, Lewis had never actually heard her

02:01:54.010 --> 02:01:55.400
cry before.

02:01:55.400 --> 02:01:59.189
Whatever the case, he needed to get down there
and remedy the situation.

02:01:59.189 --> 02:02:04.050
He forged on, further and further into the
dark, even as his terrible sense of dread

02:02:04.050 --> 02:02:05.050
increased.

02:02:05.050 --> 02:02:09.510
He couldn’t help but notice that no matter
how far down he went, it always sounded like

02:02:09.510 --> 02:02:11.940
the crying was the same distance away.

02:02:11.940 --> 02:02:14.999
It unnerved him, but Jenny had to be down
there.

02:02:14.999 --> 02:02:16.260
She had to be.

02:02:16.260 --> 02:02:20.439
And even if he gave up on her, it’s not
like he could just go back up and leave.

02:02:20.439 --> 02:02:24.479
There were monsters out there - like the monster
that’d gotten Tommy.

02:02:24.479 --> 02:02:28.019
Even down here in the darkness, he was safer
than up there.

02:02:28.019 --> 02:02:30.380
Or at least, that’s what he thought.

02:02:30.380 --> 02:02:33.070
He felt something strange under his foot and
looked down.

02:02:33.070 --> 02:02:38.829
It was an old piece of torn-up orange fabric,
with “D-9884” written on it.

02:02:38.829 --> 02:02:40.749
The phone buzzed again.

02:02:40.749 --> 02:02:42.530
A new message from Jenny.

02:02:42.530 --> 02:02:44.929
This time it read, “I can see you.

02:02:44.929 --> 02:02:46.309
Can you see me?”

02:02:46.309 --> 02:02:47.309
Lewis looked around.

02:02:47.309 --> 02:02:48.789
He couldn’t see anything.

02:02:48.789 --> 02:02:51.590
He tried desperately calling Jenny again.

02:02:51.590 --> 02:02:54.260
He yelled her name until his voice went hoarse.

02:02:54.260 --> 02:02:55.789
No reply.

02:02:55.789 --> 02:02:59.409
Just that same soft weeping so far below.

02:02:59.409 --> 02:03:01.869
Maybe she was on the flight below him.

02:03:01.869 --> 02:03:03.070
She must have been close.

02:03:03.070 --> 02:03:07.630
He doubled his speed, vaulting down the stairs
to the next level, when he noticed something

02:03:07.630 --> 02:03:08.630
on the ground.

02:03:08.630 --> 02:03:14.380
A Smart Phone - One that, over so many study
dates, lunches, and outings to the movies,

02:03:14.380 --> 02:03:16.239
he’d learned to recognize.

02:03:16.239 --> 02:03:17.239
It was Jenny’s.

02:03:17.239 --> 02:03:22.630
He picked it up, studying it, only to see
that it was dead, the screen cracked and the

02:03:22.630 --> 02:03:24.059
battery lifeless.

02:03:24.059 --> 02:03:26.670
His own phone gave another buzz.

02:03:26.670 --> 02:03:28.059
A text from Jenny.

02:03:28.059 --> 02:03:30.150
It read, “Look down.”

02:03:30.150 --> 02:03:34.409
And, against all rational thought, that’s
exactly what he did.

02:03:34.409 --> 02:03:37.389
There was something at the bottom of the stairs.

02:03:37.389 --> 02:03:42.610
A strange, pale face, somehow illuminated
in the darkness.

02:03:42.610 --> 02:03:45.239
At first, he thought it could have been her,
and ran towards it.

02:03:45.239 --> 02:03:50.209
By the time he realized he was wrong, and
he was staring into the blank eyes of a face

02:03:50.209 --> 02:03:54.300
without a mouth, inches from his own face,
it was already too late.

02:03:54.300 --> 02:03:58.739
Lewis let out a scream, but there was nobody
there to hear him.

02:03:58.739 --> 02:04:03.590
But on the bright side, at least he never
would see that sunlight again…

02:04:03.590 --> 02:04:05.659
The sun’s gone bad!

02:04:05.659 --> 02:04:07.249
People and animals are melting everywhere!

02:04:07.249 --> 02:04:10.309
The world is coming to an end and there’s
nothing I can do about it!

02:04:10.309 --> 02:04:11.539
Will I be able to find food?

02:04:11.539 --> 02:04:15.729
Will I be able to defeat or avoid the horrific
flesh monsters all around me, or the desperate

02:04:15.729 --> 02:04:17.969
and hungry survivors left in this terrible
new world?

02:04:17.969 --> 02:04:20.369
Keep watching and find out.

02:04:20.369 --> 02:04:24.400
Can I survive 100 days in SCP-001 - When Day
Breaks!?

02:04:24.400 --> 02:04:26.780
Hey folks, it’s ya boy, Kyle.

02:04:26.780 --> 02:04:31.260
You probably know me more for gaming videos
than post-apocalyptic vlogs, but hey, I’m

02:04:31.260 --> 02:04:35.400
a versatile guy, and I think I might go insane
from the fear if I don’t talk to somebody

02:04:35.400 --> 02:04:36.400
about all this craziness.

02:04:36.400 --> 02:04:40.429
If you’re alive and seeing this right now,
well, congratulations, you’re probably doing

02:04:40.429 --> 02:04:44.940
a lot better than most people here, if you
can call them “People” now.

02:04:44.940 --> 02:04:48.610
But if you’re seeing this a few years in
the future, like, I don’t know, you woke

02:04:48.610 --> 02:04:52.639
up from a ten-year coma like Rick from The
Walking Dead and you’re wondering what the

02:04:52.639 --> 02:04:54.340
hell happened to Planet Earth…

02:04:54.340 --> 02:04:58.519
This video is probably gonna answer a lot
of your questions.

02:04:58.519 --> 02:05:01.340
First thing’s first, whatever you do, you’ve
gotta stay away from the sun.

02:05:01.340 --> 02:05:06.110
It touches you even for a second, you’re
dead, or worse.

02:05:06.110 --> 02:05:08.479
Welcome to Day One of the End of the World.

02:05:08.479 --> 02:05:12.229
For all of you who are still in a solid state
of matter, you’re probably wondering how

02:05:12.229 --> 02:05:13.650
I’m still alive, too.

02:05:13.650 --> 02:05:17.939
Chances are, it’s for the exact same reason
you are: Sheer, dumb luck.

02:05:17.939 --> 02:05:22.119
I was down here in my gaming basement when
day broke, just level grinding when my TV

02:05:22.119 --> 02:05:26.530
got taken over by those SCP Foundation people,
telling us that the sun’s gone evil for

02:05:26.530 --> 02:05:28.699
whatever reason and now we’ve all gotta
stay inside.

02:05:28.699 --> 02:05:33.099
Hell, if I was up there making myself a sandwich
or grabbing another can of Mountain Dew, I’d

02:05:33.099 --> 02:05:34.969
be a freaking puddle right now.

02:05:34.969 --> 02:05:38.780
It’s funny, my mom always told me spending
all day indoors was bad for me.

02:05:38.780 --> 02:05:51.219
I’ll have to mention that to her, if she’s
alive…

02:05:51.219 --> 02:06:05.070
Okay, okay, back to business.

02:06:05.070 --> 02:06:15.019
I’ve been spending the last several hours
just waiting for nightfall outside.

02:06:15.019 --> 02:06:19.070
Against all odds, the internet and the power
grid haven’t gone down yet - Guess whatever’s

02:06:19.070 --> 02:06:22.610
wrong with the sun only affects people and
animals, not objects.

02:06:22.610 --> 02:06:25.170
Thank heaven for small mercies, right?

02:06:25.170 --> 02:06:28.630
People on Twitter have been live-posting their
situations out there, sharing advice on how

02:06:28.630 --> 02:06:32.260
we might all be able to stay out of the sun
and survive this whole crazy thing.

02:06:32.260 --> 02:06:37.619
And hey, unless they’re dead or full of
hot air, maybe those “SCP Foundation”

02:06:37.619 --> 02:06:39.959
people know something about what’s going
on here.

02:06:39.959 --> 02:06:44.399
If we really can get to their buildings, maybe
we can figure out how to reverse all this

02:06:44.399 --> 02:06:45.399
mess.

02:06:45.399 --> 02:06:46.399
Maybe.

02:06:46.399 --> 02:06:48.399
For now, I’m just gonna focus on staying
alive.

02:06:48.399 --> 02:08:30.869
Hopefully night hits soon, I really need 

02:08:30.869 --> 02:08:32.550
the bathroom…

02:08:32.550 --> 02:08:39.559
Hey folks, I’m back, thankfully.

02:08:39.559 --> 02:08:41.039
Welcome to Day Five.

02:08:41.039 --> 02:08:45.579
We’ve still got electricity, thankfully,
hence why you can still see this.

02:08:45.579 --> 02:08:47.489
I haven’t heard anything from my family.

02:08:47.489 --> 02:08:50.139
I don’t wanna assume the worst, but…

02:08:50.139 --> 02:08:53.300
It’s probably just best not to think about
it.

02:08:53.300 --> 02:08:56.479
I’ve been heading up and downstairs to grab
more food at night.

02:08:56.479 --> 02:08:59.630
You’re probably wondering, “But Kyle,
why don’t you bring it all downstairs to

02:08:59.630 --> 02:09:00.709
save going up altogether?”

02:09:00.709 --> 02:09:05.829
To which I’d say, I don’t have a fridge
downstairs, smart-ass.

02:09:05.829 --> 02:09:10.309
But I’m starting to realize food is gonna
be a real issue here.

02:09:10.309 --> 02:09:15.510
It’s kinda stupid, now I think about it
- In all the zombie movies and TV shows I

02:09:15.510 --> 02:09:17.929
used to watch, it was all bullets and baseball
bats.

02:09:17.929 --> 02:09:22.239
Killing your way through all those undead
freaks and worrying about the rest later.

02:09:22.239 --> 02:09:26.919
Guess they don’t want you to think about
how you’re only ever a couple of weeks away

02:09:26.919 --> 02:09:31.760
from starvation, kinda ruins the badass post-apocalypse
power fantasy.

02:09:31.760 --> 02:09:36.280
I only have a couple days worth of food left
here, and after that, I’m gonna need to

02:09:36.280 --> 02:09:38.459
go out and search for more.

02:09:38.459 --> 02:09:40.969
Or I’m gonna need to relocate.

02:09:40.969 --> 02:09:43.570
I don’t feel comfortable here anymore, y’know?

02:09:43.570 --> 02:09:47.570
Early on, I thought when you got exposed to
the sunlight, it just killed you.

02:09:47.570 --> 02:09:49.260
But no, it’s worse.

02:09:49.260 --> 02:09:52.699
You keep living, you’re just changed into
one of those…

02:09:52.699 --> 02:09:53.699
Things.

02:09:53.699 --> 02:09:58.379
These last few days, I’ve looked out the
windows when I’ve come up at night for food.

02:09:58.379 --> 02:10:03.429
I see them sometimes, slithering in the yard
or down the street, these things that used

02:10:03.429 --> 02:10:04.429
to be people.

02:10:04.429 --> 02:10:08.599
I wonder if they’re people that I knew once,
before all this, then I try to shove that

02:10:08.599 --> 02:10:10.070
thought out of my mind.

02:10:10.070 --> 02:10:13.550
Freaking myself out about all this doesn’t
help, I know that much.

02:10:13.550 --> 02:10:19.149
I just keep thinking about how they move,
with this weird kind of… purpose?

02:10:19.149 --> 02:10:23.119
Like they’re searching for something, but
what could they be searching for?

02:10:23.119 --> 02:10:27.519
I’m just gonna go and get more food.

02:10:27.519 --> 02:10:29.539
We’ll speak again soon.

02:10:29.539 --> 02:10:32.050
Stay safe, whoever you are.

02:10:32.050 --> 02:10:34.059
Okay, okay, I’m alive.

02:10:34.059 --> 02:10:36.070
That’s enough, isn’t it?

02:10:36.070 --> 02:10:41.099
And if you’re watching this, I assume you’re
alive too.

02:10:41.099 --> 02:10:43.709
Congratulations, Welcome to day 27.

02:10:43.709 --> 02:10:50.829
Sorry that I haven’t been in contact for
so long.

02:10:50.829 --> 02:10:55.829
As you can see, I’m not at home anymore.

02:10:55.829 --> 02:11:00.189
You couldn’t pay me to go back there - Ha.

02:11:00.189 --> 02:11:02.709
Not that money is worth anything anymore.

02:11:02.709 --> 02:11:09.559
A lot’s happened since I last made one of
these, and I wish I could tell you any of

02:11:09.559 --> 02:11:10.559
it was good.

02:11:10.559 --> 02:11:16.389
Hell, I wish I could forget it all, but the
things I’ve seen and heard…

02:11:16.389 --> 02:11:21.360
I don’t think they’re ever gonna leave
my head, no matter what I do.

02:11:21.360 --> 02:11:25.489
It started a few days after my last video.

02:11:25.489 --> 02:11:31.219
When I ran out of food, just like I knew I
would, I started sneaking out at night to

02:11:31.219 --> 02:11:34.249
try to get my hands on whatever was left.

02:11:34.249 --> 02:11:36.610
There’d been some looting, but not much.

02:11:36.610 --> 02:11:40.750
Most of the perishables had already rotted
away by the time I got there, so when I run

02:11:40.750 --> 02:11:45.840
out of canned peaches and mandarins I’ve
got scurvy to look forward to.

02:11:45.840 --> 02:11:50.619
But hey, we’ll cross that bridge when we
get to it, right?

02:11:50.619 --> 02:11:56.599
I thought about making another entry now and
then, but I always found a reason to put it

02:11:56.599 --> 02:11:57.599
off.

02:11:57.599 --> 02:12:02.480
It’s remarkable how all your other priorities
fall away when you’re thinking about where

02:12:02.480 --> 02:12:07.900
your next meal is coming from, it just kind
of puts everything in perspective.

02:12:07.900 --> 02:12:16.639
Of course, during my travels, I saw more of
those freaks slithering around.

02:12:16.639 --> 02:12:19.889
Sorry, sorry, I know I shouldn’t call them
that.

02:12:19.889 --> 02:12:23.739
It’s kind of a coping mechanism, you know?

02:12:23.739 --> 02:12:31.320
It all gets a lot harder when you have to
think of them as…

02:12:31.320 --> 02:12:32.320
Ex-people.

02:12:32.320 --> 02:12:38.209
That’s another thing all those goofy zombie
shows got wrong: It’s a lot harder to separate

02:12:38.209 --> 02:12:43.780
what they were from what they are now, especially
when you know these were your friends, your

02:12:43.780 --> 02:12:44.780
neighbors, your…

02:12:44.780 --> 02:12:47.699
Well, I can’t avoid talking about it forever,
can I?

02:12:47.699 --> 02:12:52.159
I stuffed my backpack with whatever I could
grab and left my home two nights ago.

02:12:52.159 --> 02:12:56.269
It wasn’t just because I was going stir
crazy back there, though I admit, that didn’t

02:12:56.269 --> 02:12:57.269
exactly help.

02:12:57.269 --> 02:12:59.349
It was what happened there.

02:12:59.349 --> 02:13:04.789
I’d just come back from a food run, put
most of it in the fridge, then retired back

02:13:04.789 --> 02:13:09.409
down into the basement to enjoy a late-night
snack and do a little gaming to keep myself

02:13:09.409 --> 02:13:10.409
sane.

02:13:10.409 --> 02:13:14.719
I’d been doing everything I could to reverse
my circadian rhythms and sleep during the

02:13:14.719 --> 02:13:18.889
day, just so I could be fully operational
during the twelve-hour period that going outside

02:13:18.889 --> 02:13:19.889
wouldn’t melt me.

02:13:19.889 --> 02:13:24.030
But just like all those stories they told
us when we were kids: There are monsters out

02:13:24.030 --> 02:13:25.710
there at night, and they’re looking for
us.

02:13:25.710 --> 02:13:27.289
When I first heard the sound, I wondered if
it was something in-game, or maybe dripping

02:13:27.289 --> 02:13:28.320
from a leaky pipe.

02:13:28.320 --> 02:13:33.959
But no, it was too close to be fake and too
viscous to be water.

02:13:33.959 --> 02:13:39.969
That’s when I looked at the door and saw
this awful pink slime slithering in underneath

02:13:39.969 --> 02:13:40.969
my door.

02:13:40.969 --> 02:13:45.239
It was one of those things, those Ex-People,
trying to get in.

02:13:45.239 --> 02:13:49.110
That’d be bad enough, but then, it started
talking to me…

02:13:49.110 --> 02:13:50.110
“Kyle…

02:13:50.110 --> 02:13:51.110
My darling…

02:13:51.110 --> 02:13:53.650
Why are you all cooped up down here?

02:13:53.650 --> 02:13:55.030
It isn’t healthy.

02:13:55.030 --> 02:13:59.610
You ought to come out side, sweetie, get some
sun…”

02:13:59.610 --> 02:14:01.710
It was my mom.

02:14:01.710 --> 02:14:05.229
Well, it used to be.

02:14:05.229 --> 02:14:09.699
Guess she wanted to come over and visit me.

02:14:09.699 --> 02:14:16.900
Needless to say, I got out of there, and I’ve
got no intention of going back.

02:14:16.900 --> 02:14:19.400
That place is dead to me now.

02:14:19.400 --> 02:14:24.679
I don’t ever want to think about that voice
ever again, both her and so…

02:14:24.679 --> 02:14:25.679
Not her.

02:14:25.679 --> 02:14:30.940
So now, I’m on the move, guess I’ll speak
to you again when I stop.

02:14:30.940 --> 02:14:32.659
Stay safe out there.

02:14:32.659 --> 02:14:33.659
Hello, again.

02:14:33.659 --> 02:14:37.769
Guess I lied when I told you I’d record
another one of these when I stopped, cause

02:14:37.769 --> 02:14:39.479
I’ve been on the run for weeks now.

02:14:39.479 --> 02:14:41.929
Let’s give it up for Day 43.

02:14:41.929 --> 02:14:47.209
If you’ve lived this long, you really ought
to be proud of yourself.

02:14:47.209 --> 02:14:52.209
I’ve seen thousands of those slimy Ex-People,
and there are probably millions more out there

02:14:52.209 --> 02:14:55.880
- Hell, maybe even billions, if we’re being
honest with ourselves here.

02:14:55.880 --> 02:14:58.889
Am I just talking into the void here?

02:14:58.889 --> 02:15:03.939
Is there even anyone else out there who’s
human enough to watch this stuff?

02:15:03.939 --> 02:15:06.130
Maybe I just need to keep thinking about posterity.

02:15:06.130 --> 02:15:10.860
On the off-chance that the world ever gets
better, and we reach some time when children

02:15:10.860 --> 02:15:15.300
are born again and all this fades from human
memory, you’ll still have these stupid,

02:15:15.300 --> 02:15:20.829
pointless little videos to remember how awful
all this was.

02:15:20.829 --> 02:15:32.959
That way, at least I can make myself believe
this all had some kind of point.

02:15:32.959 --> 02:15:34.300
So, what’s happened?

02:15:34.300 --> 02:15:36.090
With me, not much.

02:15:36.090 --> 02:15:41.440
Still moving at night, surviving, hiding in
closets and underground parking complexes

02:15:41.440 --> 02:15:42.440
during daylight.

02:15:42.440 --> 02:15:47.209
I’m down to my last few cans, so I’m hoping
to hit a supermarket soon.

02:15:47.209 --> 02:15:52.099
What a ridiculous way to go: Starving in this
new world, with so many new, interesting ways

02:15:52.099 --> 02:15:53.099
to die.

02:15:53.099 --> 02:15:57.749
With the Ex-People, things have been a little
more eventful.

02:15:57.749 --> 02:16:03.280
There used to be one blob to a person, but
they’ve started…

02:16:03.280 --> 02:16:04.280
Joining up?

02:16:04.280 --> 02:16:05.280
That’s the best way I can put it.

02:16:05.280 --> 02:16:12.369
Things that used to be people and animals
are starting to melt together, getting bigger

02:16:12.369 --> 02:16:13.369
and bigger.

02:16:13.369 --> 02:16:19.380
They’ve never been aggressive, but I think
it’s best to stay out of their way.

02:16:19.380 --> 02:16:26.769
Whatever all this is about, I’m streetwise
enough now to know it can’t be anything

02:16:26.769 --> 02:16:28.059
good.

02:16:28.059 --> 02:16:43.059
I’ll just keep moving, and I hope you can
do the same, whoever the hell you are.

02:16:43.059 --> 02:16:48.729
Hopefully, the next time I check in with you,
it’s with better news than this.

02:16:48.729 --> 02:16:54.639
Note to self, in the future, don’t even
dare to think “How could things get any

02:16:54.639 --> 02:16:55.639
worse?”

02:16:55.639 --> 02:17:01.590
Because if I’ve learned anything since this
whole nightmare started, that is never a rhetorical

02:17:01.590 --> 02:17:02.590
question.

02:17:02.590 --> 02:17:03.889
Welcome to Day 64.

02:17:03.889 --> 02:17:06.540
If you’re still alive and watching this,
I’m so sorry.

02:17:06.540 --> 02:17:08.670
So, I’ve got good news and bad news.

02:17:08.670 --> 02:17:12.659
I’ll give you the good news first: I’ve
seen people, real, honest to god people, since

02:17:12.659 --> 02:17:14.979
the last time I recorded one of these.

02:17:14.979 --> 02:17:17.389
And the bad news?

02:17:17.389 --> 02:17:26.029
Last time I saw them, they were being dragged
out into the light, kicking and screaming,

02:17:26.029 --> 02:17:32.929
in the tendrils of one of those horrible flesh
monsters I was telling you about last time.

02:17:32.929 --> 02:17:39.130
They’ve gotten a lot bigger now, and when
I told you they weren’t aggressive, well,

02:17:39.130 --> 02:17:41.609
I spoke a little too soon.

02:17:41.609 --> 02:17:46.149
I can’t just sleep during the day like I
used to.

02:17:46.149 --> 02:17:51.519
These monsters - and that’s what they are
now, they’re monsters, not people anymore

02:17:51.519 --> 02:17:53.170
- they patrol, they hunt.

02:17:53.170 --> 02:17:57.800
They actively enter buildings, searching for
hiding places, searching for people they can

02:17:57.800 --> 02:17:59.909
drag out into the light.

02:17:59.909 --> 02:18:06.210
I’ve seen it with my own two eyes - The
second they’re out, they just start melting

02:18:06.210 --> 02:18:11.609
and fusing with the mass, making it even bigger,
adding another voice to the chorus.

02:18:11.609 --> 02:18:18.050
And I hate myself, cause every time I’ve
seen it happen, all I can think is, “Thank

02:18:18.050 --> 02:18:19.679
god that’s not me.”

02:18:19.679 --> 02:18:28.559
I wish I could do something to help, to save
them, but that’s not the world we live in.

02:18:28.559 --> 02:18:31.369
The second they touch the light, it’s already
over.

02:18:31.369 --> 02:18:38.269
I wouldn’t be helping anyone by adding my
flesh to one of those things.

02:18:38.269 --> 02:18:44.040
I don’t want them using my body to get other
people.

02:18:44.040 --> 02:18:52.979
There’s only one thing I can do now: Keep
moving at night, stay hidden, get away from

02:18:52.979 --> 02:18:54.030
population centers.

02:18:54.030 --> 02:19:02.010
I’ve realized where I need to go now: I’ve
still got a distant memory of those broadcasts

02:19:02.010 --> 02:19:13.670
in the early days of the event.

02:19:13.670 --> 02:19:15.619
The SCP Foundation.

02:19:15.619 --> 02:19:25.699
I noted down coordinates to the nearest facility
they had on the books - And if I’m honest,

02:19:25.699 --> 02:19:37.309
“nearest” is only a relative term, cause
at this rate, it’s gonna take me an eternity

02:19:37.309 --> 02:19:38.479
to get there.

02:19:38.479 --> 02:19:44.050
But it’ll be worth it in the end, when I
get there.

02:19:44.050 --> 02:19:49.090
It’ll all be worth it if I can at least
get some answers, at least know why the world

02:19:49.090 --> 02:19:50.489
turned into this Hell.

02:19:50.489 --> 02:19:56.440
Those SCP folks seemed better prepared for
this than anyone, so even if they can’t

02:19:56.440 --> 02:20:00.360
fix this, they’ve at least got to have answers,
right?

02:20:00.360 --> 02:20:01.990
Somebody needs to have answers.

02:20:01.990 --> 02:20:03.950
I really want to believe that.

02:20:03.950 --> 02:20:07.420
When the sun goes down, I’ll start moving
again.

02:20:07.420 --> 02:20:10.359
If you’re watching this, wish me luck.

02:20:10.359 --> 02:20:13.119
I don’t have much food left.

02:20:13.119 --> 02:20:15.659
I’ll do what I can.

02:20:15.659 --> 02:20:24.050
Yeah, I realize I’m not looking great right
now, but trust me, you should see the other

02:20:24.050 --> 02:20:25.050
guy.

02:20:25.050 --> 02:20:26.050
Welcome to day 86.

02:20:26.050 --> 02:20:33.119
Never thought I’d make it this far, but
life’s just full of surprises.

02:20:33.119 --> 02:20:36.910
Before you ask - and I mean, why would you
ask?

02:20:36.910 --> 02:20:44.069
It’s not like I can hear you - it wasn’t
one of the monsters that did this to me, it

02:20:44.069 --> 02:20:45.630
was another person, just like me.

02:20:45.630 --> 02:20:46.630
Desperate, hungry, afraid.

02:20:46.630 --> 02:20:50.920
The one difference between me and them was
the fact they had a handgun, and I didn’t.

02:20:50.920 --> 02:20:54.859
They asked for all the food I had, and when
I wasn’t exactly forthcoming, they decided

02:20:54.859 --> 02:20:58.260
to shoot me, and steal the last of my food
while I lay bleeding on the ground.

02:20:58.260 --> 02:20:59.930
Well, that’s not entirely fair.

02:20:59.930 --> 02:21:06.609
They did leave me with one protein bar, which
I had to cave and eat a couple of days ago.

02:21:06.609 --> 02:21:11.940
Since then, I’ve just been foraging what
little food I can from plants along the way

02:21:11.940 --> 02:21:13.550
during my nighttime walks.

02:21:13.550 --> 02:21:17.950
But it isn’t much, and my wound is giving
me grief.

02:21:17.950 --> 02:21:26.200
I sure hope they’ve got doctors at this
SCP Foundation, or otherwise, I may be even

02:21:26.200 --> 02:21:27.790
more out of luck than I thought.

02:21:27.790 --> 02:21:34.330
Here’s the good news for you, since I know
how much you love that.

02:21:34.330 --> 02:21:37.060
I’m not far off of the Foundation site now.

02:21:37.060 --> 02:21:41.990
Even in this state, I’m probably only a
couple of weeks away.

02:21:41.990 --> 02:21:48.040
I think maybe I can will myself to live that
long, at least.

02:21:48.040 --> 02:21:53.119
If I can get some answers just before I die,
then I can die happy.

02:21:53.119 --> 02:21:56.820
And sometimes, folks, that’s all you can
ask for.

02:21:56.820 --> 02:21:58.060
Final stretch.

02:21:58.060 --> 02:22:03.170
Let’s hope I see you again on the other
end.

02:22:03.170 --> 02:22:04.170
Stay safe…

02:22:04.170 --> 02:22:05.170
I’m here!

02:22:05.170 --> 02:22:06.170
I’m here!

02:22:06.170 --> 02:22:08.310
The SCP Foundation on Day 100!

02:22:08.310 --> 02:22:09.310
But…

02:22:09.310 --> 02:22:11.230
I don’t understand.

02:22:11.230 --> 02:22:13.430
Where is everybody?

02:22:13.430 --> 02:22:14.430
Hello?

02:22:14.430 --> 02:22:15.430
Is anybody there?

02:22:15.430 --> 02:22:16.430
God damn it, why is nobody here?

02:22:16.430 --> 02:22:17.430
I don’t understand.

02:22:17.430 --> 02:22:18.430
They were meant to have the answers.

02:22:18.430 --> 02:22:24.130
They were meant to know what was going on
here, but they’re all gone too.

02:22:24.130 --> 02:22:28.300
It isn’t fair, it isn’t fair.

02:22:28.300 --> 02:22:30.380
Is this it?

02:22:30.380 --> 02:22:31.619
Is it all just over?

02:22:31.619 --> 02:22:34.980
The end of the freaking world as we know it?

02:22:34.980 --> 02:22:38.720
It isn’t fair, it isn’t fair, it isn’t
fair…

02:22:38.720 --> 02:22:39.720
Wait…

02:22:39.720 --> 02:22:40.720
Are those footsteps?

02:22:40.720 --> 02:22:43.710
Hello? Yes, hello, I’m over here!

02:22:43.710 --> 02:22:44.710
Who’s there?

02:22:44.710 --> 02:22:48.070
Oh my god, what the hell is that thing!?

02:22:48.070 --> 02:22:52.970
No, no, get away from me, oh god!

02:22:52.970 --> 02:22:53.970
“Disgusting…”

02:22:53.970 --> 02:23:01.040
Dr. Jack Bright was having the second-worst
day of his life, and things weren’t about

02:23:01.040 --> 02:23:02.180
to get better.

02:23:02.180 --> 02:23:07.100
He ran down the main hall of Site-19, medallion
jangling against his chest.

02:23:07.100 --> 02:23:12.850
All around him, sirens blared and warning
lights flashed a stark, threatening red.

02:23:12.850 --> 02:23:16.010
He cursed and muttered under his breath.

02:23:16.010 --> 02:23:21.300
Just an hour ago, things had been under control,
neat and orderly, standard running operations,

02:23:21.300 --> 02:23:28.040
and now they were staring down the barrel
of both a Broken Masquerade Scenario and a

02:23:28.040 --> 02:23:30.440
potential XK Class End of the World Scenario.

02:23:30.440 --> 02:23:36.010
And on a more personal level, four of the
meanest, nastiest, and deadliest anomalies

02:23:36.010 --> 02:23:37.780
were on his tail.

02:23:37.780 --> 02:23:42.401
They’d breached containment, compromised
site security, and now they were out for revenge.

02:23:42.401 --> 02:23:43.401
What was happening?

02:23:43.401 --> 02:23:49.210
How had this group of anomalies gotten out,
and why were they all after Dr. Bright?

02:23:49.210 --> 02:23:55.520
And most importantly of all, how could everything
have gone to hell in a handbasket so fast?

02:23:55.520 --> 02:23:57.560
Let’s rewind the clock and see.

02:23:57.560 --> 02:24:00.899
The morning had begun in the break room.

02:24:00.899 --> 02:24:05.240
Dr. Bright was enjoying a nice cup of hot
coffee and a danish, watching the news.

02:24:05.240 --> 02:24:07.819
It was, by all accounts, an incredibly boring
day.

02:24:07.819 --> 02:24:13.260
The reports were economic figures, a few dry
statistics about unemployment, and then live

02:24:13.260 --> 02:24:16.840
coverage of a presidential press conference
on the White House lawn.

02:24:16.840 --> 02:24:21.830
Dr. Bright was halfway through a thought about
how he’d hated his brief period of time

02:24:21.830 --> 02:24:30.850
inhabiting the body of George W. Bush and
running the country when the president started

02:24:30.850 --> 02:24:35.060
melting over his podium on live TV.

02:24:35.060 --> 02:24:38.060
As well as his guards.

02:24:38.060 --> 02:24:39.449
And the press corps.

02:24:39.449 --> 02:24:40.850
And the camera people.

02:24:40.850 --> 02:24:49.939
It was a sight so shocking it made Dr. Bright
drop his coffee cup, and almost drop his danish!

02:24:49.939 --> 02:24:58.260
He slipped out his phone and fired off a text
to O5-1, the leader of the O5 Council, simply

02:24:58.260 --> 02:25:00.790
saying, “We may have a problem.”

02:25:00.790 --> 02:25:05.420
To which he received the quick and emphatic
reply, “YOU THINK!?”

02:25:05.420 --> 02:25:08.359
Dr. Bright started frantically changing the
channel.

02:25:08.359 --> 02:25:16.689
He wanted to know if some anomalous troublemaker
had just melted the President and those around

02:25:16.689 --> 02:25:21.189
him, perhaps to destabilize the US government
- that’d be a textbook Chaos Insurgency

02:25:21.189 --> 02:25:25.979
move - or if they were looking at something
considerably more dangerous.

02:25:25.979 --> 02:25:31.650
And considering every single channel Dr. Bright
changed to started displaying a “Signal

02:25:31.650 --> 02:25:38.290
Interrupted” message, now was probably not
the time to be optimistic.

02:25:38.290 --> 02:25:39.520
It was here.

02:25:39.520 --> 02:25:40.960
The big one.

02:25:40.960 --> 02:25:44.330
The one they’d all been waiting for.

02:25:44.330 --> 02:25:45.780
Battle stations: Engaged.

02:25:45.780 --> 02:25:51.250
Across Site-19, the protocols were enacted
to put everything on total lockdown: Nobody

02:25:51.250 --> 02:25:52.590
in, nobody out.

02:25:52.590 --> 02:25:56.130
It was lucky for everyone inside that hadn’t
already been exposed that this protocol also

02:25:56.130 --> 02:26:03.990
involved the automatic shuttering of every
single window in the building.

02:26:03.990 --> 02:26:10.680
Dr. Bright and a crack team of senior researchers
and administrators gathered in the site’s

02:26:10.680 --> 02:26:18.700
command center and opened up a live video
link to O5-1.

02:26:18.700 --> 02:26:28.760
They might make some sense of all this chaos
now, and figure out a good battle plan.

02:26:28.760 --> 02:26:30.319
“So, what’s the situation, boss?”

02:26:30.319 --> 02:26:31.390
Dr. Bright asked.

02:26:31.390 --> 02:26:33.869
O5-1 let out a deep, rattling sigh.

02:26:33.869 --> 02:26:39.909
“Well, Jack, things are about as bad as
they ever have been, so I’ll try my best

02:26:39.909 --> 02:26:40.909
to take inventory.

02:26:40.909 --> 02:26:49.790
A quarter of the council is gone - They got
exposed and stopped reporting in during the

02:26:49.790 --> 02:26:51.260
initial shift.

02:26:51.260 --> 02:26:57.822
That includes O5-9 on the orbiting base, given
she probably got the most direct exposure.”

02:26:57.822 --> 02:27:02.780
A senior administrator raised their hand and
asked, “Exposure to what, sir?”

02:27:02.780 --> 02:27:05.810
O5-1 took a grave pause before saying…

02:27:05.810 --> 02:27:06.810
“Sunlight.

02:27:06.810 --> 02:27:10.150
That seems to be what we’re up against here.

02:27:10.150 --> 02:27:12.359
The one common denominator that seems to connect
everyone experiencing these anomalous effects

02:27:12.359 --> 02:27:13.359
is direct sunlight exposure.

02:27:13.359 --> 02:27:14.960
It causes people to take on a kind of... gelatinous
state.

02:27:14.960 --> 02:27:15.960
And it isn’t just people, either.

02:27:15.960 --> 02:27:16.960
It’s all living anomalies, too.

02:27:16.960 --> 02:27:19.550
Something about this new sun thing, it’s
almost like it cancels out all previous anomalous

02:27:19.550 --> 02:27:20.550
effects.

02:27:20.550 --> 02:27:31.109
The only anomalies that seem to be exempt
are the ones who aren’t even made out of

02:27:31.109 --> 02:27:32.109
flesh.”

02:27:32.109 --> 02:27:34.340
Dr. Bright, who had a tendency to make jokes
as an emotional defense mechanism when things

02:27:34.340 --> 02:27:41.840
got tough, chimed in with, “On a semi-related
note, anyone here feel like taking the lizard

02:27:41.840 --> 02:27:42.840
for a walk?”

02:27:42.840 --> 02:27:46.500
The few nervous chuckles in the room were
cut off by O5-1 slamming his fist against

02:27:46.500 --> 02:27:49.660
the table over the video link.

02:27:49.660 --> 02:27:51.920
Everyone fell into deathly silence.

02:27:51.920 --> 02:27:55.540
“Damn it, Jack, this is no laughing matter!

02:27:55.540 --> 02:28:00.810
The Foundation, and the rest of the human
race, is decimated right now.

02:28:00.810 --> 02:28:05.040
We’re looking at billions dead already,
or at least, changed.

02:28:05.040 --> 02:28:09.710
And even our people, a good portion of them
are reading the tea leaves and abandoning

02:28:09.710 --> 02:28:14.010
their posts to spend what could be their last
hours with their families.

02:28:14.010 --> 02:28:17.460
It’s imperative we take control of this
situation right now.

02:28:17.460 --> 02:28:19.960
It may be our last chance.

02:28:19.960 --> 02:28:24.119
So get your asses in gear and make it happen!”

02:28:24.119 --> 02:28:29.650
And with that, the video link was cut off,
and the ragtag leaders of Site-19 got to work.

02:28:29.650 --> 02:28:32.290
Taking what they knew about this lethal new
scenario, Dr. Bright had his subordinates

02:28:32.290 --> 02:28:38.300
record and release one of the most significant
videos of the 21st century, or perhaps even

02:28:38.300 --> 02:28:44.090
all of time, to be broadcasted on every communication
channel the Foundation could possibly hijack:

02:28:44.090 --> 02:28:47.920
TV, radio, social media, YouTube, Twitch,
all global news outlets.

02:28:47.920 --> 02:28:49.510
The SCP Foundation was going public.

02:28:49.510 --> 02:29:02.689
People all over the world - huddled in the
dark, terrified of the horrors they’d seen

02:29:02.689 --> 02:29:09.210
through windows or out of the safety of shady
corners - noticed that all the screens around

02:29:09.210 --> 02:29:13.729
them started playing the same thing in perfect
synchronicity.

02:29:13.729 --> 02:29:19.880
It was flashing red, with an unfamiliar logo,
while a commanding voice droned on with the

02:29:19.880 --> 02:29:22.050
only instructions that might give them hope
for survival.

02:29:22.050 --> 02:29:24.979
“This is a Public Service Announcement from
the SCP Foundation.

02:29:24.979 --> 02:29:28.960
We are commandeering all broadcast channels
and frequencies to deliver a vital message.

02:29:28.960 --> 02:29:30.500
Avoid sunlight at all costs.

02:29:30.500 --> 02:29:31.729
Cover your bodies thoroughly.

02:29:31.729 --> 02:29:36.659
And follow the instructions on the screen
to reach the nearest operational SCP Foundation

02:29:36.659 --> 02:29:37.659
containment site…”

02:29:37.659 --> 02:29:43.760
Back at Site-19, Dr. Bright and everyone they
had left were preparing for a sudden influx

02:29:43.760 --> 02:29:45.510
of refugees from the outside.

02:29:45.510 --> 02:29:51.500
Any members of D-Class personnel who weren’t
psychopathic killers were deputized as honorary

02:29:51.500 --> 02:29:57.529
Foundation personnel and brought in to help
convert empty wings into sleeping and living

02:29:57.529 --> 02:29:58.529
areas.

02:29:58.529 --> 02:30:03.790
Bright, who was still trying to hold on to
some semblance of humor in this dark time,

02:30:03.790 --> 02:30:10.600
thought to himself, “Well if I knew we were
going to be entertaining guests, I would have

02:30:10.600 --> 02:30:12.420
cleaned the place up a little more.

02:30:12.420 --> 02:30:15.640
Maybe bought some new throw pillows and a
scented candle or two.”

02:30:15.640 --> 02:30:18.530
And make no mistake, it was indeed an incredibly
dark time.

02:30:18.530 --> 02:30:23.330
It had only been a few hours since the phenomenon
started, and already, it was Hell out there.

02:30:23.330 --> 02:30:24.930
Society had already effectively collapsed
during the chaos.

02:30:24.930 --> 02:30:27.460
The streets were filled with the shambling
remains of melted nightmare people.

02:30:27.460 --> 02:30:31.970
The sun shone far above like some all-powerful
tyrant, gazing its single, deadly eye down

02:30:31.970 --> 02:30:32.970
on all of humanity.

02:30:32.970 --> 02:30:34.830
For billions upon billions of years, it had
given this solar system life.

02:30:34.830 --> 02:30:38.449
Now, all it had for them was death and destruction.

02:30:38.449 --> 02:30:42.970
For those trapped outside, there was only
one hope of survival: Getting to one of these

02:30:42.970 --> 02:30:44.760
“SCP Foundation” containment sites.

02:30:44.760 --> 02:30:49.670
At least those people somehow knew what was
going on here...

02:30:49.670 --> 02:30:54.520
Little did these unfortunate refugees of a
horribly and irreparably changed world know,

02:30:54.520 --> 02:31:00.920
that all was not well in the world of the
SCP Foundation.

02:31:00.920 --> 02:31:08.869
As the hours marched on, Broken Masquerade
scenario and possible XK aside, it seemed

02:31:08.869 --> 02:31:15.730
to Dr. Bright that they actually had a handle
on the situation, all things considered.

02:31:15.730 --> 02:31:19.660
And what’s more, the night was about to
fall, allowing for free movement outside.

02:31:19.660 --> 02:31:24.689
This would be a boon for people hoping to
reach Site-19 with minimal risk of exposure.

02:31:24.689 --> 02:31:31.080
But refugees wouldn’t be the only thing
coming to Site-19 that day.

02:31:31.080 --> 02:31:35.529
Hours earlier and miles away, something terrible
happened at Containment Area 25b - an extensive

02:31:35.529 --> 02:31:38.659
and heavily guarded containment site with
only one inmate.

02:31:38.659 --> 02:31:44.930
During the initial panic of the event that
they were now codenaming When Day Breaks,

02:31:44.930 --> 02:31:49.840
many of the guards were exposed, and several
others abandoned their posts in panic, knowing

02:31:49.840 --> 02:31:53.819
that the world as they had all grown to know
it had come to an end.

02:31:53.819 --> 02:31:55.319
This left the place grievously under-guarded.

02:31:55.319 --> 02:32:00.479
200 meters under the sea, in the true heart
of the containment area, an obsidian coffin

02:32:00.479 --> 02:32:04.170
was surrounded by a confused and terrified
skeleton crew.

02:32:04.170 --> 02:32:09.360
And the term “Skeleton Crew” was about
to take on a far more literal meaning, as

02:32:09.360 --> 02:32:14.430
the obsidian coffin they were trying in vain
to keep contained started to rattle.

02:32:14.430 --> 02:32:18.659
Fast forward by twenty minutes and every single
member of Foundation personnel at Containment

02:32:18.659 --> 02:32:23.159
Area 25b was dead, and SCP-076-2, the legendary
immortal warrior, Able, was standing in the

02:32:23.159 --> 02:32:24.930
ruins of the base.

02:32:24.930 --> 02:32:30.859
While it would be easy to write Able off as
a mindless killing machine because of the

02:32:30.859 --> 02:32:35.279
sheer ferocity he brings to a battle, the
reality couldn’t be further from the case.

02:32:35.279 --> 02:32:40.090
He had the kind of tactical intelligence that
human supercomputers wouldn’t develop for

02:32:40.090 --> 02:32:42.859
another several centuries - if the world hadn’t
ended already.

02:32:42.859 --> 02:32:48.279
From the visual evidence he’d gleaned upon
escaping, he already knew that sunlight had

02:32:48.279 --> 02:32:52.779
turned deadly, turning everyone who was exposed
into these feeble blobs.

02:32:52.779 --> 02:32:58.950
So, if he hoped to continue his killing frenzy
elsewhere, he’d need to create his own shade

02:32:58.950 --> 02:32:59.950
during transit.

02:32:59.950 --> 02:33:01.520
That would be simple enough.

02:33:01.520 --> 02:33:06.319
With a cruel smirk, Able picked up one of
the reinforced blast doors he’d so easily

02:33:06.319 --> 02:33:07.319
burst through earlier.

02:33:07.319 --> 02:33:12.620
The sheer weight of it would have crushed
a mortal man, but for Able, this was little

02:33:12.620 --> 02:33:14.500
more than a light workout.

02:33:14.500 --> 02:33:20.420
He lifted the door above his head like a parasol,
bathing himself in the cool shade, before

02:33:20.420 --> 02:33:22.390
taking off at impossible speeds.

02:33:22.390 --> 02:33:33.430
He’d remembered a lot from his days under
the Foundation’s employ as part of the disastrous

02:33:33.430 --> 02:33:38.840
Pandora’s Box Mobile Task Force, and one
of those things was the location of Site-19.

02:33:38.840 --> 02:33:43.930
There’d be plenty of warriors for him to
send to their makers there...

02:33:43.930 --> 02:33:47.510
Back at Site-19, where Able would very shortly
arrive, Dr. Bright and his team were helping

02:33:47.510 --> 02:33:52.840
the first wave of outsiders into the confines
of the building.

02:33:52.840 --> 02:34:02.120
At first, it had seemed like a good idea,
something they could sustain, but now, Dr.

02:34:02.120 --> 02:34:04.409
Bright was starting to worry about all the
practical considerations.

02:34:04.409 --> 02:34:08.029
How would they keep potentially thousands,
or even tens of thousands of people, fed,

02:34:08.029 --> 02:34:10.359
clothed, and watered for an extended period
of time?

02:34:10.359 --> 02:34:17.130
Sure, they had the infinite pizza box, but
pizza for three meals a day for weeks on end

02:34:17.130 --> 02:34:21.180
was a fast track to everyone here dying of
scurvy.

02:34:21.180 --> 02:34:26.779
These new worries were interrupted by a siren
that signaled familiar ones: There had been

02:34:26.779 --> 02:34:30.000
a containment breach in several cells in the
humanoid containment wings.

02:34:30.000 --> 02:34:36.460
Some of the low-risk or even friendly humanoids,
like SCP-343, aka God, had been let out to

02:34:36.460 --> 02:34:39.490
assist in gathering survivors from the nighttime
chaos outside.

02:34:39.490 --> 02:34:42.730
That meant anyone still locked up really,
really, really needed to stay locked up.

02:34:42.730 --> 02:34:45.359
In other words, it was very bad news.

02:34:45.359 --> 02:34:51.580
Dr. Bright cursed under his breath and instructed
his subordinates to keep focusing on the refugee

02:34:51.580 --> 02:34:52.580
situation.

02:34:52.580 --> 02:34:57.609
He shouldered an assault rifle and took off
into the bowels of the site, hoping he could

02:34:57.609 --> 02:35:02.660
at least head off the escapees until some
guards or Mobile Task Forces from smaller

02:35:02.660 --> 02:35:06.960
sites could reach Site-19 and help him in
getting them fully recontained.

02:35:06.960 --> 02:35:09.760
This is just typical, he thought to himself.

02:35:09.760 --> 02:35:11.520
When it rains, it pours.

02:35:11.520 --> 02:35:15.020
We can never catch a break around here, can
we?

02:35:15.020 --> 02:35:21.130
I should have followed my dreams and become
a competitive ice dancer, then I never would

02:35:21.130 --> 02:35:22.130
have—

02:35:22.130 --> 02:35:26.029
Dr. Bright’s self-pity was interrupted by
a brutal punch to the side of his head, which

02:35:26.029 --> 02:35:28.140
laid him out against the wall.

02:35:28.140 --> 02:35:33.419
He turned, vision swimming, and raised his
assault rifle, hoping to find a target in

02:35:33.419 --> 02:35:34.419
the blur.

02:35:34.419 --> 02:35:40.010
That’s when an obsidian blade swung at impossible
speed and cleaved his gun in half.

02:35:40.010 --> 02:35:47.400
He could feel his breath hitch in his throat
as his vision recentered itself and he saw

02:35:47.400 --> 02:35:51.489
the imposing, tattooed figure of Able standing
above him, wielding one of his trademark swords.

02:35:51.489 --> 02:35:54.120
His dark eyes were burning with deadly intent.

02:35:54.120 --> 02:35:55.580
“Hello, Jack,” he said.

02:35:55.580 --> 02:35:57.050
“Long time no see.

02:35:57.050 --> 02:36:02.090
I imagine I don’t need to remind you who
I am.”

02:36:02.090 --> 02:36:07.399
Able laughed and Dr. Bright felt his muscles
tense in rage.

02:36:07.399 --> 02:36:08.399
Of course, he knew Able.

02:36:08.399 --> 02:36:12.520
Not only had the homicidal maniac been a consistent
thorn in the Foundation’s side since day

02:36:12.520 --> 02:36:16.330
one, but he’d also been the one to change
Dr. Bright’s life forever.

02:36:16.330 --> 02:36:21.511
It had been Able who had killed him as a junior
researcher when he was first carrying the

02:36:21.511 --> 02:36:22.511
SCP-963 medallion.

02:36:22.511 --> 02:36:26.790
It was Able who had cursed him with this terrible
immortality.

02:36:26.790 --> 02:36:29.630
Dr. Bright despised him to his core.

02:36:29.630 --> 02:36:36.540
“I’d like you to meet a few friends I
made since I got here, Jack,” Able said,

02:36:36.540 --> 02:36:38.689
gesturing into the darkness of the hallway.

02:36:38.689 --> 02:36:41.100
“Who said I was bad at teamwork?”

02:36:41.100 --> 02:36:43.760
It was like something out of a nightmare.

02:36:43.760 --> 02:36:47.819
First, SCP-106 stepped forward, dripping with
that terrible, black tar.

02:36:47.819 --> 02:36:53.439
His face twisted into a manic grin that foretold
inhuman tortures to come.

02:36:53.439 --> 02:36:57.859
Then came SCP-953, the devilish Polymorphic
Humanoid, claws and fangs bared, tails swaying.

02:36:57.859 --> 02:37:01.149
And then, bafflingly, SCP-2430, the Immortal
Hitler Clone.

02:37:01.149 --> 02:37:08.279
Dr. Bright tried to move but found the tip
of Able’s sword right in front of his face.

02:37:08.279 --> 02:37:09.930
“It’s funny, Jack,” Able said.

02:37:09.930 --> 02:37:12.250
“You’re an anomaly who kills to live.

02:37:12.250 --> 02:37:14.899
How many people have worn your little necklace?

02:37:14.899 --> 02:37:19.859
And yet you can swan around as you please,
while we rot away in containment.

02:37:19.859 --> 02:37:22.279
We think it’s time for a little payback.”

02:37:22.279 --> 02:37:23.279
Bright nodded.

02:37:23.279 --> 02:37:24.720
“That makes sense, I guess.

02:37:24.720 --> 02:37:26.119
But why’s he here?”

02:37:26.119 --> 02:37:28.409
He pointed to the Immortal Hitler Clone, who
sneered, as usual.

02:37:28.409 --> 02:37:31.859
“He thinks you’re Jewish,” said the
Polymorphic Humanoid, in Korean.

02:37:31.859 --> 02:37:38.760
Dr. Bright rose uneasily to his feet, honestly
surprised that Able was allowing him to do

02:37:38.760 --> 02:37:39.760
so.

02:37:39.760 --> 02:37:41.521
He was lucky, in a sense.

02:37:41.521 --> 02:37:49.000
It perhaps took a being as strong as Able
to hold these others at bay.

02:37:49.000 --> 02:37:55.420
As much as Dr. Bright despised the man, he
offered a much quicker and more painless death

02:37:55.420 --> 02:37:57.170
than the Old Man or the Polymorphic Humanoid.

02:37:57.170 --> 02:37:58.170
It wouldn’t be anything he hadn’t experienced
before, after all.

02:37:58.170 --> 02:37:59.170
Life could be a sick joke sometimes.

02:37:59.170 --> 02:38:00.170
”So, what’s the plan?

02:38:00.170 --> 02:38:01.420
You’re gonna kill me again?”

02:38:01.420 --> 02:38:02.420
Dr. Bright asked.

02:38:02.420 --> 02:38:04.370
The four nightmarish anomalies all began to
laugh.

02:38:04.370 --> 02:38:06.689
“Well, in the end, yes,” Able said.

02:38:06.689 --> 02:38:08.670
“But the world’s gone to hell.

02:38:08.670 --> 02:38:13.180
Your people aren’t in control here anymore,
so we can take our time.

02:38:13.180 --> 02:38:16.360
We will slaughter you and everyone else in
here.

02:38:16.360 --> 02:38:18.819
If I were you, I’d run, Jack.”

02:38:18.819 --> 02:38:21.180
He didn’t need to be told twice.

02:38:21.180 --> 02:38:27.180
Jack ran for his life, too scared to even
look behind him as the sound of footsteps

02:38:27.180 --> 02:38:28.180
got louder.

02:38:28.180 --> 02:38:32.890
The only way things could possibly get worse
is if he somehow, amidst all this, suddenly

02:38:32.890 --> 02:38:33.890
developed a hernia.

02:38:33.890 --> 02:38:36.770
And the way this day was going, he really
wouldn’t have been surprised.

02:38:36.770 --> 02:38:40.450
Dr. Bright turned a corner, and somehow, the
Polymorphic Humanoid was already there, standing

02:38:40.450 --> 02:38:41.590
in front of him.

02:38:41.590 --> 02:38:49.899
She gave a girlish giggle and swiped at him,
leaving four ragged claw marks on his chest.

02:38:49.899 --> 02:38:55.279
Dr. Bright winced in pain and started running
in the other direction.

02:38:55.279 --> 02:39:00.520
He reached an adjoining hallway and booked
it for the armory, hoping there he’d get

02:39:00.520 --> 02:39:01.520
some serious firepower.

02:39:01.520 --> 02:39:06.100
But SCP-106 suddenly phased through the wall
next to him, grabbing Dr. Bright by his lab

02:39:06.100 --> 02:39:16.210
coat and throwing him against the opposite
wall with a flick of his wrist.

02:39:16.210 --> 02:39:25.050
Bright scrambled to discard the coat, already
being eaten away by the Old Man’s caustic

02:39:25.050 --> 02:39:26.050
fluids.

02:39:26.050 --> 02:39:30.449
The Old Man just watched him and chuckled,
faintly amused at the whole affair.

02:39:30.449 --> 02:39:33.510
These damn anomalies were in control now.

02:39:33.510 --> 02:39:36.119
Dr. Bright got up and kept running.

02:39:36.119 --> 02:39:42.550
The one saving grace was the fact that all
of these anomalies were too sadistic to outright

02:39:42.550 --> 02:39:46.770
kill him just yet, so he still had the faintest
of chances.

02:39:46.770 --> 02:39:53.540
He ran into the break room, tipping over shelves,
bookcases, and furniture to obstruct the path

02:39:53.540 --> 02:39:55.890
behind him even slightly.

02:39:55.890 --> 02:40:04.609
That’s when he heard the door behind him
open, followed by a wave of furious German

02:40:04.609 --> 02:40:08.590
expletives as the Immortal Hitler Clone entered
the room to give chase.

02:40:08.590 --> 02:40:12.199
However, moments later, Hitler fell to the
ground, clutching his foot and wailing in

02:40:12.199 --> 02:40:13.199
agony.

02:40:13.199 --> 02:40:16.029
You see, due to the circumstances of his creation
- Josef Stalin, Sarkic wizards, it was a whole

02:40:16.029 --> 02:40:21.590
thing, we can’t get into it now - he felt
pain at three times the intensity of a normal

02:40:21.590 --> 02:40:22.590
human being.

02:40:22.590 --> 02:40:24.939
“Lieber Gott, I stubbed mein toe!” he
yelled.

02:40:24.939 --> 02:40:25.939
Dr. Bright kept running.

02:40:25.939 --> 02:40:31.649
From the break room, he entered a hallway
that would lead him back to the main barracks.

02:40:31.649 --> 02:40:34.390
Perhaps there, he could get some reinforcements.

02:40:34.390 --> 02:40:35.949
Then maybe he could—

02:40:35.949 --> 02:40:42.159
His thoughts were once again cut off by Able
suddenly appearing and grabbing him by the

02:40:42.159 --> 02:40:43.159
throat.

02:40:43.159 --> 02:40:45.899
His grip was impossibly strong; Dr. Bright
was already seeing stars.

02:40:45.899 --> 02:40:48.569
“Think you could get away that easily?”

02:40:48.569 --> 02:40:49.569
Able hissed.

02:40:49.569 --> 02:40:55.069
With horrifying strength, Able slammed Dr.
Bright up against one of the shuttered windows.

02:40:55.069 --> 02:41:04.439
He struck it with such force he broke through
the shutter and the glass behind it, sailing

02:41:04.439 --> 02:41:06.350
down to the ground outside.

02:41:06.350 --> 02:41:10.420
They were lucky it was nighttime, or this
all would have been over already.

02:41:10.420 --> 02:41:15.080
Able soon followed him down, cracking his
knuckles and assuming a fighting stance.

02:41:15.080 --> 02:41:18.040
“Up on your feet, Jack,” Able said.

02:41:18.040 --> 02:41:20.910
“I won’t kill a man lying down.

02:41:20.910 --> 02:41:22.550
No honor in that.”

02:41:22.550 --> 02:41:26.649
Jack rose, feeling pain in every inch of his
body.

02:41:26.649 --> 02:41:31.489
Up against a seven-foot-tall, jacked monster
of a man with thousands of years of fighting

02:41:31.489 --> 02:41:32.489
under his belt?

02:41:32.489 --> 02:41:33.710
He stood mathematically zero chance.

02:41:33.710 --> 02:41:37.470
But perhaps he could at least get some licks
in.

02:41:37.470 --> 02:41:43.750
He ran at Able, screaming with years of pent-up
rage, and tried to attack him with a flurry

02:41:43.750 --> 02:41:44.750
of blows.

02:41:44.750 --> 02:41:45.750
But not a single one of them landed.

02:41:45.750 --> 02:41:48.529
Able dodged every punch and returned them
with a force comparable to being hit by a

02:41:48.529 --> 02:41:49.529
runaway train.

02:41:49.529 --> 02:41:50.529
He knocked Dr. Bright to the ground and started
beating him relentlessly.

02:41:50.529 --> 02:41:54.949
“All these lives I’ve given you, all this
extra time, and still, you haven’t learned

02:41:54.949 --> 02:41:55.979
how to fight.

02:41:55.979 --> 02:41:56.979
Pathetic!”

02:41:56.979 --> 02:41:58.710
Able roared, punctuating his words with blows.

02:41:58.710 --> 02:42:02.810
“Perhaps in a thousand years, you might
last sixty seconds against me.

02:42:02.810 --> 02:42:05.020
You are a waste of your gift!”

02:42:05.020 --> 02:42:10.370
Dr. Bright could already feel the life leaving
his body when something new entered the equation.

02:42:10.370 --> 02:42:17.270
He could barely make it out through his bloody,
swollen eyes, but something huge was coming

02:42:17.270 --> 02:42:18.270
toward them.

02:42:18.270 --> 02:42:22.120
It was like flesh from so many of the melted
people had coagulated into some huge, mountainous

02:42:22.120 --> 02:42:23.120
blob.

02:42:23.120 --> 02:42:27.450
It was a sight so remarkable and horrifying
that even Able was distracted.

02:42:27.450 --> 02:42:30.990
And Dr. Bright took advantage of the one chance
that gave him.

02:42:30.990 --> 02:42:35.750
He took off his medallion and threw it at
the mass.

02:42:35.750 --> 02:42:40.590
The life left his old body as SCP-963 deleted
the collection of tortured minds that made

02:42:40.590 --> 02:42:44.720
up the terrible flesh monster, and replaced
them with only one: Dr. Jack Bright.

02:42:44.720 --> 02:42:47.840
He felt new immense, monstrous strength well
up through him.

02:42:47.840 --> 02:42:55.420
He was in the body of a true abomination,
and all of its power was his now.

02:42:55.420 --> 02:42:59.109
“How about a round two, Able?” he thought.

02:42:59.109 --> 02:43:03.739
The ancient warrior produced a pair of ebony
swords as Dr. Bright sent a swarm of fleshy

02:43:03.739 --> 02:43:04.739
tendrils at him.

02:43:04.739 --> 02:43:09.739
Able was quick, slicing off many of them,
but there was only so much he could take.

02:43:09.739 --> 02:43:14.160
Soon, Dr. Bright’s new body was grabbing
Able’s every appendage, holding him in place

02:43:14.160 --> 02:43:16.470
as he struggled with all his anomalous might.

02:43:16.470 --> 02:43:21.920
But now, Dr. Bright’s rage had meaning,
with this new power to back it up.

02:43:21.920 --> 02:43:25.220
He’d get a little revenge, at long last.

02:43:25.220 --> 02:43:27.979
Time to go back in the box, Able...

02:43:27.979 --> 02:43:33.079
It took longer than it would for any mortal
man, but eventually, it happened all the same.

02:43:33.079 --> 02:43:36.280
Bright pushed and pulled in every direction,
applying every kind of pressure, leveraging

02:43:36.280 --> 02:43:39.090
every ounce of his terrible new mass onto
Able’s body, until...

02:43:39.090 --> 02:43:40.090
Crunch.

02:43:40.090 --> 02:43:42.750
Dr. Bright relaxed his new body in final,
cathartic relief, dropping Able’s corpse

02:43:42.750 --> 02:43:43.750
to the ground.

02:43:43.750 --> 02:43:46.220
There was no denying, after everything that’d
happened, that it felt really, really, really

02:43:46.220 --> 02:43:47.220
good.

02:43:47.220 --> 02:43:50.740
The world may have ended, but at least Dr.
Bright got one win today.

02:43:50.740 --> 02:43:53.670
Shirley woke up screaming, louder than usual
this time.

02:43:53.670 --> 02:43:58.220
Bea, Shirley’s wife, sat bolt upright, as
though someone had just run a 20,000-volt

02:43:58.220 --> 02:43:59.220
charge through her.

02:43:59.220 --> 02:44:03.319
Her dirty hand clasped over Shirley’s mouth
until the awful sound stopped.

02:44:03.319 --> 02:44:06.750
She whispered, “Baby, it was a dream, just
a dream!”

02:44:06.750 --> 02:44:10.130
Shirley fell into a terrified silence, trying
to regulate her breathing.

02:44:10.130 --> 02:44:14.819
They’d been hiding in an abandoned apartment
block for three days now, waiting for the

02:44:14.819 --> 02:44:18.569
roads to clear, so they could take the chance
and move forward.

02:44:18.569 --> 02:44:22.270
Bea, keeping close to the ground, scuttled
over to one of the smashed-out windows.

02:44:22.270 --> 02:44:24.890
She stared down onto the street, thirty feet
below.

02:44:24.890 --> 02:44:28.560
Midnight, no stars, cars crashed up against
each other from the initial panic.

02:44:28.560 --> 02:44:31.319
And thank god, none of those things down there...

02:44:31.319 --> 02:44:36.529
She breathed a sigh of relief and returned
to Shirley, gathering her up in her arms and

02:44:36.529 --> 02:44:38.060
giving her a reassuring squeeze.

02:44:38.060 --> 02:44:39.600
Her body was still trembling.

02:44:39.600 --> 02:44:41.130
“What happened in this one?”

02:44:41.130 --> 02:44:42.130
Bea asked.

02:44:42.130 --> 02:44:43.579
Shirley exhaled and closed her eyes.

02:44:43.579 --> 02:44:45.119
“We’re close now,” she said.

02:44:45.119 --> 02:44:46.500
“We’re so close, Bea.”

02:44:46.500 --> 02:44:48.310
It was the dreams that began this journey.

02:44:48.310 --> 02:44:50.689
The quest for the last safe place.

02:44:50.689 --> 02:44:57.140
Initially, Bea had thought it was a fool’s
errand - There were no “Safe” places in

02:44:57.140 --> 02:45:02.770
this world anymore, nowhere that the sun or
its terrible monsters hadn’t touched.

02:45:02.770 --> 02:45:04.250
But Shirley insisted otherwise.

02:45:04.250 --> 02:45:06.470
She’d seen it in her dreams.

02:45:06.470 --> 02:45:09.979
A place of almost mythical wonder, a vast
and complex facility, away from the light.

02:45:09.979 --> 02:45:10.979
Site-19.

02:45:10.979 --> 02:45:13.640
Shirley and Bea grabbed their backpacks and
shrugged them on.

02:45:13.640 --> 02:45:19.199
It was best to set off now, get a few hours
of walking under their belts and find some

02:45:19.199 --> 02:45:20.960
shelter before the sun came back up.

02:45:20.960 --> 02:45:25.609
In the meantime, all they needed to do was
avoid the monsters lurking out there.

02:45:25.609 --> 02:45:27.050
It was an interesting trade-off.

02:45:27.050 --> 02:45:32.900
Once upon a time, years and years ago, when
the sun changed, there had been billions of

02:45:32.900 --> 02:45:34.220
those things.

02:45:34.220 --> 02:45:42.010
They were people once, until the new light
touched them and turned them into these slimy

02:45:42.010 --> 02:45:43.010
abominations.

02:45:43.010 --> 02:45:52.340
There was a time you couldn’t look out your
window without seeing hundreds of them slithering

02:45:52.340 --> 02:45:53.340
past you.

02:45:53.340 --> 02:45:54.340
Then, they started coagulating.

02:45:54.340 --> 02:45:57.180
Becoming these huge, mountainous masses of
gloopy, melted flesh.

02:45:57.180 --> 02:46:02.739
It was nice that they were fewer now, made
them easier to avoid, easier to sneak around.

02:46:02.739 --> 02:46:06.220
But if one of those things found you, you
were dead.

02:46:06.220 --> 02:46:10.939
Or more likely, you were about to be something
so much worse than dead.

02:46:10.939 --> 02:46:16.880
The two women trekked down the side of the
road, keeping to the shadows, even in the

02:46:16.880 --> 02:46:17.880
dark.

02:46:17.880 --> 02:46:22.420
They were on the edge of the city now, and
it wouldn’t be long until they were in the

02:46:22.420 --> 02:46:23.420
wilderness.

02:46:23.420 --> 02:46:29.590
Pro: Being away from a population center meant
lower odds of being attacked by one of those

02:46:29.590 --> 02:46:30.590
monsters.

02:46:30.590 --> 02:46:33.979
Con: Fewer places to hide when the sun came
up.

02:46:33.979 --> 02:46:37.470
In the meantime, all they could do was keep
walking and hope.

02:46:37.470 --> 02:46:45.140
It was an almost supernatural mixture of luck
and skill that had gotten them this far.

02:46:45.140 --> 02:46:50.000
They’d known each other for four years when
day broke and everything went to hell, but

02:46:50.000 --> 02:46:54.439
they’d only been married for about a month
- it’d been a hell of a post-honeymoon wakeup

02:46:54.439 --> 02:46:55.439
call.

02:46:55.439 --> 02:47:02.369
So many had been changed in the initial panic,
and so many more had been destroyed by what

02:47:02.369 --> 02:47:03.369
came after.

02:47:03.369 --> 02:47:05.170
But these two weren’t just anybody.

02:47:05.170 --> 02:47:09.260
In fact, it was the specific combination of
skills and knowledge these two possessed that’d

02:47:09.260 --> 02:47:10.859
kept them alive for this long.

02:47:10.859 --> 02:47:15.189
In the world that came before, Bea had been
a soldier who’d done two tours abroad and

02:47:15.189 --> 02:47:16.770
seen active combat multiple times.

02:47:16.770 --> 02:47:21.060
And Shirley had been a doctor, rising through
the ranks of a local hospital.

02:47:21.060 --> 02:47:25.000
Between them, they could fight for and heal
one another, despite everything.

02:47:25.000 --> 02:47:29.500
And in those early years, that’s all it
had been.

02:47:29.500 --> 02:47:36.979
They were always moving, always avoiding the
terrors outside and the tyranny of the sun.

02:47:36.979 --> 02:47:42.159
They’d avoided the dangers that’d destroyed
so many others, but they’d never dared to

02:47:42.159 --> 02:47:43.159
hope.

02:47:43.159 --> 02:47:44.620
Hope was lofty, impractical, it got people
killed.

02:47:44.620 --> 02:47:45.620
They’d only ever tried to survive.

02:47:45.620 --> 02:47:46.620
The immediate avoidance of death was a practical
goal.

02:47:46.620 --> 02:47:47.620
Anything else was just confetti.

02:47:47.620 --> 02:47:50.510
Until, of course, Shirley had started having
the dreams.

02:47:50.510 --> 02:47:58.101
Some were just fragments, like sifting through
a stack of photographs in the middle of a

02:47:58.101 --> 02:47:59.101
deep fog.

02:47:59.101 --> 02:48:05.729
Others were full narratives, like movies in
her head, viewed in the first person.

02:48:05.729 --> 02:48:14.620
But all of them were about the same thing:
She and Bea needed to make it to a place they

02:48:14.620 --> 02:48:15.620
called “Site-19.”

02:48:15.620 --> 02:48:20.260
Something about it was familiar, like a thing
she’d heard years ago.

02:48:20.260 --> 02:48:24.369
Perhaps from those strange broadcasts that
were on every TV screen during the initial

02:48:24.369 --> 02:48:25.369
chaos.

02:48:25.369 --> 02:48:28.680
Oh, and of course, there was one more element
in the dream.

02:48:28.680 --> 02:48:32.899
The one thing that made it seem more like
a nightmare.

02:48:32.899 --> 02:48:37.229
In this part of the dream, she was already
in Site-19, and walking down a long, dark

02:48:37.229 --> 02:48:39.399
hallway, somewhere far away from the sun.

02:48:39.399 --> 02:48:44.449
There are a series of heavy reinforced doors
along the wall next to her.

02:48:44.449 --> 02:48:50.290
Each one had a number on them, advancing as
she got further down the hall: 029, 030, 031,

02:48:50.290 --> 02:48:51.290
032...

02:48:51.290 --> 02:48:52.850
That’s when she’d hear the voice.

02:48:52.850 --> 02:48:54.319
That awful, terrible voice.

02:48:54.319 --> 02:48:55.319
“Shirley...

02:48:55.319 --> 02:48:56.319
Oh, Shirley...

02:48:56.319 --> 02:48:57.319
Come closer...

02:48:57.319 --> 02:48:58.710
Don’t you want to see my face?

02:48:58.710 --> 02:48:59.810
I’m Smiling, Shirley.

02:48:59.810 --> 02:49:02.010
I’d like you to see it...”

02:49:02.010 --> 02:49:07.500
She’d reach the door marked 035, at which
point, the next door slowly creaked open.

02:49:07.500 --> 02:49:13.810
Even after all the terrifying things they’d
seen in the new world, this somehow paralyzed

02:49:13.810 --> 02:49:16.290
her with fear every time.

02:49:16.290 --> 02:49:18.630
It was a kind of dread she’d never experienced
before.

02:49:18.630 --> 02:49:22.060
Something that looked like black water would
start seeping out from the opening door, then

02:49:22.060 --> 02:49:23.060
footsteps, getting closer.

02:49:23.060 --> 02:49:24.250
A shambling figure would emerge from the open
door.

02:49:24.250 --> 02:49:28.460
It was hard to make it out fully in the dark,
but somehow, she just intuitively knew what

02:49:28.460 --> 02:49:29.989
was coming toward her.

02:49:29.989 --> 02:49:37.100
It had a stark-white face, like a mask, with
nothing but darkness where the eyes should

02:49:37.100 --> 02:49:38.100
be.

02:49:38.100 --> 02:49:44.029
Down below, the body, seemingly heavily injured
and dripping with that same black water, was

02:49:44.029 --> 02:49:45.029
one she recognized.

02:49:45.029 --> 02:49:46.029
Her own.

02:49:46.029 --> 02:49:47.029
She couldn’t move.

02:49:47.029 --> 02:49:50.720
The masked figure, this masked version of
her, kept getting closer, dripping with more

02:49:50.720 --> 02:49:53.470
and more of that black water.

02:49:53.470 --> 02:49:55.760
It was shaking, almost vibrating.

02:49:55.760 --> 02:49:57.670
Then she realized, it was laughing.

02:49:57.670 --> 02:49:58.939
”See you soon, Shirley,” it said.

02:49:58.939 --> 02:50:00.729
“Can’t wait for you and Bea to visit...”

02:50:00.729 --> 02:50:03.020
And that was when she woke up screaming.

02:50:03.020 --> 02:50:07.800
Somehow, despite the grim ending, some part
of Shirley knew that she and Bea needed to

02:50:07.800 --> 02:50:08.800
get to Site-19.

02:50:08.800 --> 02:50:15.420
Only there would she be safe, and perhaps
even find the answers to how all this horror

02:50:15.420 --> 02:50:16.720
had even transpired.

02:50:16.720 --> 02:50:20.369
They were already risking their lives out
here every day, wouldn’t it be worth it

02:50:20.369 --> 02:50:21.619
to find the truth?

02:50:21.619 --> 02:50:24.710
The duo continued to trudge through the night.

02:50:24.710 --> 02:50:28.949
It was, they thanked their lucky stars, a
relatively uneventful journey.

02:50:28.949 --> 02:50:35.109
They encountered one of the flesh beasts on
the edge of town, but they were able to hide

02:50:35.109 --> 02:50:38.899
inside a nearby bus depot until it slithered
on, gibbering incoherent madness.

02:50:38.899 --> 02:50:46.990
After waiting to make sure it was gone, the
duo left the city and ventured out into the

02:50:46.990 --> 02:50:51.120
badlands, Shirley continuing to insist that
the legendary Site-19 from her dreams was

02:50:51.120 --> 02:50:52.390
definitely close by now.

02:50:52.390 --> 02:51:01.810
Bea looked up at the sky and chimed in, “I
hope you’re right, honey, or you and me

02:51:01.810 --> 02:51:03.420
are fried eggs.”

02:51:03.420 --> 02:51:08.130
Shirley and Bea walked for another several
hours, each becoming more silently anxious

02:51:08.130 --> 02:51:22.710
that the sun would rise before they reached
ample cover, and everything they worked for

02:51:22.710 --> 02:51:25.210
would be taken away from them.

02:51:25.210 --> 02:51:31.460
This terror persisted until, against all odds,
a huge, black shape started to emerge along

02:51:31.460 --> 02:51:33.010
the horizon.

02:51:33.010 --> 02:51:37.790
The sight of it made Shirley’s eyes widen
in amazement.

02:51:37.790 --> 02:51:39.720
“That’s it, Bea,” she said.

02:51:39.720 --> 02:51:40.720
“Site-19.

02:51:40.720 --> 02:51:43.720
Just like it was in my dreams...”

02:51:43.720 --> 02:51:47.890
They passed the chain-link fence around the
perimeter, approaching this large, seemingly

02:51:47.890 --> 02:51:51.659
abandoned building like it was the Lost City
Of El Dorado.

02:51:51.659 --> 02:51:52.659
Bea was astonished.

02:51:52.659 --> 02:52:00.359
She loved Shirley, of course, and had faith
in her beliefs, but something about a place

02:52:00.359 --> 02:52:07.189
prophesied in a dream actually being real
was honestly nothing short of a miracle.

02:52:07.189 --> 02:52:13.359
The two of them approached the fortified front
door and heaved it open together.

02:52:13.359 --> 02:52:15.779
Against all odds, they could see light inside
the building.

02:52:15.779 --> 02:52:17.439
They still had power here!?

02:52:17.439 --> 02:52:18.779
Nowhere had power anymore!

02:52:18.779 --> 02:52:21.439
Maybe this place really was salvation, after
all...

02:52:21.439 --> 02:52:27.220
As the two closed the door behind them, they
noticed that the windows all around them were

02:52:27.220 --> 02:52:28.220
shuttered.

02:52:28.220 --> 02:52:31.529
It must have been some precautionary measure
against the new sun.

02:52:31.529 --> 02:52:32.529
Smart.

02:52:32.529 --> 02:52:33.649
Whoever these people were, they’d thought
ahead.

02:52:33.649 --> 02:52:35.779
They’d had some kind of preparation for
all this.

02:52:35.779 --> 02:52:38.720
So, Bea couldn’t help but wonder, where
had they all gone now?

02:52:38.720 --> 02:52:44.729
As the two ventured deeper into the building,
Bea unholstered a handgun that she’d been

02:52:44.729 --> 02:52:46.630
keeping in her jacket.

02:52:46.630 --> 02:52:51.470
It was no good against the flesh monsters,
but it was a worthwhile deterrent against

02:52:51.470 --> 02:52:53.909
other desperate people out on the road.

02:52:53.909 --> 02:52:57.500
Lucky for them, she hadn’t needed to fire
it.

02:52:57.500 --> 02:52:58.500
Yet.

02:52:58.500 --> 02:53:04.760
Other than the power being on somehow, the
place seemed abandoned.

02:53:04.760 --> 02:53:12.319
They traveled from room to room - offices,
filing areas, what seemed like laboratories,

02:53:12.319 --> 02:53:13.800
libraries, rest areas.

02:53:13.800 --> 02:53:19.989
There was no real indication of what these
people, whoever they were, actually did around

02:53:19.989 --> 02:53:20.989
here.

02:53:20.989 --> 02:53:25.729
After searching through a few different files,
all written in almost incomprehensible short-hand

02:53:25.729 --> 02:53:32.290
and code, Shirley at least found a name to
put to these people: The SCP Foundation.

02:53:32.290 --> 02:53:33.939
Was it some kind of charity?

02:53:33.939 --> 02:53:38.470
They ventured deeper until they found a hall
marked “D-Class Bunks.”

02:53:38.470 --> 02:53:43.000
Inside, they found bunk beds stacked on top
of each other.

02:53:43.000 --> 02:53:45.479
Enough to hold hundreds of people.

02:53:45.479 --> 02:53:48.210
Bea wondered aloud whether this was some kind
of secret paramilitary outfit.

02:53:48.210 --> 02:53:52.710
It was the one thing that might explain everything
they’d seen here today.

02:53:52.710 --> 02:53:56.080
And in all fairness, she was at least partially
right.

02:53:56.080 --> 02:53:58.109
“Okay, ladies, hold it right there!”

02:53:58.109 --> 02:54:03.859
Shirley and Bea turned around to see a man
in an orange jumpsuit standing a few feet

02:54:03.859 --> 02:54:04.859
behind them.

02:54:04.859 --> 02:54:12.090
He was carrying an assault rifle, and training
it on both of them.

02:54:12.090 --> 02:54:17.779
He was a tall, imposing man, and yet, he was
shaking.

02:54:17.779 --> 02:54:20.140
Bea lowered her gun.

02:54:20.140 --> 02:54:24.439
Neither she nor Shirley said a word.

02:54:24.439 --> 02:54:29.770
“You’re human, right?” he said, voice
trembling slightly.

02:54:29.770 --> 02:54:30.770
“What?”

02:54:30.770 --> 02:54:31.770
Shirley replied.

02:54:31.770 --> 02:54:32.860
“Just answer the damn question!”

02:54:32.860 --> 02:54:38.840
There was a tense pause, where nobody there
seemed to know what might happen to them next.

02:54:38.840 --> 02:54:41.409
The stranger’s finger started curling around
the trigger.

02:54:41.409 --> 02:54:43.869
It was Bea who broke the silence.

02:54:43.869 --> 02:54:45.630
“We look human, don’t we?”

02:54:45.630 --> 02:54:46.630
she said.

02:54:46.630 --> 02:54:48.109
Another moment passed.

02:54:48.109 --> 02:54:50.260
The gun rattled in the stranger’s hands.

02:54:50.260 --> 02:54:54.159
He exhaled and lowered the barrel, taking
a more relaxed stance.

02:54:54.159 --> 02:54:55.229
“Sorry,” he said.

02:54:55.229 --> 02:54:59.109
“Can’t be too careful, the kind of stuff
they used to keep in here.

02:54:59.109 --> 02:55:00.109
It’d give you nightmares.

02:55:00.109 --> 02:55:01.370
I’m Mike, by the way.”

02:55:01.370 --> 02:55:05.970
He approached, tentatively shaking Shirley
and Bea’s hands, just to prove to them that

02:55:05.970 --> 02:55:07.479
he wasn’t the kind of guy who liked to gun
down random women.

02:55:07.479 --> 02:55:12.000
He asked them whether they were hungry, and
when they told him they hadn’t eaten anything

02:55:12.000 --> 02:55:17.879
but protein bars in over a week, he told them
he was about to show them something incredible:

02:55:17.879 --> 02:55:19.050
The Site-19 Break Room.

02:55:19.050 --> 02:55:24.010
Not long after that, Shirley, Bea, and Mike
were all sitting around, enjoying some defrosted

02:55:24.010 --> 02:55:25.010
hot pockets.

02:55:25.010 --> 02:55:29.620
At that moment, no three-course meal cooked
up by a five-star chef could ever even compare.

02:55:29.620 --> 02:55:32.120
As they ate together, Mike told his story.

02:55:32.120 --> 02:55:36.180
“The people who used to run this place,
believe me, they were nutjobs.

02:55:36.180 --> 02:55:40.030
Sure, back in the day, I wasn’t such a nice
guy.

02:55:40.030 --> 02:55:47.649
Me and some other knuckleheads, we needed
the money, so we robbed a few places.

02:55:47.649 --> 02:55:52.370
People got caught in the crossfire, next thing
I know, I’m on death row.

02:55:52.370 --> 02:55:59.319
Then these men in white coats, they came and
told me I could get a commuted sentence if

02:55:59.319 --> 02:56:03.250
I came and took part in some experiments for
them.

02:56:03.250 --> 02:56:09.130
That’s how they got me into their house
of horrors right here...”

02:56:09.130 --> 02:56:15.330
If Shirley and Bea hadn’t lived through
the apocalyptic hell of When Day Breaks, they

02:56:15.330 --> 02:56:17.210
might not have believed Mike.

02:56:17.210 --> 02:56:22.100
He told them about how this “SCP Foundation”
used to use humans in experiments, subjecting

02:56:22.100 --> 02:56:26.119
them to all the monsters they had locked up
down here.

02:56:26.119 --> 02:56:29.300
There was this giant reptile that couldn’t
be killed.

02:56:29.300 --> 02:56:33.899
Some freaky statue that’d snap your neck
if you stopped looking at it.

02:56:33.899 --> 02:56:38.220
But the one that’d frightened him the most,
the one that’d haunted his dreams, ever

02:56:38.220 --> 02:56:39.729
since he got here...

02:56:39.729 --> 02:56:42.760
“The Mask,” Shirley said aloud, lost in
thought.

02:56:42.760 --> 02:56:44.270
Mike was taken aback.

02:56:44.270 --> 02:56:47.649
How had she known what was he going to say?

02:56:47.649 --> 02:56:53.670
It did nothing to reduce his fear when she
told him that she’d been dreaming about

02:56:53.670 --> 02:56:55.909
the Mask for over a month now.

02:56:55.909 --> 02:56:58.029
Stark white, with black water dripping from
it.

02:56:58.029 --> 02:56:59.750
Everyone was silent for a moment.

02:56:59.750 --> 02:57:03.040
Bea and Mike just stared at Shirley, equal
parts amazed and frightened.

02:57:03.040 --> 02:57:08.199
Mike cleared his throat and told Shirley that
in the years that had passed since day broke,

02:57:08.199 --> 02:57:10.239
the population of Site-19 had slowly dwindled.

02:57:10.239 --> 02:57:14.600
Monsters had escaped, the flesh beasts had
slithered in and taken others.

02:57:14.600 --> 02:57:19.080
Over time, most of the staff had either died,
changed, or left.

02:57:19.080 --> 02:57:21.610
All that was left here was him.

02:57:21.610 --> 02:57:23.050
Him, and the Mask.

02:57:23.050 --> 02:57:26.640
Luckily for him, he’d never been directly
“cross-tested” with it.

02:57:26.640 --> 02:57:30.600
Because those who got tested with the Mask
never came back.

02:57:30.600 --> 02:57:34.930
Sometimes, he could hear it whispering in
his dreams, trying to leak itself into his

02:57:34.930 --> 02:57:35.979
mind, drip by drip, poisoning his soul.

02:57:35.979 --> 02:57:40.319
The one saving grace was that, being a mask,
it couldn’t exactly walk up and tap you

02:57:40.319 --> 02:57:41.319
on the shoulder.

02:57:41.319 --> 02:57:45.619
All you needed to do was stay away from it,
and you’d be fine.

02:57:45.619 --> 02:57:48.270
The Mask couldn’t hurt you on its own.

02:57:48.270 --> 02:57:52.750
The three of them carried on talking for the
last hours of the morning.

02:57:52.750 --> 02:57:56.840
Night had become day and day had become night,
it was the easiest way to survive these days.

02:57:56.840 --> 02:57:58.619
Or at least, survive as yourself.

02:57:58.619 --> 02:58:00.779
Bea and Shirley told Mike how they’d met
in college, all those years ago.

02:58:00.779 --> 02:58:08.609
Mike told them the story of how he managed
to survive by hiding in a broom closet while

02:58:08.609 --> 02:58:12.630
some gangly, white monstrosity chased down
and slaughtered the rest of his bunkmates.

02:58:12.630 --> 02:58:17.930
The tiredness took them all again, and they
decided to bed down in the D-Class Bunks.

02:58:17.930 --> 02:58:22.920
They were more comfortable than the bedrolls
that Shirley and Bea had been sleeping on

02:58:22.920 --> 02:58:26.880
in abandoned buildings for the last year or
so.

02:58:26.880 --> 02:58:30.939
To Mike, this place had been a living Hell
once upon a time.

02:58:30.939 --> 02:58:35.939
But now, for a brief period in this terrifying
new world, it could be Heaven.

02:58:35.939 --> 02:58:40.000
Shirley drifted off to uneasy sleep, trying
her best to ignore the whispers.

02:58:40.000 --> 02:58:44.560
In what only felt like a few moments, her
eyes fluttered back open.

02:58:44.560 --> 02:58:48.770
She was in a new place, but one that felt
oddly familiar.

02:58:48.770 --> 02:58:52.170
A long, dark hallway, bordered on one side
by a series of iron doors.

02:58:52.170 --> 02:58:55.590
She turned her head slightly, eyes still adjusting
to the darkness.

02:58:55.590 --> 02:59:00.330
The number on the door next to her read, “027.”

02:59:00.330 --> 02:59:03.630
Further down the hall, Shirley saw a dark
figure, standing tall, shoulders broad and

02:59:03.630 --> 02:59:05.100
back erect as a ballet dancer.

02:59:05.100 --> 02:59:08.290
As her vision came back into focus, she saw
the shock of orange.

02:59:08.290 --> 02:59:10.250
It was Mike, wearing that same orange jumpsuit.

02:59:10.250 --> 02:59:13.200
He was standing about ten or fifteen feet
away, staring in the opposite direction.

02:59:13.200 --> 02:59:14.200
“Mike?”

02:59:14.200 --> 02:59:16.090
Shirley said, operating on the foggy half-logic
of a dream.

02:59:16.090 --> 02:59:17.090
He didn’t respond.

02:59:17.090 --> 02:59:19.260
With growing concern, she started stepping
closer.

02:59:19.260 --> 02:59:20.260
“Mike?”

02:59:20.260 --> 02:59:23.390
That’s when she noticed something else:
Black water, pooled around Mike’s feet.

02:59:23.390 --> 02:59:28.470
The sight of it stopped her in her tracks
and froze the breath in her throat.

02:59:28.470 --> 02:59:31.010
With an almost audible creak, Mike turned
around.

02:59:31.010 --> 02:59:33.550
A grinning, white mask covered up his face.

02:59:33.550 --> 02:59:34.710
“Hello, Shirley,” it said.

02:59:34.710 --> 02:59:38.619
“So lovely to finally meet, face to face,
eh?

02:59:38.619 --> 02:59:41.739
I bet you’re so glad you ran into me.

02:59:41.739 --> 02:59:43.819
After all, sleepwalking can be dangerous...”

02:59:43.819 --> 02:59:47.649
Shirley screamed and ran in the other direction,
wasting no time at all.

02:59:47.649 --> 02:59:51.689
She heard the slow, measured footsteps of
Mike, wearing the Mask, behind her.

02:59:51.689 --> 02:59:56.130
It was in no rush; it walked like a masked
killer in a horror movie.

02:59:56.130 --> 02:59:57.130
“Why run?”

02:59:57.130 --> 03:00:01.660
the voice said, “I’ve got all day, and
I know you can’t leave while the sun’s

03:00:01.660 --> 03:00:02.660
up, darling.

03:00:02.660 --> 03:00:06.540
You and your beloved are all mine, it’s
only a matter of time...”

03:00:06.540 --> 03:00:12.069
Somehow, despite her being so far ahead, the
voice sounded like it was right next to her

03:00:12.069 --> 03:00:13.069
ear.

03:00:13.069 --> 03:00:18.680
Like it wasn’t speaking out of its mouth,
it was just talking directly into her brain.

03:00:18.680 --> 03:00:24.689
With every syllable, her mind started to slow,
like the Mask’s voice was tar seeping in

03:00:24.689 --> 03:00:25.689
between the gears.

03:00:25.689 --> 03:00:27.029
Soon enough, her legs stopped working.

03:00:27.029 --> 03:00:30.771
Shirley was frozen in place, hearing the footsteps
of the Masked Mike behind her.

03:00:30.771 --> 03:00:34.899
Her brain was flooding with dark thoughts:
Death, pain, torture, grief, the final smothering

03:00:34.899 --> 03:00:38.609
of all hope and happiness.

03:00:38.609 --> 03:00:44.700
There was really nothing like it, like every
nightmare she’d ever experienced being collected

03:00:44.700 --> 03:00:47.300
into one terrible elixir and poured onto her
mind.

03:00:47.300 --> 03:00:49.550
So this was what that horrible mask could
do...

03:00:49.550 --> 03:00:54.369
Soon enough, the Masked Mike had passed her
and turned to look directly at her face.

03:00:54.369 --> 03:00:57.739
There was an awful smell, like chemicals and
burning flesh.

03:00:57.739 --> 03:01:04.399
She turned her gaze downwards, seeing that
it looked like Mike’s body was somehow melting

03:01:04.399 --> 03:01:07.130
away from all that black water.

03:01:07.130 --> 03:01:09.060
It was like acid.

03:01:09.060 --> 03:01:17.540
“Mike was a weak host,” the Mask said,
as if it was replying to her thoughts.

03:01:17.540 --> 03:01:23.310
“He won’t last much longer, but that’s
fine, I’m looking forward to taking you

03:01:23.310 --> 03:01:24.710
for a whirl, Dear Shirley.

03:01:24.710 --> 03:01:28.420
And when your hands are mine, you can only
imagine the terrible things I’m going to

03:01:28.420 --> 03:01:30.450
do to your beloved Bea with them.”

03:01:30.450 --> 03:01:35.640
Shirley, with all her willpower, was able
to move her mouth enough to force out the

03:01:35.640 --> 03:01:37.580
words, “Don’t you dare touch her...”

03:01:37.580 --> 03:01:40.880
The monster laughed, just like it always did
in her dream.

03:01:40.880 --> 03:01:44.130
“That’s not your choice to make, anymore,”
it said.

03:01:44.130 --> 03:01:48.500
The Mask reached for her with Mike’s melting
fingers, fizzling away with all that black

03:01:48.500 --> 03:01:50.800
slime, getting so close to caressing her tender
cheek.

03:01:50.800 --> 03:01:55.880
All that awful silence was only broken by
the sound of a soft clicking behind it, then

03:01:55.880 --> 03:01:59.420
a gunshot passing through the back of its
stolen head.

03:01:59.420 --> 03:02:02.819
The Mask turned, more shocked than anything,
to see Bea standing there, wielding Mike’s

03:02:02.819 --> 03:02:05.090
old assault rifle and aiming it with deadly
accuracy.

03:02:05.090 --> 03:02:08.359
Before the Mask could breathe another poisonous
word, Bea opened fire, releasing a volley

03:02:08.359 --> 03:02:15.449
of bullets into the last of its melting body
as it staggered back into the darkened hallway.

03:02:15.449 --> 03:02:20.790
Shirley watched in astonishment as the creature
fell, Mike’s body splattering into a puddle,

03:02:20.790 --> 03:02:24.080
and sending the Mask itself skidding down
the hallway.

03:02:24.080 --> 03:02:33.710
Maybe it was just a trick of the mind, but
she could swear the Mask was frowning now,

03:02:33.710 --> 03:02:34.920
not smiling.

03:02:34.920 --> 03:02:40.790
Bea breathed a sigh of relief and said, “I’m
thinking it’s probably better if this place

03:02:40.790 --> 03:02:41.790
stays abandoned.”

03:02:41.790 --> 03:02:46.979
Shirley, who hadn’t quite regained her speech
yet, just smiled, nodded, and embraced Bea.

03:02:46.979 --> 03:02:51.880
They shared a kiss, just happy to still be
together and alive, despite it all.

03:02:51.880 --> 03:02:54.310
Come nightfall, they left again, in search
of another place.

03:02:54.310 --> 03:02:58.030
The Possessive Mask remained there, laying
on the floor of some anonymous Site-19 Hallway.

03:02:58.030 --> 03:03:03.130
Just waiting for some unlucky, weak-minded
survivor to wander in, and try it on...

03:03:03.130 --> 03:03:07.399
While some higher-level researchers, specialized
guards, and containment experts at the SCP

03:03:07.399 --> 03:03:12.770
Foundation have fixed anomalous projects tailored
to their very particular set of skills, for

03:03:12.770 --> 03:03:16.609
many lower-level operatives - including junior
researchers, guards, janitorial staff, and

03:03:16.609 --> 03:03:21.350
even the dreaded D-Classes - every day is
showing up, spinning the wheel of misfortune,

03:03:21.350 --> 03:03:23.200
and finding out how you might die today.

03:03:23.200 --> 03:03:25.510
Will you be hacked to pieces by a murder monster?

03:03:25.510 --> 03:03:26.510
Pulled into a portal?

03:03:26.510 --> 03:03:28.119
Turned into a doll?

03:03:28.119 --> 03:03:29.119
Eaten alive?

03:03:29.119 --> 03:03:32.390
Made into a living nest by bugs?

03:03:32.390 --> 03:03:34.279
Stretched out and broken?

03:03:34.279 --> 03:03:35.279
Drowned?

03:03:35.279 --> 03:03:36.279
Exsanguinated?

03:03:36.279 --> 03:03:37.279
Set on fire?

03:03:37.279 --> 03:03:42.630
Beaten to death by a volley of anomalous tomatoes
because you decided to drop a cringeworthy

03:03:42.630 --> 03:03:44.859
dad joke or a rancid pun?

03:03:44.859 --> 03:03:51.149
There are perhaps only a handful of anomalies
that not only will not harm you, but will

03:03:51.149 --> 03:03:55.220
actively enrich your life by getting to spend
time with them.

03:03:55.220 --> 03:04:01.149
And of course, chief among these is the legendary
SCP-999, also known by its adorable moniker,

03:04:01.149 --> 03:04:02.489
The Tickle Monster.

03:04:02.489 --> 03:04:09.369
We don’t need to go into too much detail
in describing this gelatinous ray of sunshine,

03:04:09.369 --> 03:04:14.620
his anomalous delightfulness has made him
somewhat of a celebrity compared to the murderer’s

03:04:14.620 --> 03:04:17.500
row of terrifying entities and monsters around
him.

03:04:17.500 --> 03:04:23.739
The researcher assigned to him today was troubled,
having seen one of his favorite coworkers

03:04:23.739 --> 03:04:27.220
devoured by SCP-682 the day before.

03:04:27.220 --> 03:04:32.810
Getting assigned to feeding and checking on
SCP-999 today was exactly what he needed.

03:04:32.810 --> 03:04:40.189
As he entered the room, several bags of M&amp;Ms
in hand, the creature cooed, perhaps sensing

03:04:40.189 --> 03:04:42.270
his tension, and approached him.

03:04:42.270 --> 03:04:47.670
Immediately, the researcher felt a wave of
calm and contentment wash over him.

03:04:47.670 --> 03:04:53.300
The incredible and rare feeling that perhaps,
everything is going to be alright for once.

03:04:53.300 --> 03:04:59.490
999 rubbed up against his leg as he poured
M&amp;Ms into its eager mouth, radiating good

03:04:59.490 --> 03:05:01.090
vibes the whole time.

03:05:01.090 --> 03:05:02.290
What an asset.

03:05:02.290 --> 03:05:03.500
What a gift.

03:05:03.500 --> 03:05:12.590
And to think, this adorable little goober
was prophesied to someday save the world.

03:05:12.590 --> 03:05:13.630
That much seemed almost funny.

03:05:13.630 --> 03:05:20.390
But he was certainly more than capable of
saving the life of someone who hadn’t felt

03:05:20.390 --> 03:05:24.409
the warmth of internal sunshine in quite some
time, and for that much, the researcher was

03:05:24.409 --> 03:05:25.409
profoundly grateful.

03:05:25.409 --> 03:05:30.680
He left 999’s chamber that day with a renewed
sense of hope for the future.

03:05:30.680 --> 03:05:33.080
That maybe, just maybe, they might be able
to pull through, to make a difference, to

03:05:33.080 --> 03:05:39.420
push this crazy ball of rock we call our home
in the right direction.

03:05:39.420 --> 03:05:44.020
Maybe someday, the sun would rise on a perfect
world.

03:05:44.020 --> 03:05:49.000
Who would have thought that a strange sunrise
could change everything?

03:05:49.000 --> 03:05:56.600
Emergency sirens went off across the globe,
but in every case, they were drowned out by

03:05:56.600 --> 03:06:00.030
a terrible and endless chorus of screams.

03:06:00.030 --> 03:06:07.380
But below all that, you’d hear another gut-wrenching
sound: A low but pervasive sizzling, like

03:06:07.380 --> 03:06:15.790
an egg on a hot pan, as billions of human
beings started to change their states of matter.

03:06:15.790 --> 03:06:17.260
The SCP Foundation had fought off and contained
so many seemingly impossible threats.

03:06:17.260 --> 03:06:20.220
From interdimensional horrors like the Hanged,
Sealed, and Scarlet Kings, to nightmarish

03:06:20.220 --> 03:06:27.410
mass killers like SCP-106, the Old Man, SCP-096,
the Shy Guy, and SCP-682, The Hard to Destroy

03:06:27.410 --> 03:06:28.410
Reptile.

03:06:28.410 --> 03:06:30.920
In their many battles against the anomalous,
they’d developed incredible methodologies

03:06:30.920 --> 03:06:31.920
and exceedingly advanced technology.

03:06:31.920 --> 03:06:36.920
But what good would any of it do when the
very center of our solar system decided in

03:06:36.920 --> 03:06:42.040
an instant that rather than being the lynchpin
of our delicate cradle of life, it would instead

03:06:42.040 --> 03:06:46.430
be the horrible instrument of all of our demises?

03:06:46.430 --> 03:06:51.390
This awful hypothetical was answered upon
the emergence of SCP-001 - A terrible day

03:06:51.390 --> 03:06:53.090
also known as When Day Breaks.

03:06:53.090 --> 03:06:58.710
In the snap of one’s fingers, half the world
was plunged into terror and death.

03:06:58.710 --> 03:07:02.050
Rays of stark, red light swept through the
streets.

03:07:02.050 --> 03:07:07.370
People lucky enough to be in the shade, or
inside buildings with a view to the outside,

03:07:07.370 --> 03:07:12.100
saw the people in the streets seize up and
begin to shriek in terrible pain.

03:07:12.100 --> 03:07:14.790
Their skin sagged and their bones liquified.

03:07:14.790 --> 03:07:19.189
Their bodies dropped down to the ground and
coalesced into gurgling, retching puddles

03:07:19.189 --> 03:07:20.540
the color of melted flesh.

03:07:20.540 --> 03:07:23.800
Those who saw this abomination happening would
never forget it.

03:07:23.800 --> 03:07:27.939
It would stay with them for the rest of their
lives.

03:07:27.939 --> 03:07:35.130
It would endure, like a stain on their retinas,
an afterimage burned into the plasma of an

03:07:35.130 --> 03:07:36.399
old TV screen.

03:07:36.399 --> 03:07:43.080
But here’s a slight consolation: For most
of the human race, “the rest of their lives”

03:07:43.080 --> 03:07:44.890
wouldn’t be that long.

03:07:44.890 --> 03:07:50.520
The sudden insanity taking over the world
caused the SCP Foundation to do something

03:07:50.520 --> 03:07:52.470
drastic: Step out of the shadows.

03:07:52.470 --> 03:07:53.470
Metaphorically speaking, of course.

03:07:53.470 --> 03:07:57.970
The legendary Foundation motto had been reversed:
They would die in the light so that humanity

03:07:57.970 --> 03:08:00.229
could live on in the dark.

03:08:00.229 --> 03:08:06.239
Thankfully, the very concept of the day gave
them one advantage: While one side of the

03:08:06.239 --> 03:08:11.410
earth was effectively doomed the second the
process began, the other side had a twelve-hour

03:08:11.410 --> 03:08:13.910
head-start before the sun turned its terrible
eye to them.

03:08:13.910 --> 03:08:21.150
Sirens went off in the middle of the night,
waking people up, groggy, rubbing the sleep

03:08:21.150 --> 03:08:22.609
from their eyes.

03:08:22.609 --> 03:08:27.199
Every television and radio and internet-enabled
device in their home was playing the same

03:08:27.199 --> 03:08:31.069
message, direct from the SCP Foundation - Which
had now effectively commandeered the entire

03:08:31.069 --> 03:08:33.850
US government, along with the rest of the
world.

03:08:33.850 --> 03:08:39.529
It gave them directions to the nearest Foundation
containment site and told them that if they

03:08:39.529 --> 03:08:43.630
didn’t immediately comply and find their
way to safety, they would experience a terrible

03:08:43.630 --> 03:08:44.630
death by sunrise.

03:08:44.630 --> 03:08:48.500
And if you’re at all familiar with human
beings, you’ve probably already predicted

03:08:48.500 --> 03:08:53.720
that they didn’t just calmly wrap themselves
up, make their way to their cars, and form

03:08:53.720 --> 03:08:57.569
an orderly line to the various Foundation
containment sites located around them.

03:08:57.569 --> 03:08:59.679
It was, in fact, total pandemonium.

03:08:59.679 --> 03:09:05.500
As the solar clock ticked down, slowly marking
off the seconds that anyone outside had left

03:09:05.500 --> 03:09:08.570
to live, the only human beings with a meaningful
head start began to go insane in a number

03:09:08.570 --> 03:09:10.130
of varied and interesting ways.

03:09:10.130 --> 03:09:14.619
Some who took the situation seriously, and
acknowledged that the sun was indeed going

03:09:14.619 --> 03:09:18.029
to wipe out most of humanity, simply cracked
under the pressure.

03:09:18.029 --> 03:09:26.090
There were those who went into totally catatonic
states, rocking back and forth in the corner

03:09:26.090 --> 03:09:28.350
and refusing to respond to any stimuli.

03:09:28.350 --> 03:09:32.579
Some became erratic and violent, with unpredictable
behavior that harmed themselves and others.

03:09:32.579 --> 03:09:38.489
After all, what was the point in acting normally
anymore when the complete crumbling of human

03:09:38.489 --> 03:09:44.850
civilization was imminent, and their only
hope was some shadowy organization they’d

03:09:44.850 --> 03:09:46.460
never heard of?

03:09:46.460 --> 03:09:51.930
Some, not heeding the warnings seriously enough,
and prompted by greed and opportunism, tried

03:09:51.930 --> 03:09:54.410
to take advantage of the situation financially.

03:09:54.410 --> 03:09:59.399
Some smashed windows of local stores and looted,
or broke into the homes of neighbors who had

03:09:59.399 --> 03:10:02.220
already fled in hopes of purloining their
property.

03:10:02.220 --> 03:10:07.570
Others were a little more creative: Setting
up short-term, everything must go, nighttime

03:10:07.570 --> 03:10:13.770
fire sales for their brands of essential oils,
nootropics, and nutriceuticals, claiming all

03:10:13.770 --> 03:10:20.750
of them have the power to ward off or cure
the new effects of the sun.

03:10:20.750 --> 03:10:27.979
Others completely denied the possibility that
any of this was real, and claimed that the

03:10:27.979 --> 03:10:33.310
messages from this so-called “SCP Foundation”
were actually just a front to take away their

03:10:33.310 --> 03:10:36.149
freedoms and trap them in underground government
camps.

03:10:36.149 --> 03:10:40.790
They staunchly refused to follow any of the
safety guidelines that the SCP Foundation

03:10:40.790 --> 03:10:45.739
put out, claiming, “If you try to take away
my first amendment rights, you’re gonna

03:10:45.739 --> 03:10:47.390
taste my second amendment rights!”

03:10:47.390 --> 03:10:51.070
While riding around in their pick-up trucks
and SUVs, blasting Kid Rock’s “Don’t

03:10:51.070 --> 03:10:53.560
Tell Me How To Live” at earsplitting volumes.

03:10:53.560 --> 03:10:56.979
Naturally, they had all melted into screeching
puddles of liquid flesh by sunrise.

03:10:56.979 --> 03:11:02.479
Some were not victims of their own bizarre
choices but were doomed by the sudden terrible

03:11:02.479 --> 03:11:05.320
fear and panic of the circumstances themselves.

03:11:05.320 --> 03:11:08.810
The highways were gridlocked, cars stuck as
far as the eye could see.

03:11:08.810 --> 03:11:13.029
So many had rushed to escape during the initial
wave that means of transport soon became choke

03:11:13.029 --> 03:11:15.149
points, like clogged arteries in a dying man.

03:11:15.149 --> 03:11:17.899
People do irrational things when they’re
scared: Riots broke out on the streets.

03:11:17.899 --> 03:11:18.899
Fighting, killing, burning.

03:11:18.899 --> 03:11:22.540
Everyone hoping for some means of meager control
over a situation that had long been out of

03:11:22.540 --> 03:11:24.250
any of their hands.

03:11:24.250 --> 03:11:27.699
Some were too far away with not enough time
to close the distance.

03:11:27.699 --> 03:11:30.060
What could they really do but just sit around
and wait to die?

03:11:30.060 --> 03:11:33.720
The hours marched on as the earth made its
slow turn towards the sun, leaving one decimated

03:11:33.720 --> 03:11:37.449
half in darkness and the other a sitting duck
for its terrible effects.

03:11:37.449 --> 03:11:42.159
Hundreds of thousands had made their way to
the containment sites and safely gotten inside,

03:11:42.159 --> 03:11:45.170
but still, so many millions were left outside.

03:11:45.170 --> 03:11:46.670
It was a slaughterhouse.

03:11:46.670 --> 03:11:51.779
One great, big global meat grinder, and every
moment that passed, the handle turned, and

03:11:51.779 --> 03:11:56.520
any humans left outside got just that little
bit closer to the grinding, gnashing gears

03:11:56.520 --> 03:11:57.520
below.

03:11:57.520 --> 03:12:01.380
Site-19, being the largest containment site
on the Foundation’s books, also became humanity’s

03:12:01.380 --> 03:12:02.380
only bastion.

03:12:02.380 --> 03:12:04.450
It was the greatest hope of escape from the
horrors going on outside.

03:12:04.450 --> 03:12:06.410
The Foundation had figured out so many ways
of counteracting deadly anomalous forces before,

03:12:06.410 --> 03:12:11.880
given enough time and enough personnel, surely
they could figure out a solution to even this?

03:12:11.880 --> 03:12:17.360
This was, however, when the most startling
realization yet swept over all the survivors:

03:12:17.360 --> 03:12:19.880
Those who were melted by the sudden hostile
sun weren’t dead.

03:12:19.880 --> 03:12:23.470
They were very much alive, in fact, but they’d
been changed - In both body and mind.

03:12:23.470 --> 03:12:26.069
What had once been humans now became terrible
beasts.

03:12:26.069 --> 03:12:28.790
Half-melted gelatinous nightmares that coagulated
into even bigger beasts.

03:12:28.790 --> 03:12:32.920
They got it into their head that they were
grateful for the transformation.

03:12:32.920 --> 03:12:40.229
That they had been liberated from their old
forms in the old world.

03:12:40.229 --> 03:12:43.649
They were something so much more now...

03:12:43.649 --> 03:12:47.680
And they wanted everyone else to join them
in their liberation.

03:12:47.680 --> 03:12:50.950
Survivors outside the Foundation containment
sites were systematically hunted by these

03:12:50.950 --> 03:12:52.560
great masses of altered flesh.

03:12:52.560 --> 03:12:56.729
Even those smart enough to cover every inch
of their bodies with clothes to protect from

03:12:56.729 --> 03:13:02.460
the sun, to only move at night, to carry weapons,
were dragged out by meaty tendrils from their

03:13:02.460 --> 03:13:05.279
refuges in basements and the lightless hearts
of buildings.

03:13:05.279 --> 03:13:07.710
Dragged into the searing gaze of the sun.

03:13:07.710 --> 03:13:10.449
To melt, to change, to coalesce into something
greater.

03:13:10.449 --> 03:13:13.180
It was the inevitable fate of all of humanity.

03:13:13.180 --> 03:13:16.600
When most of the stragglers were dragged out
and changed, the flesh masses started turning

03:13:16.600 --> 03:13:21.790
their attention to the Foundation containment
sites that were keeping all these poor, deluded

03:13:21.790 --> 03:13:23.100
people from salvation.

03:13:23.100 --> 03:13:28.529
They mounted offensives against the bases,
which the Foundation, with what remained of

03:13:28.529 --> 03:13:33.910
their manpower and advanced weaponry, did
what they could to repel the attacks.

03:13:33.910 --> 03:13:36.810
But every single day, it got harder.

03:13:36.810 --> 03:13:41.630
Thankfully, for the people holed up at Site-19,
there was one consolation out there to help:

03:13:41.630 --> 03:13:42.630
SCP-999.

03:13:42.630 --> 03:13:47.280
The Foundation was at war with the sun and
its terrible disciples, and contrary to many

03:13:47.280 --> 03:13:51.620
people’s beliefs, it takes more than men,
equipment, and bullets to win a war.

03:13:51.620 --> 03:13:56.329
999 provided the essential element that brought
it all together: Morale, hope, the will to

03:13:56.329 --> 03:13:59.540
go on, even when it seemed like all could
be lost.

03:13:59.540 --> 03:14:04.460
After a long day of battling the fleshy abominations
at the gates, Foundation guards, Mobile Task

03:14:04.460 --> 03:14:08.619
Force members, and even civilian volunteers
were drained and traumatized by the horrors

03:14:08.619 --> 03:14:09.619
that they’d seen.

03:14:09.619 --> 03:14:14.770
Once a day was over, 999 would move among
their ranks, cuddling up to them, warding

03:14:14.770 --> 03:14:20.030
off the despair that was easy to set in during
the downswing of a terrible apocalypse like

03:14:20.030 --> 03:14:21.030
this.

03:14:21.030 --> 03:14:24.350
Without his presence, there would be no hope
of fighting that good fight.

03:14:24.350 --> 03:14:30.189
So many of them would have given up, walked
into the sun, and joined the monstrous force

03:14:30.189 --> 03:14:31.220
they were fighting.

03:14:31.220 --> 03:14:34.420
After all, they seemed happy enough, and it
would certainly be easier.

03:14:34.420 --> 03:14:38.579
999 had become, once again, an indispensable
asset to the SCP Foundation.

03:14:38.579 --> 03:14:43.680
The solar betrayal may not have been the Scarlet
King’s doing - Well, as far as our current

03:14:43.680 --> 03:14:48.570
intel suggests, anyway - but he was playing
a crucial part in saving the world, exactly

03:14:48.570 --> 03:14:49.570
as predicted.

03:14:49.570 --> 03:14:53.530
999 didn’t fully understand what was going
on outside sometimes, why there seemed to

03:14:53.530 --> 03:14:57.859
be fewer people as the weeks went on, and
why the people there seemed so sad all the

03:14:57.859 --> 03:15:03.210
time whenever he wasn’t helping them, but
he was more than happy to help, whatever the

03:15:03.210 --> 03:15:04.210
case.

03:15:04.210 --> 03:15:08.399
Many of the humanoid and some even non-humanoid
anomalies which realistically posed no harm

03:15:08.399 --> 03:15:12.380
to people inside the site were released from
their containment chambers.

03:15:12.380 --> 03:15:18.069
They needed everyone and everything they could
get in what seemed like a hopelessly one-sided

03:15:18.069 --> 03:15:20.409
fight against the very concept of being outside.

03:15:20.409 --> 03:15:27.500
Many of the larger, vacated chambers were
now filled with refugees from the outside,

03:15:27.500 --> 03:15:32.640
many of whom had lost everything and everyone
they’d ever known to the horrors out there.

03:15:32.640 --> 03:15:40.659
999 made the rounds in these areas regularly,
and through the D-Class cots, which had been

03:15:40.659 --> 03:15:45.619
repurposed into more sleeping areas for the
thousands of desperate and terrified refugees.

03:15:45.619 --> 03:15:50.430
The adjustment to this new life - and to the
knowledge of all the secrets that had been

03:15:50.430 --> 03:15:54.739
kept from them for all these years - wasn’t
exactly easy on their psyches, but spending

03:15:54.739 --> 03:15:57.859
time with that soft, yellow blob that seemed
to smell exactly like their favorite scent

03:15:57.859 --> 03:15:59.560
from the old world made everything better.

03:15:59.560 --> 03:16:05.370
He was a savior in dark times, slithering
from person to person, giving hope where there

03:16:05.370 --> 03:16:06.370
was none.

03:16:06.370 --> 03:16:10.109
People opened up about their problems and
their fears - which, these days, were remarkably

03:16:10.109 --> 03:16:18.380
similar - and though 999 couldn’t reply,
for many it was enough just to feel like they

03:16:18.380 --> 03:16:19.990
were being listened to.

03:16:19.990 --> 03:16:26.660
He was a soft, blobby shoulder to cry on,
and after day broke, everyone needed a good,

03:16:26.660 --> 03:16:27.660
old-fashioned cry.

03:16:27.660 --> 03:16:35.199
However, one day, while wandering the long,
dark corridors of Site-19, he saw a different

03:16:35.199 --> 03:16:36.500
kind of crying.

03:16:36.500 --> 03:16:41.000
It was a woman with an unfamiliar face - probably
one of the rare new refugees - bawling her

03:16:41.000 --> 03:16:45.420
eyes out to a Foundation Senior Researcher
and an accompanying guard with an assault

03:16:45.420 --> 03:16:46.420
rifle.

03:16:46.420 --> 03:16:50.430
Hot, fat tears were rolling down her dirty
cheeks, her shaking hands clasped in prayer.

03:16:50.430 --> 03:16:53.479
She was begging the researcher and the guard
to let her go outside.

03:16:53.479 --> 03:16:59.989
She said that her son was still out there,
hiding away in the back room of a bank where

03:16:59.989 --> 03:17:01.979
she used to work.

03:17:01.979 --> 03:17:02.979
They got separated.

03:17:02.979 --> 03:17:06.159
She needed to go back and find him.

03:17:06.159 --> 03:17:13.880
Sadly, the researcher and the guard told her
that this was out of the question.

03:17:13.880 --> 03:17:20.200
Official orders stated that anyone outside
the base at this time was to be considered

03:17:20.200 --> 03:17:21.200
lost.

03:17:21.200 --> 03:17:26.350
Letting her out there to find her son would
essentially be condemning her to death.

03:17:26.350 --> 03:17:29.550
No human could go out there safely.

03:17:29.550 --> 03:17:33.260
But of course, SCP-999 isn’t, by any definition,
a human.

03:17:33.260 --> 03:17:38.840
When night fell, and everyone else was hunkered
down inside, 999 found a small crack in the

03:17:38.840 --> 03:17:40.430
wall and slithered out of it.

03:17:40.430 --> 03:17:44.909
It may not have been communicative in the
way most humans were, but 999 was indeed an

03:17:44.909 --> 03:17:48.470
intelligent being, and knew intuitively that
if it left through a more obvious route, its

03:17:48.470 --> 03:17:54.090
human caretakers here at the SCP Foundation
might try to stop it.

03:17:54.090 --> 03:17:59.640
And when it came to saving this little boy,
999 refused to let anyone stop it.

03:17:59.640 --> 03:18:02.199
999 slithered out and through the broken streets.

03:18:02.199 --> 03:18:03.270
There were no bodies, of course.

03:18:03.270 --> 03:18:08.590
It seemed even the dead could be revived and
assimilated through the power of the sun.

03:18:08.590 --> 03:18:10.550
Talk about a mixed miracle.

03:18:10.550 --> 03:18:14.030
But the broken-down world outside Site-19
undeniably reflected the pandemonium that

03:18:14.030 --> 03:18:15.030
took place here.

03:18:15.030 --> 03:18:20.800
999 would need to do his best to find the
little boy trapped out here before the sun

03:18:20.800 --> 03:18:21.800
rose.

03:18:21.800 --> 03:18:26.050
He lost a few hours even finding his way to
the nearest town, where it was safe to assume

03:18:26.050 --> 03:18:28.279
that the little boy was trapped.

03:18:28.279 --> 03:18:29.279
He saw some of those...

03:18:29.279 --> 03:18:30.899
Things... on the way there.

03:18:30.899 --> 03:18:34.960
Those moving, wailing mountains of melted
human flesh, each talking and chattering to

03:18:34.960 --> 03:18:36.859
themselves in a hundred different dead voices.

03:18:36.859 --> 03:18:38.970
999 had been cross-tested with SCP-682, and
still, those monsters frightened him.

03:18:38.970 --> 03:18:44.399
He decided it would be best to stay away from
them and make sure that they never saw him

03:18:44.399 --> 03:18:45.399
out here.

03:18:45.399 --> 03:18:47.710
Eventually, 999 reached the town: Similarly
dilapidated and broken down in the months

03:18:47.710 --> 03:18:51.720
since the world as we had all known it disappeared
in a ray of terrible sunshine.

03:18:51.720 --> 03:18:55.380
More great, gibbering blobs of flesh patrolled
the streets, looking for converts to integrate

03:18:55.380 --> 03:19:00.600
into their biomass. 999 could only hope it
wasn’t already too late for the little boy.

03:19:00.600 --> 03:19:03.899
999 discretely slithered from building to
building, until it could identify one as this

03:19:03.899 --> 03:19:06.050
“bank” that the boy’s distraught mother
had been mentioning.

03:19:06.050 --> 03:19:10.390
It thankfully had some awareness of what a
bank actually was, from the years of stressed

03:19:10.390 --> 03:19:14.489
Foundation employees telling it about the
money troubles they were suffering outside

03:19:14.489 --> 03:19:15.489
of work.

03:19:15.489 --> 03:19:17.550
It was another great example of it paying
to listen.

03:19:17.550 --> 03:19:20.100
Eventually, it did slither into the correct
building, and it heard the extremely quiet

03:19:20.100 --> 03:19:22.270
whimpering of the little boy inside.

03:19:22.270 --> 03:19:25.310
It could feel the sadness and the fear radiating
off of him, as it was the creature’s natural

03:19:25.310 --> 03:19:32.199
instinct to help the needy, and it used those
signals like homing beams to find the scared

03:19:32.199 --> 03:19:33.199
little boy.

03:19:33.199 --> 03:19:36.310
He’d hidden inside a broom closet and was
quietly weeping into his hands.

03:19:36.310 --> 03:19:42.180
He hadn’t eaten in days, and was only surviving
by drinking the filthy water from the mop

03:19:42.180 --> 03:19:44.039
bucket sitting next to him.

03:19:44.039 --> 03:19:49.100
999 immediately embraced the boy, covering
him in its healing energy until the tears

03:19:49.100 --> 03:19:51.040
on the boy’s face eventually dried.

03:19:51.040 --> 03:19:54.819
999 cooed and chirped pleasantly until the
little boy was laughing again.

03:19:54.819 --> 03:19:56.189
But this momentary joy was soon interrupted.

03:19:56.189 --> 03:20:01.170
A great, heaving weight dragged itself down
the hall outside.

03:20:01.170 --> 03:20:06.910
Both 999 and the boy could sense its monstrous
presence.

03:20:06.910 --> 03:20:12.109
As it got closer, they could hear all of those
chattering voices, those poisonous whispers.

03:20:12.109 --> 03:20:20.199
When it passed the door, they heard it speaking,
its voice practically vibrating with the hum

03:20:20.199 --> 03:20:21.199
of malicious lunacy.

03:20:21.199 --> 03:20:24.159
“Turn, pretty flowers, turn towards the
sun.

03:20:24.159 --> 03:20:26.010
Feel it on your face.

03:20:26.010 --> 03:20:29.199
Feel yourself change and sluice and mix into
us.

03:20:29.199 --> 03:20:31.500
Become one with our army of one.

03:20:31.500 --> 03:20:34.800
It must be so lonely to be you, little flower.

03:20:34.800 --> 03:20:36.789
Walk into the sun and be us...”

03:20:36.789 --> 03:20:42.540
999 and the boy remained silent in the broom
closet for hours as the great shape patrolled

03:20:42.540 --> 03:20:45.210
the bank outside, searching for converts,
for victims.

03:20:45.210 --> 03:20:48.930
At times, it seemed too frightening to even
breathe, fearing that would be enough to make

03:20:48.930 --> 03:20:51.130
the monster detect them.

03:20:51.130 --> 03:21:01.659
It felt like an eternity until the monster
eventually did slope off, and leave them in

03:21:01.659 --> 03:21:06.680
the comforting quiet and darkness of the closet.

03:21:06.680 --> 03:21:08.430
Now, they might be free.

03:21:08.430 --> 03:21:14.300
999 could escort the boy safely back to the
containment site and into the arms of his

03:21:14.300 --> 03:21:15.300
terrified mother.

03:21:15.300 --> 03:21:23.850
But when they opened the door, they saw a
terrible sight: Light, in the distance, pouring

03:21:23.850 --> 03:21:26.699
through the windows and the glass double doors.

03:21:26.699 --> 03:21:31.640
It wouldn’t be safe to go out that way.

03:21:31.640 --> 03:21:41.399
Upon seeing this, and putting together what
it meant, the boy began to cry.

03:21:41.399 --> 03:21:45.600
He couldn’t take another night in the closet.

03:21:45.600 --> 03:21:48.229
It was all going to whither from here...

03:21:48.229 --> 03:21:50.560
Until 999 had a wonderful idea.

03:21:50.560 --> 03:21:55.520
Hours later, when the Foundation’s guards
manned the turrets at the entrance of Site-19,

03:21:55.520 --> 03:22:15.279
waiting for the inevitable onslaught of the
melted flesh creatures, they tensed up, seeing

03:22:15.279 --> 03:22:18.590
a bobbing gelatinous form slithering towards
them in the distance.

03:22:18.590 --> 03:22:34.090
The guards, who’d learned the hard way from
too many lost men that 

03:22:34.090 --> 03:23:01.480
it was better to 
be safe than sorry, drew a bead on the distant

03:23:01.480 --> 03:23:22.930
shape and prepared to fire - When suddenly,
their superior raised a hand and said:

03:23:22.930 --> 03:23:24.530
“Wait! Hold fire!

03:23:24.530 --> 03:23:25.590
Is that...999?”

03:23:25.590 --> 03:23:31.120
And it was.

03:23:31.120 --> 03:23:51.850
They all stared in astonishment as 999, chipper
as ever, came towards them through the sun.

03:23:51.850 --> 03:24:06.200
It looked as though the opacity of its yellow
cytoplasm had increased, but other than that,

03:24:06.200 --> 03:24:07.979
it was unaffected.

03:24:07.979 --> 03:24:14.010
Turns out, the sun couldn’t melt what was
already melted.

03:24:14.010 --> 03:24:24.100
The guards parted to allow 999 safe passage
into the facility, watching in amazement,

03:24:24.100 --> 03:24:32.930
and once it was inside, 999’s slime parted,
releasing its contents: One very relieved

03:24:32.930 --> 03:24:33.930
little boy.

03:24:33.930 --> 03:24:49.189
It seemed through turning up its own opacity,
999 had given the boy safe passage through

03:24:49.189 --> 03:24:54.590
the sun and back into the facility.

03:24:54.590 --> 03:24:55.920
The boy was saved.

03:24:55.920 --> 03:24:57.790
It had won.

03:24:57.790 --> 03:25:07.819
Not long after that, there was a tearful reunion
between mother and child, and a brief flash

03:25:07.819 --> 03:25:11.500
of hope in this dark and terrible time.

03:25:11.500 --> 03:25:29.409
999 didn’t stop to bask, of course - It
returned to its duties, keeping up staff morale

03:25:29.409 --> 03:28:04.090
and helping the refugees heal from 

03:28:04.090 --> 03:28:05.770
the horrors they’d seen.

03:28:05.770 --> 03:28:12.000
In its own little way, and for a lot of people,
SCP-999 really was saving the world.

03:28:12.000 --> 03:28:13.630
Or what was left of it, anyway.

03:28:13.630 --> 03:28:18.850
Oh, and of course, if SCP-999 has won your
hearts just like it’s won ours, you’ll

03:28:18.850 --> 03:28:24.550
be pleased to know that you can purchase your
own adorable, high-quality SCP-999 plushie

03:28:24.550 --> 03:28:25.850
at scpswag.com.

03:28:25.850 --> 03:28:29.260
Check the link in the description to get your
own.

03:28:29.260 --> 03:28:47.180
Trust us, having a 999 to cuddle really does
make any apocalypse a whole lot easier.

